        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, The sad death of her Majesty will have an enormous effect upon fashion I am thinking, and everything will wear an aspect of mourning. Black is conspicuously fashionable. Black skirts are mostly of fine cloth, much tucked, or of silk and wool cashmeres and vigognes, and these, lined with silk, and with glace or satin trappings of somewhat fanciful design, make the very smartest of garments. The fur toques are very becoming, espec.ia4y when the hair is in bright tones of brown or chestnut. Black is seen on everything now. Eat crowns in silk or velvet are embroidered in black stitchery. It is said that the shepherdess shape is coming in in the spring. It is a low crown and a round brim which dips down in front. But the number of shapes is</p>
        <p>The new method of dressing the hair low in the nape of the neck is only a resuscitation. Women whose faces have lost the fresh bloom of earliest youth are ill-advised to adopt it without a very earnest consideration of their profile in the looking. glass, when, should they be critical, they will probably decide that the chignon is trying and not nearly so " smart " as the coiffure built high. There is a droop about the effect that is quite dowdy unless a special concentration is made upon the tresses on the brow and the crown of the head, tending to raise them, and provide the height and dignity that most countenances require. But undoubtedly the fresh of face and soft and round of countenance need not hesitate, and should their hair be short, what prettier newer method is there of dressing it than en queue' For the long-haired there are coils; for the short all that is needful is that the hair be turned under and secured either with a ribbon band, or with a jewelled comb.</p>
        <p>Satin-cloth is scarcely a material which can be considered suitable for dressy afternoon blouses, but it is warm, lasts well, and is quite appropriate for ordinary every-day wear. It is beet made up rather plainly. The fronts, for instance, may be slightly full with a central box pleat, and the top turned back with a well-cut sailor-collar to show a small vest of tucked or corded glace. The sailor. collar may be of the satin-cloth, stitched or plain, or of velvet, or, if the blouse is of the prediatinat- ing colour, a white cloth collar edged with a strapping of the darker cloth looks nice. The sleeves of these cloth blouses are usually of the ordinary shirt shape, with straight, stitched wrist- bands, while the belts are of either stitched cloth or leather. There is nothing very novel in the way of silk shirts to chronicle. Just at present they are chiefly useful to wear under thick coats, and so are rarely elaborate in style. Glace silk continues fashionable, and many blouses of it have fronts with a single broad box-pleat on either side of a vest of chiffon, mousseline, or crêpe, strapped across with little buttoned tabs of the checked silk.</p>
        <p>A costume of rough-surfaced cloth has no fur on the outside, but the fold of the skirt, which opens from waist to foot at the left side, is lined with sealskin—as are the fronts of the short coat and the half-high collar. The under sleeves are seal- skin, and the cloth over them is sewn down in pleats, the fulness forming a deep pagoda shape halfway between elbow and wrist. It is probable that the fur is not carried up to the top of the sleeves but is applied only to the lower portion. Another walking costume in cloth has an ermine vest, and the fronts are lined with ermine. The skirt opens up the front over a curious fancy mate- rial. The sides of the cloth fronts are faced with ermine. The idea is to represent an entire under- dress of fur, with the opening over it. In the same way a coat, matching the skirt, opens upwards from the waist to slicw a rich lining of sable. This is a very becoming form to a slight figure. Round the neck of the wearer was a voluminous tie of gauze, bordered with narrow lines of the fur. Despite all predictions to the contrary, rough friezes are still much worn, and many delicate costumes are to be seen in fashionable quarters even on the most wintry days. A fashionable type of walking gown is of frieze ,• the skirt is cut with the still popular deep-shaped flounce, which is trimmed with seven narrow stitched bands of glace, the back of the skirt being put into the band in two box-pleats. The bole° is of the Russian blouse type, with a belt of folded glacd and wide revers trimmed with strappings and a I narrow outline of sable. This coat or bodice—it ' is really something midway between the two—can ' be worn quite closed, or open to show the vest of lace. The hat to go with this dress is a toque stitched velvet, trimmed with choux of glac6, sable tails and a cluster of flowers. Frieze makes up very smartly in a similar style to that j ustdeseribed, but with trimmings of black glace and caracol, the vest in this case being of lace and black glace ribbon, and the hat of black felt with black ostrich feathers and pink roses. Your loving JENNIE.</p>
