        <p>, nature, which have been,madeln reeent-,years Majesty's resources. There ore also various ai windfalls left to the Queen by private persons, matter of fact, there are only two of whitili tl: record. One was the half.a million left to Fi* by the eccentric John Camden Neild. in 1852, lother was £lO,OOO which fell into the Queen', nurse, through the death of someone in their,'" Lancaster, without heirs. Neild, who develm, cllarracteristics of the miser, left a few sraptlj...— da the Queen afterwards increasiAridd l'Her Walt, :v1 .. rebuilt the chancel of North Marston ChUrch, oil which Neild was the patron. It was only after finding' at the dead man had left no relations, that the Queenl accepted the bequest, which 'mounted to nearly, £500.000, and was left to her for her sole use and benefit! 'Me death of our Queen has not been allowed to inter- I ere with the date fixed for the marriage of Queenl Walhelmina, but it has, of course,altered the programme I arrangements and festivities considerably. With, our, own great loss fresh in our minds, it is not easy to bring our thoughts to bear on festivities of any kind, but we ran hope, and this we do most truly..that the putch may be as blessed in their girl Queen, as we babe 'been in her, whom to-day the whole Empire tdourns. I In the first number of the "Lady's Magazine," there, lis a very interesting account of Queen Wilheimiaa, Ons Wilhelmintje "as the Dutch people eall_her. Her Majesty it seems, is a very fluent writer and speaker of English, French, and Germ.tn, and she is also familiar with Malay, the language spoken by millions of her subjects in the Dutch East Indies,, the young -Queen is intensely patriotic, and proud of her country, and the Dutch language is spoken Bt Cohrt,rineteid a Ifrenati"--1 the chosen tongue of courtiers and diplomatists. The 'Queen also plays and sings. but she has no ear for music iand dces not care for it. In this she differs greatly feorn her future husband, who is passionately food eif muJic. While the Grand Duke is also an ardent sportsman, the liven is against all killing of birds ay animalsr rattle referring whenever possible to make veta of them. ' QUOSO Wilhelmina is having an eignisire service of china made in this country, for which Sir Alma Tedium. who boasts of his Dutch descent, •hag undertaken thej designing. It is a full dinner service, and is being made at the famous Royal Worcester works. The pattern is to be old English on white china bordered with an orange coloured band. In the centre of the plates will he the Royal arms, and in panels round the edges!, ithel arms of the eleven States of the Netherlande. will ' he' painted. All the colouring and figuring is to be done by hand, and the cost of the service will be over four, hundred pounds. .</p>
