        <p>The universal Mourning will mean a serious loss tol trade, with the exception of that in black g0e,d0.,, Trani general mourning is to last until April 17th while' Society folk will be in the deepest blaek for six months, and in half-mourning until January 1902. Many, of the more wealthy class, will spend the greater part of the year abroad, and all the fashionable weddings, which were going to make such a ptir this season, will now be conducted in the quietest manner pos,ible. This toget- her with the shutting down Of all entertainments miens a great loss to the better class of tradespeople ; whilt among the smaller ones, hootmakers and umbrella, makers are already complaining, that people have spent all their money on dresses, coats, and hats. making old ,bo..ts and umbrellas do in the place of new ones, which In the ordinary course they would have bought. Like death itself, thelaabit ,of mourning for the deed is universal. It springs from an instructive feeling of our common nature, and has found its expression in all ages and among all peoples. Mournine was expres.ed by the Ancients, chiefly by personal inflictions, fasting., and verions discomforts. The command given to the Israelites, "Ye shall uct cut yourselves, nor make any baldness between your eyes, fee the dead," shows that maceration of the body as a sign of grief prevailed at al very early period. The practice of mourning is Dow ex- pressed solely by the dress worn, and in ?wore black is the colour which has been generally chosen, for both public and private mourning. ',llllCoui</p>
