        <p>This was not always the case, RA in former times in this country,, there. were many changes in therlenlo 'Court mourning. Yellow sa'in was the rnnurni _ .the Court in Henry VIII' time, mod scarlet remained for ng afterwards, while the Kings of France wed to uirn in violet. Even now, black is by no mean.- anlversat colour. In China, white is the rule, sup, to denote purity. In Turkey it is blue or violet, ex- pressing the happiness which it is hnprei the deceased enjoys. In Egypt it Is yellow, the colour of the tea yes when they fall, and signifying the end of life. In Some other countries it is brown, the colour of the earth, to which the dead return. In Europe, the choice of solemn black for mourning seems to have had its origin in its in- dication of the darkness of the tomb. l • 1 For the next few weeks, Mark. unrelieved, should be worn. Many will complain that they find the hard blac' line round the neck unbecoming, hut let them try th effect of a dainty chiffon or live collar.lightiv swathed, and finishing in two ends, tagged with jet,falling almost to the waist. They will thee probsh•-• chance theii mMds about the " hard black line•" Erni a for outdo wear, are being built mostly f black silk, edged with chiffon, or ribbon rurhe, or chenille. So soon as tl- wea.ther permit. chiffon and net will take the place the silk. tile. ellfect being much more becoming a' desirable, from every point of view. It is curious how people's opinions on the ," mourning." differ. Some appear to think that nothit less than crape trill do, while others deem velvet quit.. Jeep mourni— thel is 1. ind. iPrid L</p>
