      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRll),g; Y, FEBRUARY 1 1901</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>l t-vPStrtUnt• )</p>
        <p>RUBY LOVEDAY. BY SILAS K. HOCKING, Author of "The Strange Adventures of Israel Pendray," "The Day of Recompense," "God's Outcast," "In Spite of Fate," "Her Benny," /co.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: The Lovedays, ric 'k, stran.ers from London, take furnished apartments atEndilloe Farm, tenanted by the Pentyres, a half-ruined family. Young 'Justin Pentyre is discontented at the prospect of their poverty being observed, and endearours unsuccess- fully to persuade his people to refu.so the offer. The Lovedays prove to be very agreeable persons, but Justin failu for several days to catch sight of their beautiful- daughter Ruby. He returns. one night, however, to And that she is missing irony the farm. They fear she has fallen down a disused mine, and Justin volunteers to go down. Meanwhihs, Ruby is lying at the bottom of the cliffs on the seashore, unconscious, and with a sprained snide,- which caused her to fall over the side. Not finding her in the mine, Justin searches the beach, and ditoovers her just as the tide is enveloping her. Mr carries her home. She rapidly falls ill, and the doctors give up all hope, as oth,or injuries have madeethete- selves visible.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IX. Anti TO PAIL "Pleasures are like poppies :Tread. You seize the flower, its broom is shed." A month later Jastin had entered into a inor experience, and an experience that in all his dream&amp; and imaginings he had never contemplated or deemed possible. It came to him as a painful. shock which, for awhile; seemed to shift the very' centre of gravity and made him uncertain of him- self and everything (Aire,. That Ruby was unwittingly the cause of this may be readily guessed. Ruby persisted in living in spite or the fact that'. Dr: Morrison gave up her case as hopeless. Vigilit the tide of 4;ier life had ebbed out to its fUrthdet limit, and Dorothy rushed off' into the fields, blind and choking with grief, to tell Justin that the viry worst had come, Ruby looked up and smiled; then fell into a sweet rehashing sleep from which she awoke several hours later and declared to her mother that- she felt ever so' much better; and that she thought she would be' Able to get up on the following day. She did} not, however, get up the next day or the nett week, but within' a' month she was well enough. to be carried downstairs, and to receive visitors:. Justin was the first of these to be admitted into her preapnce—and he' was not a little elated at thelavour9shewn to him.. "</p>
        <p>Dorothy met him at the end of the lane one Thursday afternoon on his return from Trelford. "Go and make yourself presentable, Justin," she said, "for Ruby wants to see you." "Wants. to see me ? " he questioned, for Dorothy's annponcement had so taken him by,surprise that he had'nothing• better to say at the moment. .haa spoken about you. iota of tithes lately, and said how thankful she felt,' Dorothy went on, "but I fancy:sheswints just to tell you herself " "I hope she does not want to do anything of the kind;" heinterrUpted. "I shall* j-ast make an ass of myself if shiebegins to thank •me." "Not since you know beforehand," Dorothy said with a smile. "And after all it is the most natural thing-in the world that she should want to see you." "Yes, I' suppose so," he said, absently, for he remembered troiv foriieeks he had been literally pining for-just a glimpse Pt her face, and how he had lietened* constanfiy in the hall, hoping that some tonieflier voice would float down the stairs to him. "You had' better put on your coat of mail," Derothy said; banteringly, "for I really think she looks prettier than per." "Inched! I thought you told' nte she had wasted to a shadow."' "Oh, that was'weeks 'ago. She looks pale still, of course, but that is scarcely any drawback." Justin walked slowly into the house, and retired at 'once to his'own room. He .had played his part before Dorothy remarkably well, and she had no idea how intensely excited ho was. • : At list he was disposed to dress himself in his Sunday best, but thought better of it. "I'll go just as I am," he said to himself. "What can it matter? She knows Igo to Trelford every day. Besides----" but he did not finish the sentence. His. heart was in a tumult, and he was half angrywith himself that it should be so. • ..Dorothy was waiting fcir him at the foot of the stairs, to condect him to the Loveday apartments. "Why,. you have not donned your best after all," she said; inquiringly. "I thought you were, making a most elaborate toilet,. by the•tiine you've been." "Then you' are mistaken for once." "Don't yoolvisli to see her ? - "I don't Want to be thanked at any rate." "Oh, ittliat'lt. all, you'll soon get over it." "I hope so."' She darted an inquiring,glanee at' him, then led the way out of the room. Whether Justin was absolutelyindiffiarent, or whether it was all put on, was a point she was not quite able to decide. Ruby.wao waiting for her visitor without trepida- tion and without even curiosity. She felt no interest in Justin except in so far as he had rescued her from an. awkward and dangerous situation. For this, of' course, she was grateful—exceedingly grateful. She had nearly forgotten what he was like. She. had a vague recollection that he was tall, muscular, and rather . good-looking, with a strong, resolute face and kind-looking eyes. But he was out of' her world completely. He was only the son of &amp;farmer, and served in a shop for a less salary than her father paid his 'gardener. Nevertheless, he had saved her life, and she felt grateful, and wanted to tell him so, and when she I had done that she would have done her duty, and there would be an end of it. Dorothy was quite right when she said that Ruby looked lovelier than ever. Reelining on a roomy old sofa, well and attired in a gown pob of el mostst eyr ea dll p becomingc t u r ew t (mliaskilei°anng'd material, she made as • as one could wish to see. The afternoon's sunshine filled the room with a soft warm tight, and tinged Ruby's cheeks with the faintest suggestion of colour. Justin caught his, breath when he came into her presence. The reality out distanced far and away all bpi fancies. He often wondered afterwards. what she was dressed in, but ho could never describe a single detail. Dresses at Endilloo were. made for wear and not for artistic effect—that was but a secondary consideration. Ruby's dress might be of very little use in a farm-house; from an. utilitarian point of view, it would be an utter failure, but as a work of art, as a study in neutral tints, as a setting for natural beauty of no common order, nothing could have been better. Justin advanced shyly and somewhat awkwardly, and when she held out her hand to him, he was almost afraid to take it in his lest it should crumple up in his grasp—it looked so white and small and fragile. "I am glad you have come," she said, in her bright frank way. "I have wanted to see you for days." Justin couldto hosraidfortnwitehokfusllybotthahtehweishealdy been pining sire kept silent on • that point, and simply answered that he was glad she was able to get downstairs again. "I little thought when you parried me upstairs that night," she replied, "that I should have been kept a prisoner so long." think we, none of us, imagined you were so seriously hurt--" "Butt I'm nearly all right again now," she inter- rupted, brightly. "Won t you sit down? You are not driven for time, I hope?" "Oh, no; I have nothing to do," he Said, colour- ing' slightly, and dropping somewhat awkwardly into the chair she indicated. She saw in a mement that he was nervous, and so 1. exerted herself to place him at his ease. Dorothy' having seen him. to the door, had retired to an ' adjoining room to talk to Mrs. Loveday. Mr. Lovcday was in London again, looking ,after his business. "Do you know I'm pining to get out of doors 'again," she said, glancing towards the 'window. "I'm half afraid this fine weather will give out before I've a chance to enjoy it." "I ,don't think it , he answered., "We often get lovely weather right into October.° / "I hope it will be so this year. What a-holiday ' I've had, haven't I ? Think of it? I'm really ashamed of Myself for having spoilt everybody's pleasure." "But your getting better makes amends for every- thin"" he said, bluntly. " kind of you to say that," she said, looking at him with frank, smiline eyes. "You have all</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>oeen so good to me. shall have to get used to the idea of being permanently in debt." "Tfl the thought an unpleasant one?" "Oh, no, not in the least. 11 might not be pleasant to be in .some people's debt, f9rsliff might make themselves nasty over it. But Airy - body at Endilloe is so genuinely kind that the Idea of going away in your debt is rather pleasant than otherwise." "We shall all feel sorry when you go," he said, after a pause. "Do you really mean that," she asked, with a little laugh. "I do indeed. We shall miss you awfully." "But you have scarcely ever seen me, or any of us, in fact." _ _</p>
        <p>"But we have grown used to you being in the house," he answered, colouring. "You have created a new interest, if you will allow me to say so. Why, even NE.than and Amos will feel lost when they can no longer inquire at the house how you are.' "Is Amos the man with the son in London'?" "The same." "Oh, then, I like him, Ile's nature unadorned. Don't you think so ? " "Well, yes. There's not inuch adornment about Amos."</p>
        <p>"He quite pitied me Because I did not know what muffles were, and' wanted to know if my father had never sent me to suhool. Dorothy and have laughed over it till we have ached." "He would mean no disrespect," he answered; 'Oh, I know that. That'S, why I like him so. much. He is so simple, sincere; and transparent. I wish I could imitate his manners. I shall have some further conversation with; him when I am able to get out." "Nothing will delight him more. Did Dorothy tell you what he said when the doetor had given you up ? " "No, what did he say?" "I told him the doctors had darter their utmost, and left you in the hands of Provid'ence. 'ls it come to that!' he questioned. l'fold hitti it had. And you should have seen his face brighten. Now, Mr. Justin,' he said, there's a chance for the little maid. Man's extremity is' God's oppor- tunity, and ho turned to his work quite cheer- Ruby did not reply, but a far away look came into her eyes as though she saw something far beyond the bounds of the room. Justin watched her with a strange tninult in his heart. Ile knew well enough that this brown- haired sweet-voiced maiden was all the world to him; and yet had she dwelt in another planet she could not have been further removed.' It was one of' the ironies of lifg, that he should Tall in love. with one so completery out of his sphere,. and after all his protestations, too. Fate seemed to be persistently unkind to the eentyres. They had been robbed of their inheri- tance; so they honestly believed ; they had struggled in vain against bad times; they had steadily come down in ''the world until; to put it ,bluntly, they had to take in lodgers. Dorothy had given her heart to' a man whose memo was never mentioned in the house, because- he was looked upon as a "ne'er-do-weel." And now, to crown all, he had been caught in the toils of an absolutely hopeless love, and would have to nurse an unconfessed grief to the end of his days. Ruby's soft musical voice recalled him to himself</p>
        <p>again.. "It is not easy to thank people, is it ?"'' she was saying: "But you will not think me ungrateful, will you? I can assure you I shall never forget that you saved my life." "Please do not say any more, Miss , Loveday," he interrupted. "I do not want to be thanked, I don't really. It will be the pleasantest memory of my life that I was able to render you a service. "Then we understand each other," she said, with a smile. "And now we will talk' about other things."' Justin by this time felt quite at his ease in Ruby's presence, and for a full hour conversation flowed freely and without a break. Ruby became more and more interested in him. She was sur- prised to discover how much he knew and how well he could talk on matters that he was interested in. She had been inclined to think that city people only were clever and well informed,,.and that country people were necessarily, slow, and altogether behind the age. She had shed several illusions since • she. came into this western county, and some of.' her experiences IA humbled her considerably.. Prom henceforth she would think less and less of outside show. Polish was not everything, after all, especially when there was little or nothing under- neath.</p>
        <p>This Justin Pentyre, with his strong grave face and his shy, hesitating manner, was not to be dis- missed with a wave of the hand as though he were not worth a moment's thought. The longer she talked to him the more she became- interested in him. He belonged to a type that she did not meet every day. His conversation was fresh' and strong, like the wind that blew, in, frogs' the Atlantic. Moreover, he seemed to view many qoestions from what was to her an entirely new standpoint. She echoed the opinions of some London drawing- rooms, which she deemed almost infallible, and he laughingly shook his head and then proceeded very quietly and gently to turn them inside out. So the minutes slipped away imperceptibly. She was so interested in having things-set before her in a new light that she did not mind being worsted in argument. "Men will come and see me again, won't you ?" she questioned, when at length he rose to take his leave-. _</p>
        <p>"I will come as often as you will let me,"' he answered. He knew he would be a moth scorching its wings every time he came into her presence, but be,conld not, help himself. There was some com- pensation in the pain after all. "I suppose you will be late home to-morrow ?"' she questioned. "I am afraid I shall be. Friday is our markett day." 'And Saturday ? " "-We keep open late on Saturday also." ; "Then I must wait till Sunday." “And I may come and see you on Sunday ?" he questioned, eagerly. "I shall be very pleased it you will." So they parted, and he walked through the house into the orchard, his nerves tingfaig with e new sensation, his heart throbbing with a, new joy, and yet a joy that was so near akin to pain that he, knew not whether to rejoice or be sorry.</p>
        <p>-- CIIkPTER X. • A MERE NOBODY. " Jealousy is the forerunner of lc ve, And sometimes its awakener." f-;-7</p>
        <p>In a few days that which was merely " akin * to pain became a very acute agony. To love Baby, even -when there was not the smallest hope of winning her, wag not without its compensations. Ile could dream about her all day long, and weave nretty fancies about her name; he could pay her homage and win a smile from her now and then. He was not an intruder when he went to see her, for she had expressed a wish to see him. But all this was quickly changed. On his return on the following evening Dorothy had news.for him that affected him strangely. "Who do you think has been here to-day," slut said, as he sat eating his supper. "What a question, Dorothy," he answered, with a smile. "Bali a score of people may have been here."</p>
        <p>"Yes, bist I vras not thinling of St. lago people. A stranger has been here from London?" 66 To see me ?" "No, I don't think so. I fancy Ruby is the attrattion."</p>
        <p>"Oh, indeed. Anyone that I know." "Yes, you know him quite well." "Do you mean Cousin Phil 2" "Yes."</p>
        <p>For several minutes there was silence hi the room. Justin appeared to be much more interested in his supper than he had been before. Dorothy watched him curiously for a moment, then took up a book she had been reading and began to turn its leaves. • Justin was the first to.break the silence. "Where's father ? " he asked.</p>
        <p>"He'; gone to'bed. You are so late home to- night." "And mother ? "</p>
        <p>-:She's somewhere about. You know mother is slwsys busy whatever time of day it may be." Another long silence followed— Then Justin spoke again. "How is Phil looking ? " - - • - -</p>
        <p>"WI; well indeed': And what a handsome fellow he is. There is something in London after all." "Why do you say that?" "Well, think of Phil's upbringing and 1004 at him now. Why you would think he was 'to the manner born.' as they say. He is so easy. so self.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>possessed, so tree from angularities. There is nothing shy or awkward about him. He seems to know always what is.the right thing to 30 and the right thing to say." flle has etidently impressed you to-day." "Well he has, Justin, in a way. The St. lago young men Its a rule never know what to do with their hands or feet, and will persist in standing when they ought to sit down, and when they doMt ,down always elect to make themselves tin- aemfortable on the extreme edge of the chair. Now Phil was the opposite of all this. I was with Ruby when he was shewn into the room, and I was struck with his grace of manner. He was frank, unobtrusive, sympathetic, and what he said to Ruby was in perfect taste." - • "Indeed ! " "I am sure city life gives polish to a man." "And evidently that is what women admire." "Yes, no doubt they do up to a pertain point." "And was Miss Loveday surprised to eee him P" "I don't think so. She seemed to take his coming as a matter of comme. You know they have known each other. a very long time. It was through Phil that the Lovedays came here.* "Yes, I know that. And you think she was pleased to see him?" "I am sure she was. You can always telP when a girl is pleased: I have been wondering ever since whether there is any kind of understandng-tietweeltt them. I feel erre Phil is very fond of her." "Likely enough. She is not a bad looking girl,* he answered, without looking up from his plate. "Not bad looking!" Dorothy said, ,reproachfully, "she is perfectly lovely, and the w*eetest-tempered creature I ever met."</p>
        <p>To this he made no reply, and soon after took himself off to bed.. ,He was much more distressed by what he had heard than he yet realised. The thought of Ruby being. loved by 'another and returning that lore, struck deeper than he knew. It is the surface wound that yielda the quickest pain. Wounds that are deep and alaiost mortal are not felt at the moment.</p>
        <p>He was tired and despondent, and even while he was trying to probethe wound caused' by what ho Had heard he fell asleep 'The pain at his heart/ was mueli more acute when he awoke next morning. He began to understand what jealousy meant. He-had read about it in books, but as the day parsed on he realised some- thing of its torment; bat it was not until, the following day that the pawn exercised all its strength. He had looked forward to Sunday. Ruby had invited him to come to see her. The morning broke beautifully fine. The long summer seemed loath to depart. Justin'awoke'to the twittering of bird i under the eaves and to the smell of ripe apples that floated in through ,his open% window from the orchard. . • The beautiful sunshine and the,fresh perfumed air were as atonic to him. It was difficult to feel depressed on such -s- glorious morning. Perhaps he had been worrying himself over nothing. It was the most natural thing lathe world that Phil, having bnsiitess in Cornwalk should call. at Endilloe. Perhajis Ruby cared nothing fey him. Dorothy might be altogether mietaken. He dressed himself in his very best Perhaps by paying a little attention to details, he could look as well as Phil Passmore. He intended calling on Ruby soon after breakfast. .Mr. Loveday was dill in London, and Ruby might wish to ,be wheeled' out into the garden: She was still too lame to walk any distance. " He waited very impatiently for ten &amp;Clock to strike. He learned from Dorothy that Ruby had got down to breakfast, and that she was looking wonderfidly well; and wae!in excellent spirits.- He. knocked very timidly at the Lovedays'• door, and was at once admitted. "I to to see if I could be of any service," he said, sp,c‘king rapidly. "I thought perhaps Miss Loveday would like to be wh4ele4. into the garden —Or—or—carried up into the orchard.' H is lovely owt of doors this morning," and he- looked at Ruby, who was deep in an erierchair; and tried not to feel embarrassed. • • ",</p>
        <p>"It is very kind of you," Mis... Lo. Vedfirsiaid, hesitatingly. "Oh, it would-be delightful," Ruby interrupted, "but it would be keeping you from chapel."' - "Don't let that trouble you in • the feast,'" lx answered quity. "To tell you the truth, In in no particular humour for chapel this-morning," "Then you can go to church if you like," Buby• said, turning to her mother. "I know you•••feel condemned unless you go once on the Sunday!' Bolt came to pass that Justin had Ruby, all to himself; and so completely was he fascinated by her, that he forgot all about Phil Passmore,.or if he thought about him at all, he supposed that he waf3,back Again in London and quite out of the way. After Seeing Mrs. Loveclay off th'ei:lrernises, he wheeled 'Ruby in her. easy thair•• att into the garden,. and when the sun -got-too,bt, he arranged a seat for her in the orchard Land! carried het thitherrin his anis: _ _ _</p>
        <p>For two hour '• and more he .lived'in Paradise, and reerthitely bolted the door ageinst the serpent and every other disturbing element. He knew only too well diet his pleasure would -soon, oome to an end, but he would not think of that now; he Would make the most of it while it lasted:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>"1 Wonder you have not pined yoursett to death," he answered, with a laugh. -"But we shall see you in the park again before the summer is ended." Then the conversation drifted away to matters that Justin knew nothing about. They discussed places and people with great animation and gaiety; they referred to events he had never heard of before. He felt himself outside-1 the Charmed circle; he was an interloper and an intruder. He edged himself nearer and nearer the door, and no one seemed to notice his movement. Con- versation was flowing freely and embroidered with little ripples of laughter. Whether he went ot stayed was a matter of no moment to anybody. He was a mere nonentity. Ruby had forgotten him, to all appearance—forgotten the errand on which he had come.</p>
        <p>_ He grew hot and cold by turns, for he had all the Pentyre's family pride and was acutely sensi• tive. He mentally contrasted his appearance.with that of his Cousin Phil. His best clothes, which were now two summers old, were positively shabby by the side of Phil's well-fitting suit; neither had the easy grace of manner and conversation that his cousin had. How glibly he talked. How -Gam/liar-he was with the Lovedays and yet not too familiar. Hew absolutely he monopolised tipcon- versation and yet never seemed obtrusive. "Yee, 'all, that Dorothy had said was. quite true. The city man had the advantage. He was good renough to entertain, Ruby while there was no one better near, but directly Phil appeared upon the scene he was forgotten. He clenched his fists till the nails made marks in the palms of his hands. He tried to hate the Lovedays and hia cousin, and every one connected with them. And yet all the while Ruby's low musical voice stirred' his heart with a stiangg rapture and filled 'him with an inexpressible longing. What a contrast between the afternoon and the morning. Then he was in Paradise, now he was in Purgatory. 'Then love alone dominated him and filled the world with sunshine. Now jealousy—' mad, unreasoning jealousy—tore him like a legion of devils.</p>
        <p>He was now close to,the door, which stood ajar. No one noticed him or addressed a single question to him. Ruby's face was towards him—a bright animated face—but her eyes were fixed on phiL Mrs. Loveday was looking at her visit&amp; Troia another angle of .the room, while Phil himself, handsome, confident,. Was fbr the moment monarch of all he surveyed. Tustin could bear it no longer. Ifelelt crushed, humiliated, and stung almost to madness. Re stepped back and back, till, the door stood between him and the others. Then he paused and listened. But the conversation flowed on without a break, and the snatches of laughter rang out as before. Re was a nobody. He walked straight through the house into the orchard, and from thence across the field up* into the plantation, but he was toe, restless, too angry to remain still anywhere.. In a few -tinutes he was out on the -cliff pacing restlessly up and down. Then he descended to the beach, and walked out on the sand until the green, curling, wave* stopped his further progress. Why was it that Phil asmore was preferred before himself. Phil had not saved Ruby.- Phil had not risked his life on her behalf, and he was sure that he did not love her as he did. Oh, it was intolerable to be slighted and thrust aside just because he was poor and could not dress in; the latest fashion.</p>
        <p>But why was he. poor?: Of course he would always be poor if he remained in a sleepy Etas village like) Trelford: There was no scope for man where he was.: Phil would have been %to better off than he if helad,remained at home. He sat down on a rock at length and tried to think calmly and dispassionately. He did not heed the sinking sun or the voice of the incoming tide. Slowly a desperate resolution was taking shape in his brain, which was destined, to het° further reaching consequencel than either he tog anyone else-could see. (To :id continued.),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>VERY LATEST INSUHANCE</head>
        <p>THE VERY LATEST INSUHANCE</p>
        <p>AGAINST CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>llnsurance companies nowadays are prepared to - ,- nearly every possible risk, if only they receive a dent inducement. Life policies have been in existen." ifor many years, and so have those on fire and accideeot; but no company has presumed so far as to defy th Ipower of Providence by insuring anyone against tl° dread dangers of consumption. That insurance nlnt and does, and will always !ePend en the natural :IW'. Fational safeguards employed by men and women to Prc7' ere themselves and their offspring from ~,he liability 0, 'attack by this fell and fatal disease. Chills muse be avoided, or their conseqoences remedied. Cougb:ll must be taken in hand before they grow hard a° ! system-shaking, and every possible measure used I°,' Order to bring abotit an early and complete recovery 0' health- Nothing in the world is more injurious to Oa' constitution than a bard and racking cough—nothing 1 imore dangerous or fatal to.the individual and the bora° !circle. Don't risk getting-it, but take timely remediet iLavan's Lung Linctua will cure a cold or cough, an° any other similar throat or lung affection. Try • bottP yourself, and judge by its results—we will stand 5 your verdict. Lavan's Lung Linctua may be had at ,Chemists. Drug Stores, and Co-ops., and post free. is. lid., 2. 9d., and 4s. 6d., from the proprietors,Gree° and Co„ Chemists, Bolton,</p>
        <p>acc°o</p>
        <p>eldreek</p>
        <p>Ruby looked her best, and was as, gracious with him as he could desire. To be out in the sun- shine once more was a real joy, to• her. No one oould'look into her face without realising that she was supremely happy. Justin waited upon her as though she had been a child. She expressed no wish that, he did not try to gratify. He fetched flowers fen her and fruii and books, and carried her anywhere and' every- where she desired to go, and when she wished tc be still he sat at her feet and read to her fragments from Hyawatha. Now and then he glanced at her with eager, hungry eyes. She was so fair, so sweet, that he felt he would give the world did he possess it even for the hope of winning her.. The moments sped away on - feet of down, the hours passed like a dream.. Anti with every passing moment the flame of his passion grew. lie loved her before, but no word' was strong enough tc °repress what he felt now. Ruby, all unconsciously, was binding him hand LOVAS. ELECTIONS. The Local G and foot. Had she known, she might have bestowed had issued alter?Pieq together with covernmen 13" rd forwarded a t elections of of the General Ordcr which fewer smiles upon him, and asked far less atten- ausuirgdthe dates for the proceedings tion. But she was only a girl in her Own eyes, Ft and this strong, sober-eyed. young present year. The lanssubsant?tutDedistrdicatteCsouanscilelorasrdine man seemed almost old to her, and, she never imagined for a elections of Guardisns and District Councillors gin t moment, that he would' ever think of love. present So she allowed him to pay her all the attention year were as follows I—Notice of election 11` later than Friday, the first day of March; eeceiP he desired to pay, and she accepted it with grati-, of nomination papers, not later than 12 o'clock at 000:f tude and without misgiving. It was very pletiaanb on Thursday, the 7th day of March ; sending notice, to be waited on, to be carried in his big strong decision as to validity of no