        <p>'Barrovians speedily beat off .arr ,attempt at inViaien by the Warringtonians, and the visiting backs fielding' the oval, in ~smarter Jashion than the home set, the locality of .the attack Was generally at the Warrington end.' Hallana failed to respond to a )kick by Dickie, and after stiff scrummaging near the posts, the ball was ieeled one in double quick tim.e to Plett, who equalised he score with an exceedingly clever drop. Barrow rade the pace warm. and Warrington were the defend- mg party. Hallam had another kick charged, and the ball was taken over the home line by the Barrow for- wards, but Elwyn'. with a fine turn of speed, upset c al- calatione by kieking dead. Lucky Warrington. During he later stages of the gene,' the players resorted to Ude kick-and-nesh principle, and little or: no . interest was taken in the play; and with the rain fanny.; piteously until the cloy, ties end came as a relief to players and spectators ahke with the score a goal (two points) each. Rtencorrt v. Salford. The severe gale, of 'Saturday, was blamable for toe yew poor attendance at the above match. Rain &amp;- moist incessantly. e and the ground. was very slippery and muddy. To ke.things more unsavory, Salford efaseed, the train connection ;Areirce their appearance on thefield .was a late one, and the funds of the Canoe should beiwelled by a soverign. in cen-equenee. how- ever, when'they did appear, it V 759 noticed that both "fifteefis" were representative. Given the order te start, Farmer landed the oval in the serenity of Philips,which player made a capable return. Richardson kicked well, and relieved enmewbat. Griffiths, marking from a drop it, gave the Salford backs an opportunity, and Ronan ssing was attempted, to come to grief only with a forward pass. The home forwards then kicked, tackled and ?allowed-up keenly, with the result that Salford were kept vary busy in their own half. Smith fielded enartly, and altered the complexion of things. Jolley ailing to gra