        <p>vTHE SEA RAIDERS:</p>
        <p>A A STRANGE TALE OF THE DEVON AND CORNISH COAST.</p>
        <p>slinging to this iwitil tiiree of .its sucker-set ten- Ades, threw four others over the gunwale, as if " ' pith an intention either of o'Vereetting the boat or l'of dram)sering into it. Mr. Fison at once caught • !'tijo Vi. boathook; 'and jibbing :furiously' ,at thii. 11oft tentacles, forced it to deiist.-'IW 'Wad otrijolk• - in the back and almost pitched overboard by the ,boatman, who was using his am' lid resist a similar :f • ttack on the other side of the,. boat. But , the :1• ntacles on either .side at once relaxed their hold at 4 is, slid out of sight and splashed into the water. 5 "We'd better get out of .this,7 104,30, Fison, th -,• ho was trembling violently. He went to the ~ f tiller, while the boatman and one of the Workmen eated themselves and began roleing.,, The other torkman stood up in the fore part of the boat, l ith the boat-hook, ready to , strike any more ;4 Macias that might appear.' Nothing else seen* 'to have been said. Mr. Fieon had expressed' the -immon feeling beyond amendment. In a hushed, 'fired mood, with facets white and drawn, they set ,out escaping from the position into which they. ad so recklessly blundered. . But the oars had scarcely dropped into the water iefore dark, tapering, serpentine ropes had boned ,hem, and were about the rildder ; and. creeping up the sides of the boat with a looping motion came the suckers again. The Mon gripped their.- oars and Pulled, but it wae'like trying to. move a boat lin a floating raft of weeds. "Help here!" cried ithe boatman, and' Mr. Fison and the sedond Work-% pan rushed to helping at the oar. ' ' : I Then the Man with the boat-hook=his'imine was Ewan, or Ewen—sprang, up with a curse, and Ibegan striking downward 'over the side, as far' as lie could reach, at the bank of tentacles that now t iclustered along the boat's bottom, And at the Name time the two rowers stood up to get a better purchase for the recovery of their oars. The boatman , handed his to Mr. Fison„ who lugged desperately, .and . meanwhile the boatman opened a _big clasp-knife, and, leaning over, the side of:. thleboat, began hacking at the . spiting ephil upon the oar shaft. • . Mr. Fison, staggering with the quivering rocking, of the ,boat, his teeth set, his breath coming, short, and the veins starting on his hands, as be pulled at his oar, suddenly cast his eyes seawards. ' And there, not fifty yards off, across the long Killen of the incoming tide, was a large boat standing in towards them, with three women and a little child in it. A boatman' was rowing; and a little man in a pink-ribboned straw hat and white, stood in the stern, hailing them. For a moment, , of course, Mr. Fison thought of 'help, and then 4 he thought of the child. 'He 'abandoned his oat" forthwith, threw up his arms in a frantic gesture; and screamed to the party in the boat to keep • away "for 'God's sake. It says much for the .modesty and courage of Mr. Fison that he does not seem to be aware that there was any quality of heroism in his action at this juncture. The oar he had' abandoned '' was at once drawn' under, and presently reappeared,. floating about twenty yards away. At the same'' moment Mr. ' Fison felt the boat under him lurch violently, and h hoarse scream, a prolonged cry of terror, from. Hill, the boatman, caused him to forget the party .of excursionists altogether. He turned, and saw Hill crouching by the forward rowlock, his face convulsed with terror. ,and his right arm over the side and drawn tightly down. He gave now a succeesion, of short, sharp cries, "Oh! oh! oh I—oh I ",,,Mr., Fison believes' that he must have been hacking at the tentacles below the water-line, and have been grasped by them, but, of course, it is quite impossible to say now certainly' what•hadhappened. The boat was heeling over' So that the gunwale was within ten inches of the 'water; and both'EVran and the bther labourer were striking down into the water, with oar i silid bOat-haek, on either side of Hill's arm:: gi. ;4 t Pisou inetiectively placed himself to couaterPoise ' them..: .'. ~ . Then Hill; who was a burly, powerful man, made a ;strenuous effort, and rose almost to a standing pition..' Ife• lifted his arm, indeed, clean out of tile water.. Hanging to it was a complicated tangle of' brown 'ropes; and the eyes of one of the brutes that had hold of him; ' glaring straight and resolute, shelved momentarily above the surface. The boat heeled more and more, and the green-brown .water , came pouring in a cascade over the side. Then Ifill slipped, and fell with his ribs acre-Us the side, and ' his arm and the mass of tentaclesaboat it Splashed , back into the water. He rolled over, his boot kicked Mr. Fison's knee as that gentleman rushed, ' forward to seize him, and in another moment fresh' ' • tentacles had whipped about his, waist and neck, 1 and, after a brief; convulsive .struggle, in.. which - ; the boat was nearly capsized, Hill wait lugged over-, i board. The boat righted with a violent jerk that 1 all but sent Mr. Fison over the other side, and hid , i the struggle in the water from.his eyes. He stood staggering to recover hiS balance for :4 a Moment, and as he did 'so 'he became aware that 1 the struggle and the inflowiag tide had carried 1 them close Upon the Weedy rocks- again'' Not four yards off a table 'of teele atilt rose in rhythmic imovements above the in-wash of the tide. In a ' 'moment Mr. Fison seized the oar from Ewan, way one vigorous stroke, then, dropping' it, ran 'to the' bows 'and lemied. ' He felt his feet slide over the rock, and, by a frantic effort, leaped again towards 1 ' "a further mass.- He stumbled over this, came to : his knees,'.and rose again: • 5 - ,1 "Imok out:" cried someone, and a large , drab body !;trUck.him. rfie, was'knopked,flat,A4,' a tidid. Eiriebayrdonerne ootfhtehreedwogornn'g''t,"" as he. wentl, itvned 1 . lat ' the' time cane from Hill. -Then tie :....intd himself -Marvelling at the shrillness and variety ci# . Hill's voice.' Someone jumped. over him, and a curving-rush of foamy water peered over him, and passed. He scrambled to his , feet dripping, and, without leokieg seaward. 'an, ab,last as his, terror would Ist him,, shorewm.l., Befara„bim, over the flat space of scattered rocks, stumbled the two i workmen—one a dozen yards in front of the othor, P, He looked over his shoulder, at Ist, and seeing ; he was -not pursued, faced' about,;" He was .' astonished. From the moment of the rising of•the 1, cephalopods out of the water, he had been acting too swiftly to fully comprehend his liOrione.''•Now ii it seemed to him as if he had i dsle4yriampad 1 out of ati,evil ctmam, . , For there were the sky, cloudless and blaziag, with the afternoon son, the sea weltering 'under its pitiless brightness, the soft etaapsf" foam of the breaking water, 'and , the loW;dong,''etkik ridges of j rock. The righted boat float's&amp; rising and faliing 7. gently •on the swell, about • a dozetlards • from shore. • Hill and the monsters, all the 'Strese and tumult of that fierce flit for ,life,' had vanished lik 'though they had nevec..been; Mr. Izison's heart was beating !violently; he wee 'throbbing to the.finger-tips, and hit breath mune 1 ,deep. , . There was something missing., For aome,seconds . , he could not think clearl,y:enough what. this .might be. Sun, sky,-sea; rocks—what was it' Then he remembered the boat-load of excursionists. It hall i vanished. He wendered:whether he had" imagined i it. He turned and saw the Aito'lyorkinen standing ' 1 side by side under the ProjiCtirig &amp;seWsl.if the tall pink cliffs. He hesitated Whether he shourcl make One last, attempt to save the man Hill.' 'His physie cal excitement -seemed to desert hilitt,suddenlj, and leave him aimless and helpless.' IT. tarried ishott;- wards, stnmbling and wading towards his. Ltwo companions'. __ . . _ _</p>
        <p>By H. G. WELLS. Author of "The Island of Dr. Moreau," "The Time Machine," and "The Wheels of Chance." •</p>
        <p>; L The first human being to set eyes upon a living Paploteuthis—the ffirit htiMan being to survive, that is, for 'there can be little doubt now that Pae wave of bathing fatalities and boating accident that travelled along the 'toast of Devon and Cornwall s in' early May was dike ,to this cause—was a re- tired tea-dealer of ,the ..mame of, FiSon, who was stopping at a Sidmouth boarding-house, It was in the afternoon, and hei was walking along the cliff path between Sidmouth and Ladrara Bay. The cliffs in this direction are very high, but -down the red face of them in one' plate a kiad,of ladder staircase has been made.. ,He ,was 'near.,this when his attention was attracted by what at first: he thought to be a clnster•of birds struggling over a fragment of food that caught the sunlight, and glistened pinkish-white. The tide was right At, and this object'waenot only far below him, but remote across a broad waste of rock reefs, ,covered with dark seaweed and interspersed with silvery shining tidal pools. And he was, moreover, dazzled by the brightness of the further water. In a minute, regarding this again, he perceived that his judgment was in fault, for over this struggle circled a number of birds, jackdaws and gulls for the most Rut the latter•gleaming blind- ingly when the sun ht smote their wings, and they seemed minute in comparison with it. And his curiosity was, perhaps, aroused all the more strongly because of ,his first insufficient explana- tions. As he had nothing better to do than amuse himself, he decided to make this object, whatever it was, the goal of ,his afternoon walk, instead of Ladram Bay, conceiving it might perhaps boa great fish of some sort, stranded by some chance, and flapping about in its distress. And so he hurried doWn the long,, steep ladder, stopping at intervals of thirty feet or so to take brtath and scan the mysterious movement. At the foot of the cliff he was, of course, nearer his object than he had been; but, on the other hand, it now came up against the incandescent sky, beneath the sun, so as to seem dark and indis- tinct. Whatever was pinkish, of it was now hidden by a skerry of weedy boulders. But he perceived that it was made up of seven rounded bodies, distinct or connected, and that the birds kept up a -onstant croaking and. screaming, but seemed afraid to approach it too closely. - Mr; Fison, torn by outliosity, began picking his way across the wave-warn rocks, and, finding the wet seaweed that covered them thickly, rendered them extremely slippery, he stopped, rTnOi.ecl hoes and gocks, and coiled his trousers abbye' his knees. His object was, of course, merely to avoid stumbling into the rocky pools about him, and perhaps he was rather glad, as all men are, of an excuse to resume, even for a moment,. the sem- firms of his boyhood. At any rate, it is to this, no doubt; that he owes his life. He approached his mark with all the assurance which the absolute security of this country, against 0 forms of animal life gives its inhahitanti. The round' SAW moved to slid fr 6, but it was only when he surrnotinted•the slrerry Of boulders I have mentioned tat he,realisecl the horrible nature of, the discovery. , ttime upon him with some sud- denness." The rounded bodies fell apart as he came into sight over the ridge, and displayed the, pittliish eb- ject to be the partially-devoured body of a human, being, but whether of a man or woman he was unable to say. , And the rounded bodied.' were new and ghastly-looking creatures, in shape somewhat. resembling an octopus, and with huge and very long and flexible tentacles; coifed copiously on the ground. The skin had's,' glistening texture, un- pleasant to see, like shiny, leather. The downward bend of the tentacle-surrounded mouth,, the curious excrescence at the bend, the tentacles and the large intelligent eyes, gave the aeatures.a, grotesque sug- gestion of a face. They were , the size, of, a fair, sized swine about the bocly, and the tentacles seemed to him to be many feet' in length. There&gt; were, he • thinks, seven or:: eight at least of thei , creature!. Twenty, yards boyond them, amid the surf of the now, returning tide, two others were emerging from the sea. Their bodies .tay flatly on the rockfi, and their eyes regarded,him with evil interest; but it does not appear that Mr. g son was afraid, or that he realise' that he 'Was 'in any dan;ger. Possibly his, confidence is to be aiicribed to the limpness of their, attitudes.,But he washorrified, of course, ,and in- tensely excited and indignaut at such revolting creatures preying Upon 'human flesh. He' thought they had, Chanced upon a drowned body. He, shouted to them, .with the,idea of drivin,g them off, and finding they dienot budge, cast 'about 'him, picked lip a big 'rounded limp. of rock, and flung it at one. . - _ _</p>
        <p>i—And them, slewly uncoiling their tentacles, they all began moving towards him—creeping at &amp;at' deliberately; and making a soft purring sound to' ' each other. , ‘ e;tt• In a moment ;Mr. Fison realised that he walk iRi- , danger. He shouted again, threw both his boot, and started off, with a leap, forthwith. Twenty ! Iyards off he stopped and faced about, judging them slow, and behold! the tentacles of their leader were already pouring over the rocky ridge on which he had just been standing! 1 At that he shouted again, but this time not threatening, but a cry of dismay, and began jump- I ing, striding, slipping, wading across the uneven expanse between him and the beach. '' The tall red cliffs seemed suddenly at a vast distance, and-he i saw, as though they were creatures in, another ' world, two minute workmen 'engaged in the repair 1 , of the ladder-way, and little suSpecting the race for life that was beginning below them. At one time he could hear the creatures splashing in the ' pools not a dozen feet behind him, and once he I slipped 'and almost fell. I IThey chased him to the very foot of the 1 cliffs, and desisted only when he had been joined by the workmen at the foot of the ladderway up the cliff. All three of the men pelted them with `stones for a time, and then hurried to the cliff top and along the path towards Sidmouth, to .secure assist- ance and a boat, and to rescue the desecrated body frothe clutches of these abominable creathres. t. 11. And, as if he -had not already been in sufficient i peril that day, Mr. Fison went with the boat to point out the exact spot of his adventure. 1 As the tide was down, it required a considerable 1 detour to reach,the spot, and when at last they ? came off the ladder-way the mangled bodf had disappeared. " The water was now running in, sub, merging first one slab of slimy rock and then another, and the four men in the boat—the work- men, that is, the boatmen, and Mr. Fison—now i turned their attention from the bearings off shore ' to the water beneath the keeL 1 At first they could see little below them save a i dark jungle of laminaria. with an occasional dart- ing fish. Their minds were set on adventure, and they expressed their disappointment freely. But 7 presently they saw one of the monsters swimming "t through the water seaward, with a curious rolling ! motion that suggested to Mr. Fison the spinning - roll of a captive balloon. Almost immediately lafter, the waving streamers of laminaria were i extraordinarily perturbed, parted for a moment, i and three of these beasts became darkly visible, i struggliug for what was probably some fragment iof the drowned man. In a moment the copious 1 olive-green ribbons had poured again over this writhing group. I At that all four men, greatly excited, began i beating the water with oars and shouting, and . immediately they saw a tumultuous movement I i among the weeds. They desisted to see more clearly, and as soon as the water was smooth they, saw, as i it seemed to them, the whole sea bottom' among I the weeds set with eyes. 1- "Ugly swine!" cried one of the men. "Why, i there's dozens!" ! And forthwith the things began to rise through the water about them. Mr. Fison has since de- ! 1 scribed to the writer this startling eruption out 1 of the waving laminaria meadows. To him it i seemed to occupy a considerable time, but it is , probable that really it was an affair of a few : seconds only. For a time, nothing but eyes, and ‘1 then he speaks of tentacles streaming cut and parting the weed fronds this way and that. Then , these things growing larger; until at last the , bottom was hidden by their intercoiling forms, and , the tips of tentacles rose darkly here and there i into the air above the swell of 'the waters." 1 One came UD boldlv„to therside a the -boat-</p>
        <p>He looked back again, and there were now two boats floating, and the one:furthest out at 130 a pitched clumsily, bottom upeggd.</p>
        <p>- .; - : ,;;X• . So it Was Herplabnthis .ferox made' iteilltrat iki;,. corded appearance upon the Devonshire coat. Mr.'. :Fison's account, taken together with. the 'rave a ;boating :and bathing casualties, and the absence. 'Of fish from the Cornish coasts that year, Obints. Clearly to.' a Shoal c.f these voracious deett-','o6. Monsters prowling slowly along the sub-tidal eoalk 1 , ,line. Hunger migration has, I know, been sur 1 ; estec, as the force that 'drove them hither; but, I 'for my own part, t prefer to balieve th'• alterriati*l theory Of Hentidey.Herneley_llds that a pack' M'' shoal of these creatures may have become enamoured of hniiian flesh by the;isocident of i'lotintlet4fir Sitiis I 1 o"ing,'.among them,- and have '• wandei-ed in v search of it out of their accustomed- Zone; firo. ,* waylaying and following ship's, snacr-so condog 1 ' to our shores in the wake of the Atlantic trailic. kat to discuss Hemsley's cogent find Admirablrj ; stated'artiments would be out of place' here. ' 1 .119Atould ' seem that the appetites of the shoal I 1 'were satisfied by the catch of eleven L;eople-L:-for so 1 far as can be escertainod • there were ten 'people in i , the second boat—and certainly these creatures gave : no further signs of their presence off Sidinonth ' that day. The coast between Seaton and Bialleiola i: Salterton was patrolled all, that eyening and . nigT.#l Fby four Preventive Service boats, the rrOM ha i</p>
        <p>Which were-;;;;;;d"- ith 'l");rpoMe 'and cutl,aBile' and as JO evening aciruneed a number •inure dr .e . 1 e similarly equipped evnechtions oisAnised cV.`ii 1 • )1g Private in le: s, joined them. Itr. tot 00 rt,. in any qi these erpedithins. .17.1 About tmdnigiit excited bath; were be.ird from p boat about a cot rge 'o•Prniiiii-tUtt; at • fit sva:,ll-04k4 sixttuguth.; oxid)wiafitur,li</p>
