      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>[poi cared ~.aabout 71-',.had go</head>
        <p>[poi cared ~.aabout 71-',.had go</p>
        <p>waving in a strange manner' to and fro and ,up ainl r down. The nearer boats at once hurried towards i the alarm. The venturesome occupants of the 1 boat, a seaman, a curate, and two schoolboys, had ;'aptually seen the monsters passing under their boat. The creatures, it seems, like most deep-ses , organisms, were phosphorescent, and they had been floating, five fathoms deep or so, like creatures of I moonshine through the blackness of the water, their i tentacles retracted and as if asleep, rolling over 1 and over, and moving slowly in a wedge-like formation towards the south-east. These people told their story in gesticulated I fragments, as first one boat drew alongside and then tt another. At last there was a little fleet of eight or nine boats collected together, and from them a 1 • tumult like the chatter of a market-place rose, into , the stillness of the night.' ' On such occasions as this there is usually some person of prompt and decisive will who seizes the 1 leadership. This person was found on this occa- sion in Mr. Edwin -Blake", ,an actorPfrOm London, with a remarkably fine ,voice, who. happened to be on board one of the larger boats. -"All lights out I" he shouted' suddenly 'algid 'the din. "Spread.' iout and 'ioiv 'south:east!" doing it with snch an assuraece of authority that bis order wag 11 promptly repeated between boat and boat, and , almost immediately obeyed: To most of those who: I partook in that night's adventure he was • indeed ,but a voice, a will, and beyond that—nothing. Yet his arbitrary decision, carried them all. 'iv They rowed until about half-past one in the . morning, and there was already murmuring enough . t' against, this self-imposed commander,, especially • among those who ware with him upon his Own boat, ' k r when the chase was overtakes. A hailing began `l4 between' boat and boat, and the scattered boats. began' drawing together again:- Itialv Over. theni! I' and get ahead I" cried the voice. , The watchers in,: the bows saw the rolling little puffs of phos- phorescence driving aloag, steadily and slowly far below. So seen, they'-'isettelizifthing but formid- -5 able. And then came a splash in the water, and / it was said 'a lead sinker had been pitched at the ; brutes. "Lights!" bawled the voice. "Light up I" t The watchers peering down, shading theig eyes ' from the lanterns, sew that the ofiward movement of the shoal had ceased.- The strange beasts were . now gathering closer, and their arms .became apparent, each of the suckers ,which beset them P ; shining, a bright spot in the filmy irradiation. I "Beat the water!" cried the, voice. Here and ; there someone obeyed," but Most remained staring. I I' And forthwith, far down, shone the eyes, reflecting ; ' the lanterns, and in another ten seconds the middlel P 0 1 water . was alive, With phosphorescence. ' "They'rej !, coming up!" cried a number of voices, some exul- tant, some alarmed. "Ship oars! Stand by with'! P cutlasses and harpoons! " cried the voice; and the i 1 oars came rattling in and the crew of each boat I . crouched ready, peering overboard, expectant for i , the tentacles that were presently to come writhing 1 i out of the water. "We'll give it 'em I " screarned an old man; intensely excited, carving the air with . a cutlass. , I Only the tentacles did not come writhing out 'i of the water. 1 The pause lengthened. The beast-killers saw, the • things going to and fro beneath 'the 'beam,' saw 1 waving anus aad fluctuating gape of darkaesif. t There was a splashing as eager people 'cut' and ii stabbed at them, though they were yards out of reach. Then the peOple in the boats beedme were U of a rhythmically repeated gnawing gonna,' coming from. the bottoms oflthe boats.. Men said "Hiiiii l : 'and peered questioningly at one another in.:, the' I darkness, then craned dangerously on the aides:: ; What the d--1 were the thiags at now? -‘. !i The realisation came slowly. With--irc-iiiie-the noise of oars thrown leastilyeaito rdvslocka and a splashing' Of water. "They're scuttlibg ha I-6' Blake ;I was equal to the 'occasion or it .is doubtful if anyone would have escaped alivethat night. "Boats keep near each other I he 'bawled 'in-that provi- dential voice of his. "It's no good rowing away,' 1 Each boat clear its -neighbour's bottom.' Ther6 P i was a pistol shot from one of the boats, and then.. with cries and tumult, and one heavy collision, the' boats drew near until each was half an oar's ; length from its neighbour. The shot was repeated, 3 and my informant saw by the light of. the pink 1 flash the startled . faces of the me i; in" the boats 1 i, staring dmin at the pallid 'ghosts of outgo fish . in I the dark water, and gripping oars and boathooks. I I The water splashed into my informant's face, and 1 I he did not see the effects of the shot, but Mr. 1 i Church, the ..man who fired it, claims to have 1 f killed or wounded. eight of these creatures that 1 i' night. It is certain that one bullet at least, fired i k too obliquely, ricocheted, splintered the mast of I 4 4 one of the coastguard boats, and' narrowly missed Io a man. • '4 And then began the strangest p struggle, ,at first chiefly with oars and 'boathooks, !and Paterwards with cutlasses and harpoons lashed to the oar- -1 blades, to fend these beasta from the bottom of the boats. Under the belly of the boats it was im- penetrably dark; one could see the cuttle-fish, but .one could not see one's- oar to aim-, a blow; the . refraction cheated, one,. and, as 'often as not, tlie 1 blades cut and slashed the 'boats, they were defend-. ing. There was' a 'cursing -and--gasping, aa mew c leant uncomfortably' over the sides, a shoutittg of' er gratuitous advice, the blows ,of oar agaiast oar, 4, the -occasional"report of . the revolver,. a cry of 1,: exultation as the blade drove home into the tough ...p: flesh. And when the beasts were struck they would loose their hold, drop with ,sprawling tentacles, ; • then rise again,. swimming towards some un- r' protected space of planking. They seemed to grow 1 p brighter, to glow with the excitement'of battle. 'lhey gripped 'at the oars,' tried to lug themslrom the holders; three they snapped, and two they •' captured; they allowed themselves to be pulled near the heaving surface of 'the, water, and then cutlass and harpoon had a tantalising taste of use. 1 arms Were aching,- the' breath' of-many of-those in the boats came sobbing, end still the struggle continued. But slowly it seemed that the men ' ""Were tvinning. Two' at least of the enemy had gone 'cloWn into the blackness 'of the deep water, inert, 1 , and with limp extended arms, and it seemed had ' , not returned. And'a tentacle slashed off had been i,.ltiggedinto one of the, larger beets as a trophy,. ! and, 'still alive, hopped luminously among the. feet of the occupants.- And then a coastguard boat . came, with a measured'beat of oars, and hailiog ii ' Cheeringly, to join the struggle. .- Then suddenly a strange thing happened. All about the panting, perspiring sea-hunters was light 1.—blinding light, into!, sified moonlight, as it ' leernad, with a touch of violet in its quality, and they saw each other ghastly and dishevelled.' Then they turned their e•tes, in amazement seaward, • and saw the incandescent focus of a searchlight. I-* It was the gunboat Lynx. . Strange and puzzling to the last degree indeed r., must that fight 'have seemed to the sailors on kboard of her—a distant murmur of voices. ,and ~i, dim lanterns rocking close to' the water, and then, r, in the light, a drifting little cluster of boats upon the broad stillness of the starlit summer sea, with ..i j every man aboard striking, as if for dear life, over the side.' But the light from the gunboat was so bright and dazzling that when the men in the boats , turned to the water again they saw it black, with spots of 'colour shewing against its blackness. And suddenly there was a cry of despair. "We're sink- ing! " It came from the little boat which had k first.discovered the shoal that night. They had fonnd the water bobbling into them. A. dozen seconds convinced ' them of the'Ampossi- , bility of baling, and they hastened to board the large boat alongside. And then came the tragedy --', 'of the nigh t. One of the lads missed his leap, and went down between the boats as they came together. %. , Z He ratist have gone down like a stone. There was '. visible, to such as the electric light had not blinded, a, feint writhing lutninosity far down in the. water, a writhing luminosity that darkened, dwindled, and vanished into' the depths.- And I then, one by one, from this boat and that, single stellate ghastly shapes whirled down after that 1 central struggle, and left the water clear and dark. 5 The sinking boat drifted reluctantly downward, .t.iirited And rose again with its keel out of water. ' No one in the boats was anxious to renew the fight. For a long time the boats hovered uneasily, anticipating 'a return of. the enemy, and they came back to Sidmontli, rowing'slowly and painfully, in a loose, -stra . ixtino• n line, after the sun had rise. . - . ,</p>
        <p>, IV. • And now to tell what is perhaps the most astoilishing fact in this whole astonishing raid. We have not the slightest knowledge of the subsequent ;. moveinenta'; of the'',hoal, although the whole South-west roast was now alert for it. But it may, ' perhaps, be significant that a cachalot was ' stranded off Sark on June 3rd. Two weeks and three rlayi- after this Sidmouth affair, a living , haploteuthis Caine ashore on Calais sands. , It was alive, because seviral witnesses inn, its tantacles , itioying in 4 convulsive way. But it is probable that it was dyin.4. A gentleman named Pouchet ,c.. 4 obtained a rifle and shot it. That was Cl,, last appearance of a living ~.7 inploteuthis. -No others were ,seen on the Prench g coast. On the 16th of June a deid body, almost 'L‘i complete, was riAiSd ashore -near • Torquay, sad a i .. -..:—.:. .•4.14.4.4-....111.i...n. ,t-,</p>
