        <p>lOW clays later a L•mtt trom too utarineleaf Station, engaged in dredging off Plymouth, picked •up a rotting-specimen, slashed deeply with a cutlass wound. How the former specimen had coma by its death, it, is, 'impossible to say. And on the last day of June Ur., li:gbert Caine, an artist, bathing near Newlyn, threw pp his arms, shrisked, and wa drawn under. A friend bathing with him made no 4tempt to eve him, but swam at once for the.ehore. "Thbuis the'last.fact to tell of this ex- traordinary raid from the deeper sea. Whether it is really the last of' 'thes'e horrible creatures it is, es yet, premature to say. But it is believed, and certainly it'is to be hoped, that they hays returned now, and returned for good, to the sunless depths of theTtaiddleu seas, ant, of miiich they. hay, so strangely and So inysterionaly arisen. But it Will,be long before people learn again to regard the sea with the' easy confidence of formal days ' fun</p>
