        <p>RUNCORN- LIFIERAI,. CLUBr- The annual meeting of this Club was held on ;Wednesday evening at the lecture room of the club. 199 r vg ,B Brimelow (chairman) presided, end he was supported by Messrs T Lea, .T Dean (trustees) and IA Riley (secretary)._ . _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman i3efore commencing the business of ,the evening said as they all knew on Saturday nest was tue date fixed. for the funeral of the Queen and he did not think that they .as members of the ,Liberal Club should be behindhand in the matter, and he thought they should close the club, and therefore proposed the following resolution; "Chat this the Liberal Club of Runcorn,in general meeting assembled hereby deplore the great loss the nation !has sustained in the death of our, beloved Queen, /and hereby resolve that the club shall remain closed ,en Eaturday, next., the day of the funeral until four o'clock." , lie, trust'ad the vote would be carried 'unanimously. Mr.T. Doan seconded and thought it was a proper 'thing to do as Liberate, though they were not generally credited with so much loyalty. The resolution was then put and carried. The Chairman in an interesting resume of the ,year's work spoke of the splendid alterations 'carried out during the sear, and referred in terms of gratitude to the splendid services of Mr R Moore, who acted on the Building Committee. He also thanked all those who had assisted in giving prizes for handicaps, and periodicals for the rea ,ing room, and , thus made it possible for the co imittee to carry out the 64 ggescion of Mr W Millin . . . .</p>
        <p>gUi</p>
        <p>!ton. He also submitted-the balance sheet of tke !Building Committee, which bad been duly audited by the club's auditors, and it was unanimously !passed.</p>
        <p>lr" . A Ri ey, Submitted the annual report for the P 4-7.. ,past year, Though,not chewing any great financial ;progrese, still there was a slight balance in favour of the year's working an. which, owing principally 'club ineonvenience of anciently carrying on the club during the reconstruction of the premises, might be considered as satisfactory. As will be re: membered this last • annual meeting was held in the !temporary club at Porestess Hell, and it was not until several months afterwards that the club again I ,assembled in it. own premises. At the opening ewe- Amy their President,StrJ T Brunner.Bart.,ll.P.,pre- lided. The proceediugs paasee off with considerable enthusiasm and would be long remembered as a red letter day in the history of the club. Referring to the general election the secretary said it was emin- sstitlY satisfactory to be able to state that the club ,as reconstructed was found convenient, suitable, iand adequate,. in every respect as the local rallying i tplace tihring the etcitement of the general election. The club and its member; w/th the cordial help of la lame number of,Liberals who bad not previously ;identified themselves, with the club as member; took no mean share in securing for the fifth time the election of their President. 6ir John T Brunner, IBart., as member for the Nerthwich Division. The icommittee in previous, reports had also regretted Ithat.the premises did not &amp;Cow of their making :suitable.provision for the literary and educational [side of the work. Since the election this work, with 'enlarged premises, had been vigorously taken up lind.a number of lectures had been given and dis- cussions taken place which 'could not fail to have ati7tducational influence on all the members of. the , Club' and especially those who had 1 listened to or taken . part therein. The attendance at thee had been encouraging and the f *lel committee who had undertaken the arrang- merits intended to continue the work of Liberal education. A separate reading room bad also been i/opened, well - supplied, in addition to the daily paperelwith many of the magazines of the day. A .number of these bad been provided by the gener- lositY Of members; to whom the thanks of the corns Imittee were duelbe reading room and its facilities 'were much appreciated by the members. The corn- Mittee also intended to hare a Library in connec- ;Von with the reaiing-;com, to which, Sir John, ,had with his usual generosity contributed a full set of the works of Dickens. The committee would be glad. to receive donations of books from either members of the Club .or _members of the Liberal Party. As alesult of improved premises nod the quickening interest of a general election,the membership of the. club had considerably increased, especially of younger men,, and it, was hoped their association an. nsernhere would fit them to worthily uphold the ' ftag LA itieralisre in the century on which we had i ‘surecently entered:. The statement of accounts for the past year, and also the special accounts for the</p>
        <p>,01!)t</p>
