        <p>Iplement as a steel pen did not come into use' 1820, when thefirst'gross of three-slit pens Awas sold wholesale for £7 4s. A better pen is now</p>
        <p>Muni</p>
        <p>td for fourpence a gross. Pens, first used in 1 )and by Catherine Howard, were of brass, and fit was only in 1824 that they were manufactured 1 by machinery under a Yankee patent, and thus be- _ a synonym for something worthless. Tea 1 and sugar and salt were articles so heavily taxed that they were regarded as luxuries ,by the poor. The restrictions:on the importation of foreign corn, so great,that half ,the,population were ill-fed. 1 'There was:no literature for the people, and news- !papers were so- taxed that only the well-to-do' J /icould secure them. Even bad they been reduced</p>
        <p>Engl</p>
        <p>"rcame</p>
        <p>Nwere</p>
        <p>1 in price to something likeYwhat they are now, the vast majority would have been unable to read I ,them. The Society for the Diffusion of Useful Knowledge had not then started, and penny serials were at least as unknown and unthought of, as penny telegrams are now. Music, now the enjoy- ment of the million, was then the heritage only of the affluent. There:was:no Royal Academy of Music, and twenty yeart had to elapse before Mr. IHullah and others began to popularise the study. 'There were no harmoniums; and pianos, now articles of furniture and playthings for workhouse 'inmates, were then objects of luxury. Lucifer matches had not been invented. To procure a ht the old flint and steel arrangement had to be Jmployed, and after the light had been procured it was not to be compared with, say the present day 'electric light ! In the year of Her Majesty's first birthday certain classes were denied the most coveted privilege of citizens. Her Majesty was nine years old when,the,Act was repealed that re- quired all officers, civil as well as military, to receive the sacrament; and take the oath against " transubstantiation "; and she was only a year I older when the Roman Catholics were emancipat- The Queen had reigned no fewer thani twenty-one years before one of her Jewish subjects' could take a seat to which he had been elected in the House of Commons. In 1820 high treason was not an uncommon crime, and in May of that yeari five •nen were executed as traitors for what wasl kilo</p>
        <p>Alig)</p>
        <p>lied.</p>
        <p>[own as the Cato-street conspiracy. There arel not wanting in this:year of grace, 1901, signs thatl changes of even greater magnitude, political and scientific, are approaching. It seems as if science is now about to grasp the secrets of nature, and to utilise her forces at will. It is probable that pro- gress for the next eighty years, in this direction,' will be even more rapid than in the past. But in one respect there is, happily, no indication of any new departure. Loyalty to the throne does not, abate with a change of its occupant. It has im- measurably increased and deepened during the past' sixty-four years, and there is every reason tol believe it will continue to do so, and that the Con-1 'stitution will become stronger and more deeply Irooted in the affections of the people as the years Igo by.</p>
        <p>THE arrangements for The Queen's Funeral the QUEEN'S funeral are and its Lesson. being settled in general ;accordance with precedent and the country's wishes. The event will be rich with international significance and eloquent of domestic affection.l Every civilised Government in the world will bel represented, in some cases by heads of the Reign- ig House. There will be royal princes, ambassa- idors, and representatives of the people. Courts and chambers will send their tokens of respect. The world feels that some binding force, some in- tellectual power, and some moral dignity has lamed away, and in bearing testimony to this ,ct a new recogaition is given of the position of Great Britain amongst the nations, a sensible 'effort is being made to preserve the equipoise which had been obtained, and countries regarded as hostile are falling into line with the rest, compelled to do so by an irresistible sentiment. Naturally, we are prouder of this appreciation than of anything else in the way of pageantry and splendour. The squadron of German ships which is coming to take part in the naval spectacle is only one of many indications of the friendliness 3etween the KAISER and EDWARD VII. as repro- ating the dead QUEEN. The rest of the world as been touched by the KAISER'S demonstrations. 'lltiey are welcome to us all. They have inspired !other Powers to actions of corresponding import- ance, and we begin to realise that this country is of the most potent factors in the modern orld,the champion of liberty and peace, the cohe- sive bond of the several Monarchies, the holder of (the balance of power. The logic of all these testimonies cannot be denied,and even the Repub- , are strongly impressed by it. The QUEEN'S death has compelled this survey of the situation, and revealed what had been hidden out of sight. Her funeral will bring it more and more into prominence. It is the one great fact with which the new century starts. There had been whispers of new rivalries, and of the decline and fall of. Great Britain. No one foresaw any 'change, any swift and dramatic interruption to the chatter of young prophets. But the blow vhich laid low a venerable Sovereign has given a - from the:invisible, and the nations are better able to understand their power and their weak- The supremacy of England is established. re nations perceive , what subtle influence has 3manated from this country, and what has been achieved by a Woman, high in principle, versed in (diplomacy, indefatigable in devotion to the well- being of her country, praying always for the: of the world. No other and better lesson can ever )0 taught by the funeral of a Monarch, and every- is being quietly and orderly prepared to lake the lesson effective, pictorial and permanent. reaction on the British race of this revelation destiny ought to be immense. We have to live up to the position events have made for us, and the will and wisdom of a great Sovereign have com- bined with the high-mindedness of our statesmen to establish beyond all cavil. It presents itself to us as a magnificent incentive. In what mood, with what hope, shall we begin the new century ? we have been asking. The answer and the inspiration! have come.</p>
