        <p>Rivill</p>
        <p>Special memorial services for the Royal deadl gill be held at almost all places of worship, and we doubt not that they will be attended by a devoutl ,throng anxious to pay one last tribute to her whom they so loved and venerated in life. The Mayor of Wartington has already announced his intention to attend divine service at the Parish :Church at noon to-morrow, and invited thei ttownspeople to accompany him. After thei religious observances the declaration of the !accession of King Edward VII will be fread from the Town Hall steps by His [Worship, who received the important document' on MoLday last. The:idea of withholding it until! Ithe remains of the Queen had been interred is onet that will meet with general favour, and the Mayor, • is to be congratulated on having so fittingly; !utilised the occasion. Warrington people are in-; tensely gratified at the opportunity which will thus' be afforded them of acclaiming their loyalty to His Majesty the King, especially as, a week ago, I ithe Boroughs looked likely for being ignored in</p>
        <p>RKing</p>
        <p>Iwas</p>
        <p>that</p>
        <p>4wit</p>
        <p>the matter of reading the proclamation. Such an event is one of great inspiration and import, and to miss it would have solely grieved the inhabi- tants of the " good old town.'</p>
        <p>lln the course of a few days the attention of the ,public of Warrington will be very effectively drawn! Ito the approaching simultaneous and united mis- sion, promoted by the Evangelical Free Churches] of the town. Every house in the Borough is to be visited two or three times and already a large army of volunteer canvassers is in possession of the leaflets which they are to distribute on their first round. The mission has already commenced in London and on February 16 th,the unprecedented spectacle will be witnessed of a simultaneous' mission throughout the length and breadth of this country. Such an outburst of religious zeal in which all the Free Churches have unitedly joined hands is a tangible proof of the unity of purpose of this important branch of the Christian Church in England. It is true that credal reasons are responsible for the fact that two of the great religious bodies are taking little or no part in this great evangelistic work, hut nevertheless it is a great step in advance to see such a huge body of Christian workers agree- ing for the time being to ignore their various !sectarian differences, and co-operating for a com- mon object. It is a unique enterprise of Christian' endeavour in this country, and the enthusiisml which has already been aroused augurs well for its ,success.</p>
        <p>We have always had a warm admiration for; Friendly Societies, and particularly for the Recha-I bites, whose principles of total abstinence make' them a body all the more deserving of commenda- tion. The Rechabites had their birth in, and have 'grown with, the Victorian era; and their progress has been not the least remarkable event of that truly wonderful period. Two years before the late: Queen ascended the throne the Rechabite Order,l was formed in Salford with ten members. Since' then it has grown and grown until there are few parts of the civilised world where it is not known and respected. Warrington itself possesses a thrivingt Rechabite community over a thousand strong, in connection with which important meet- ings have been held this week. Brother A Bennett, High Chief Ruler, has beeni present and delivered thoughtful and encouraging, addresses to the workers, in the course of which he' referred in words of practical wisdom to the drink evil and its solution. Mr Bennett is a believer in! local option and argues with unanswerable logic that as King Edward VII. exercises his prerogative of keeping his private estates clear of drink-shops, so the subjects of the King should have the right to say whether or not they would have these, ,sources of temptation in their respective communi- ties. There is an excellent panacea for the drink here, and the Rechabites would do well ester 9" I prof. finani</p>
        <p>foster any such beneficent scheme that may Ipounded. They are strong numerically and dancially, and no task should be too great nor i any purpose to high. v</p>
        <p>The Warrington Board of Guardians has takeni a step in the right direction in agreeing to adopti the new dietaries order of the Local Governmenti Board without any unnecessary quibbling in re- gard to the advantages or disadvantages of thel proposed change. Under this order, the Warring- ton Board is empowered to add little luxuries to the daily diet of deserving inmates. Until within' a comparatively recent period, the good and the dicious,the deserving and the undeserving poor,have been compelled to mix together in the workhouse and to accept the same diet. By means of a classi- fication scheme the inmates have now been separated into several classes and under the new dietary order those in the most respectable classes will be entitled to receive little extras, which will no doubt be as welcome as they are desirable. Those who have had an opportunity of examining workhouse dietaries will readily admit that the rntions are neither excessive nor luxurious. The chief objection to the new regulations is that of Npense. It is argued that a more extensive diet- ary will result in waste and that the additional cost of cooking etc., will be considerable. Now this aspect of the case has been thoroughly sifted, by a committee of the Warrington Board, andl after going into all the details this committee is ofl opinion that the increased cost will be infinitesi- mal, and that the advantages gained far outweigh! any monetary consideration.</p>
        <p>Rexpense,</p>
        <p>Train-travellers of both sexes have lately had 'ample cause for disquietude and even alarm clor-i ing the course of their journeyings to and fro. First of all there was the brutal murder of an un- I offending old gentleman in a London train, andi ',hen, very shortly after, the outrages on ladies in 'ie trains running between Manchester and Stock- ,ort occurred. The first case is still sub judice, but sentence was passed yesterday in•the others, the prisoner being committed to penal servitude for periods amounting in the aggregate to seven rears. This is severe punishment, especially when . is considered that no serious crime was perpe trated, but the intention was evident and the prisonor has not been too harshly dealt with. Railway travelling under its present conditions ex- poses the public to many perils of the kind in- stanced, and the judges of assize cannot be too severe in their punishment of offenders against the srson, or a very horrible and dangerous class of ..ime would rapidly increase. Safety will only be ensured by the more general adoption of corridor ! trains, which will do away with the present unsafe and inadvisable isolation of individuals.</p>
        <p>p?rpe-p</p>
        <p>Some reaction against the much-talked-of' (" tube "system of railways appears to have sot mlil London. It is not that the electric undergrour lines are less popular that they used to be. Tht. crowded carriages from the City to Bayswateri every morning and evening prove that positively ; And there ia also to be found a negative demon-1 ration in the decline in the receipts of the Met politan District, and the neccesity which now arises of substituting electric for steam traction' in the tunnels, the foul atmosphere of which has so long been a reproach. It is, rather, that the, other side has at last been heard. The convenience' of the public is not for a moment in question; but the owners of property along the route of the " tube " railways have found a voice, and are to be heard complaining. vigorously of vibration pro- duced by the running of trains, albeit at a depth ' of from fifty. to a hundred feet below the surface. The matter is at present under inquiry,and it is not a little interesting from a scientific point of view,L waves of vibration flung to considerable distances,, sometimes hundreds of feet vertically and laterally,' have strong resemblances to seismic disturbances,l and will, no doubt, be tested in precisely the same manner. If, however, the injury to property is found to be serious, there is no doubt it can easilyl be remedied by the construction of double tunnels, or in some similar manner. The other great objection comes from the residents of Hampstead, who are afraid of damage to their famous heath. It would be deplorable if the heath should in any ,respect lose its character, and yet it does not appear v the beautiful district, which has not been. !spoilt by hundreds of thousands of Bank Holiday! merrymakers, will be ruined by the advent of al new means of approach. In any case Londoni 'must grow, and there is the consolation that in its, !progress there is more respect for beauty and; 'antiquity than in the past.</p>
        <p>lkind Ina will</p>
        <p>tro-N</p>
        <p>,asT!</p>
        <p>gamela.,nd</p>
        <p>greatk,</p>
        <p>Some controversy, of rather more than historical , 'interest, has arisen as to whether the title or "Defender of the Faith." which has been proudly borne by British Sovereigns from the time of , King Henry VIII., is not the one conferred on; that monarch by Pope Leo X., but a second con- ferred by the British Parliament. It is viell known, that the original grant was made in consideration' of the King's having written;in Latin a work against Luther, entitled " Assertio Septem Sacrament- !arium." The book, under Wolsey's instructions, , sent to the Pope, who was pleased with its icontents, and still more satisfied, probably with Ithe Royal authorship, for if the word of the then English Ambassador at Rome is to be trusted, Leo commended the book supra sidera. At all events, Cwith the consent of his cardinals, he conferred on the King the title of " Fidei Defensor." Fourteen years lat3r, however, when Henry jhad renounced the Papal supremacy, and had 'sealed his rupture from Dome with a series of I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>:executions of those who declined to admit hif 'claim to be Head of the Church, the then Popes Paul 111., naturally considered that the raerits of the King's treatise had been over estimated, and by his famour Bull excommunicated the Monarck and withdrew from him all titles of honour, including that of "Defender of the Faith.' The King at once had an Act passed to confirm him in the title, and declaring it high treason for anyone to attempt to rob him or his successors of any -Mon of the style by which the Kings of ,ngland had been known. It seems a mere aatter of the point of view whether this particular Ititle was conferred by the Pope, by Parliament, or by both; but no doubt there is much room for argument of the hairsplitting kind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. ---- A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. ---- A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Memory will scarcely serve with most people to carry them back to the funeral procession and the crowds that gathered in London from all parts of the country at the death of William the Fourth, although many of us are old enough to remember !the overcrowding by country people at the funeral obsequies of the Duke of Wellington. The manage- :ment of the crowds to-day has been a subject of 'serious deliberation with official London during the week, and it is said by a correspondent that every imaginable obstruction will be placed in the way of people travelling from the provinces, or even from the suburbs, to Nlitness the funeral procession of the beloved Queen Victoria. It seems that on this auspicious and solemn occasion, it has to be " Lon- don for the Londoners," and even worse. For the residents in some of the southern districts even of the metropolis hay e had notice of an entire stoppage of railway communication with Victoria station until mid-day; and it is alleged that the Scotland Yard authorities have cancelled all the driving licenses for to-day. From all of which it may be gathered that very little opportunity will be afforded provincial people who may have been thinking of journeying to the scene of to-day's somewhat imposing, though truly mournful cere- mony.</p>
        <p>vf(ml</p>
        <p>4lpeo</p>
        <p>daus'</p>
        <p>gaffi</p>
        <p>IThe arrangements for the funeral of England's Olean are on a scale befitting a monarch who was r not given to ostentatious display. His Majesty I'the Sing chose the happy medium by deciding that the funeral should be naval and military, and yet ,not unduly splendid in pomp and ceremony. If her Majesty was "the daughter of a soldier," and the head of the Army, she wa3 also " mistress of the ea," and both branches of the service will have lhad the rare honour of taking part in the funeral of la British Sovereign. Numerically, the fleet to.. /day compares favourably with that which was seen at the Naval Review held in the Diamond Jubilee year, Throughout the Kingdom the people have expressed and made man ifest their grief, and each 'subject would have liked in some way to mark how deep was his or her lova for the departed 'Queen. This, however, has been found impossible. IA few thouQands of people will be able to do hom- age to ihe memory of Queen V‘ctoria as the procession pisses from Victoria Station to Paddington. Then the coffin will be conveyed to Windsor, where the last rites of all will end the history of a noble life and a glorious reign.</p>
        <p>Ahovi</p>
        <p>be</p>
        <p>I It is scarcely possible to realise as yPt the 'numerous and sweeping changes to which the i whole country will have to accustom itself by-and- 4 bye, as the result of the accession to the throne of : King Edward VII. Unfamiliar allusion will for a time Eeem to be encountered at every turning. The , Prince of Wales is lost in the King Emperor, while 1 the gracious lady who was known 83 long as the n; Princess of Wales is now the Queen Consort. In ' colloquial language her Majesty is spoken of as 'Queen Alexandra; and thus the accession of King ' Edward and Queen Alexandra to the throne of 1 IGreat Britain introduces one entirely new name into the record of those :who have occupied I that position. This is an event of no little histori- • ical significance The Prince who lately held the * 'title of the Duke of York may by special patent ' henceforth during the new reign assume the title 4 of Prince of Wales, and his wife that of Princess of Wales. Much of the phraseology of Parliamen- tary forms will be altered; and the Nati,nal , Anthem will undergo revision. The Queen's Bench Division will become the King's Bench Divi- sion, and the Q C.'s will become li.p.'s : the Queen's Navy will now be known as the King's Navy. and 44 the " Soldiers of the Queen" will be transforrxrd a' into the" Soldiers of the King." From the !stint new coins, bearing the effigy of the King, will have A to be issued, or possibly the precedent may be ' followed of William and Mary, on whose ciins were busts of the King and Queen, with the words " Rexet Regina" on the reverse. The existing device on the postage stamps has suddenly become • an anachronism, and will have to be superseded by another historically accurate. Edward VII. is the first English King to have his " head " printed on a postage stamp. Postage stamps were introduced in 1841, four years after Queen Victoria's accession. The revision of the currency of the postal franks will involve enormous labour and expense, but the , work will have to be accomplished and probably the postal changes will come first. Meanwhile,the . Queen's stamps will remain quite valid,and Victoria coins will not be withdrawn under any kind of pressure. The coins of George and William re- mained legal tender a long time after the accession • ,of Queen Victoria.</p>
        <p>Fri'</p>
        <p>dAni</p>
        <p>4Thl</p>
        <p>For eleven days now the nation has been in deep mourning, and although the people loyally and will- lingly make the sacrifices it implies, the truth can- not be disregarded that it means something more than a period of self-denial for Very many families of the poorer class. It falls in the midst of the pantomime and winter entertainment season, and thousands of persons who live from hand to mouth, as the saying goes, on what they can make by giving Ileasure to others, are suddenly bereft of the means earning anything. Business of nearly every has been at a standstill, and most tradespeople experience a sense of relief when next week their custom and customers return, as it ii anxiously expected they will,</p>
        <p>,1f</p>
        <p>--- The duty or necessity for closing to-morrown not having been too well or generally understood by tradesmen in most places it may be mentioned here that the preamble to Act 34, Victoria, Chap. 17. quoted by "Order of the King," is entitled: "Ai Act to make provision for Bank Holidays " It has no bearing on any business or profession outside banking; nor is there any existing law rendering partial or total closing on Saturady compulsory.</p>
        <p>The encyclical letter of the Bishops of the Church of England which has been published appears to have caused great perturbation in the minds of the sympathisers with those clergymen who refuse to obey "the godly administrations "of their Bishops. The organ ot the recalcitrants in the press, says of the encyclical that it " undoubtedly marks a new stage in the Catholic revival, and heralds, we fear, very troublesome times both for the English Bishop and their flocks." It is admitted by one writer thats 4 practically brands those who refuse obedience, "as rebellious,against all authority and as guilty of the sin of schism." None of the clergy who write thus allude to the fact, which is patent to all, that the letter was not sent to clergymen individually, bat was merely published in the press. The truth is, that the Bishops in this case recognised the weight of public opinion, which has become so strong that they were bound to show they were doing something to procure order in the Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>tettzts to tlit Obitor. NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. HUMANITY—No room this w,,k.—ED. “ExestiNEß."</head>
        <p>tettzts to tlit Obitor. NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. HUMANITY—No room this w,,k.—ED. “ExestiNEß."</p>
        <p>Our Late Queen A Reminiscence. Sir,—At this time of universal sorrow for the gracious ad beloved Sovereign who has just passed away the Collowing incident may not be without interest to some if your readers who may have seen at different times ty verse' in " The Examiner." In 180 I printed a small volume of original prose ,nd verse for private circulation. Ou the appearance )1 the little book a dear friend, long since gon to his est, strongly urgea ins to send a copy to her Majesty the ground that he believed there was something in I) spirit of the pieces that would be congenial to her lad. I complied with his advice, and the following II copy of the reply I received : " Sir Thomas Biddulph as received toe Queen's Commands to thank Mr lindinning for his little book " Quiet hours," whico :sr Majesty has been graciously pleased to accept." " Osborne, 24th April, 1868." The incident is small in itself, and I only quote it *0 addition to the innumerable instances ot gracious (Condescension and sympathy for the lowliest of het which marked the life of our late beloved Queen. Yours truly, J. U. W. CLINDINNING. , P.S. I enclose the !dot for your inspection, sa°l st convenience.</p>
        <p>an fo' of and of tt rest. L oo t ; the . nalr . a c has I Cli • c HE • an Cot ; people ; Mita</p>
      </div>
