      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY, RUNCORN. On Monday evening, a prize distribution took place at the Salvation Army Barracks. An excellent" tea was provided, free, for about 200 children who seemed highly pleased with the repast. The prizes were distributed by Mrs. Dodd, who gave a helpful address to the juveniles. Addresses were also given by the officers end teachers. TESTIMONIAL TO A WIDNES FOOTBALLER. On Friday evening a meeting was held at the Alexandra Hotel to consider the proposed testi- nionial to Mr D Brennan, of the Widnes F.C. and to recognize his merits as a footballer. Councillor Smith was elected chairman, Councillor Gerrard treasurer, Mr Owens secretary. Books have been placed in the hands of helpers at each works, and it is hoped that a ready response will be made. SWEARING TO SERVE THE KING. Last Monday afternoon, at two o'clock, Superiu tendent Keighley, Inspector Strickland, Sergean, Ratcliffe, Williams, Carter, Bennett, Cummings, Blackhall,and about forty constables in the Widnes and Ditton division of the Lancashire County Con- stabulary were sworn in before Mr Samuel Sadler, County magistrate. The ceremony took place in the Police Court, Mr C Spencely, magistrates' clerk, ad- Ministering the oath: The swearing of allegiance, to King Edward VII. was of a purely formal char- 'aCter, though none the less necessary. CHURCH OF ENGLAND TEMPERANCE SOCIETY, WIDNES. Special sermons were preached on Sunday in connection with the above society, at qt. Mary's Church,by the Rev. Webster Hall, MA, vicar of St. Cleopas, Liverpool, in the morning, and by the Rev 11 E Corlett in the evening. At both services re ferences were mede to the loss the nation had sus-) tained in the death of her Majesty. At St. Paul's' Church the morning service was conducted by the Rev W C Unwin. and the evening service by the Rev W Stanton Jones, vicar of St. Polycarps, Liver- pool. The Queen was eulogized by both preachers as being in every respect a model woman. MEMORIAL SERVICE AT ST. PAUL'S. The Nonconformists of Runcorn are determined to show they give place to none in the matter of their loyalty to the memory of our late Queen Vic- toria, and at one o'clock on Saturday, the doors o St. Paul's Church, Runcorn, will be opened, an Memorial music played therein until 2 p.m., when United Nonconformist Memorial Service will cornraenee. The service will be token part in by all the Nonconformist ministers and choirs in the town, and Miss Clara Taylor has also kindly consen- ted to sing the solo "0 rest in the Lord." All seats Late free, and arrangements for an overflow meeting "Jave been made at Bethesda Church. BIINCORN FREE LIBRARY. A meeting of this Committee was held on Thurs- day evening,tlr S Pope presiding. The librarian(Mr D Jones) reported that Mr A Hughes, of Llan- dudno, an old Runcoraian, has presented several volumes on miscellaneous subjects Sir John T Brunner, Bart., M.P., had sent papers issued by the Gold Standard Association. The Librarian reported that an application form for a series of Gilchrist Lectures had been forwarded to them, together with certain particulars. It was decided to have a course of lectures if funds were forthcoming. The question of the misconduct of children in the reading room and vestibule was again discussed, and it was deci- ded that children under 15 years of age should be excluded after 6.30; and that a notice to that effect be fixed in the vestibule. It was also decided to engage a caretaker for the evening. RtrNCoRN "FISHING CLUB" ANNUAL MEETING. The annual meeting of the members of the Run- corn Anglers' Association was held at Mr A. Riley's, Bridge-street, on Monday evening. Mr. John Bate occupied the chair, and there was a good atten- dance. The report for the past year showed the association had passed through a very successful season, the number of specimen fish weighed in by the members being a decided increase on any pre- !ions year. The balance sheet revealed a very sat- isfactory financial position, there being an available balance, after the payment of all expenses, of £2 13s, There were between 50 and 60 members on _the books. The prize winners were as follows :- Roach ; LA Rawlinson,l4loZ3 1 drm ; 2, T Worrell 14-i ors 3 drms. Pike; 1 and 2, Lea, slb 13 oz and 32 lb. Trout ; 1, T Worrell, ln ozs; 2 J, Ildlicitt, 9 ozs 3 drms. Bream, 1, W Hayes, 2lb 2 7 ozs ;2, T Worrell, 2 lb. Perch; 1, T Lea, Ilb 4 ozs . George Hayes, 14 ozs 2 drms; It was decided • o,r the next season to include gudgeon, dace and eaqb in the prize list. The following officers were Vected for the ensuing year ;—President, Sir R M r,2olce, Bart. ; vice-presidents, Messrs. F J Norman E unison, W R Frith, J Stokes, S Walton .1: Lea and T Dean ; treasurer, Mr P Comes ; secre- tary, Mr J Bate ; committee, Messrs. S Bailey. 3 Elliott, E Starkey, J Robinson, G H Davies, S Wor- rell, r Plant. jun . F Able, T Lea, 8 Barker, Isaac Clare, and E Evans. A PUBLICAN FINED. At Frolsham Petty Sessions on Wednesday James Worrell, landlord of the Bridge inn, Prod- sham, was summoned for permitting drunkenness on his licensed premises on January lltb.—Mr. Brassey of Chester, appeared to prosecute on behalf of the police, and Mr, A Browne, of Warrington tepresented the defendant.—P.C. Dicken gave evi- dence to the effect that he was passing the Bridge, tan,at closing time(lo p.m.) on the date named,and e saw 20 men come out of the house. The meni 'eft the house in two batches,and among the second Portion there was a man named Parkinson 'who was so drunk that he had to be supported' by tae, men down the lobby. Subsequently he was found lying in the road by the police, and he was taken to the police station and locked up. The next morning he was brought before the magis- trates, and having pleaded guilty to being drunk he was Convicted. After lodging the man in the Police-station, Sergeant Hunt and P C Dicken went back to the Inn, and wakened up the defendant. They asked him what he was doing to turn out Parknson in such a drunken state, and he said he did not know he was in such a condition. Parkinson was called by the police, and he now denied that he was drunk. The l defence was that Parkinson was an expert at play-1 ing a tin whistle, and he was entertaining the corn-' pany with some favourite selections. It was ad- mitted that the man had whisky, beer, and stout, but he left the house quite sober. He went across to Mrs Caldwell 's shop and had a bottle of "pop." A number of men who were in the heuse with Par- were called and stated that in their opinion be was sober when he left the house. The bench Considered the case proved, and fined the defendant .£1 and £1 64 coste, and ordered him to pay an ad- vocate's fee of one guinea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>-- Annual Meeting at Runcorn. The annual meeting of the CheshirelGrand Division 'Vas held on Saturday in the Greenway-road schools. Mr William Dunbabin G. W,G., occupied the chair. The president in kis opening speech, thanked the officers and members for their loyalty during the Past year. Ha rejoiced in the progress they had th, ade, and he strongly advocated legislation on the, lines of Lord Peel's report. He had a pleasant duty to perform and that Was to present a number of prizes, kindly given by Mr John Brown of; We-ton. The Ist prize was a splendid book, given to Leonard V hyte, 'Men who win" with the Inscription.—" For noble work done in the Cadet ',Section." The 2nd prize was given to Frack John- son, entitled "Living to purpose." This book con- ftained the same inscription "For noble work done la the Cadet Section." Both these youths had pre- , vtously won prizes. The 3rd prize, an emblem of the order, was given to Lily Atherton of Weston ruint. Special attention is given to the young, With the result that there is a steady increase in numbpr. Mr Thomas 3 Holt, grand scribe, gave an exhawtive report on the financial and numerical Po:.ition of the Order, showing it to be 'much batter than it had been for years. The report was unanimously adopted. The officers for the ensuing year were then elected. Mr Daniel Bisbrown, Runcorn, was appointed G G P jpresi- tient) ; Mr Samuel Dodd, Weston, associate ; Mr Thomas lleesom, Runcorn, treasurer; Mr John itarrilet, chaplain ; Mr Alfred Price, Crewe, con- ductor ; Mr Philip Leicester, Northwich, sentinel ; Messrs Joseph Bowering (Crewe), Thomas R Whyte (Runcorn), nd John Jones (Warrington), trustees ; Messrs James Johnson and Alfred Noden, auditors; Mr John Brown, of Weston, was appointed grand Patron of cadets. The following gentlemen were paced on the Management Committee :—Messrs Thomas Cooke, Thomas Clarke, William Hazlehurst and John Dunbabin. Weston was selected for thel, 11. place of meeting. An excellent tea was pro-1 vyled by Miss Shinglar, to whom a hearty vote of thanks was passed. DELICIOUS MAZAWATTIIII TEA DELICIOUS MAZAWATTEN TEA DELICIOUS MAZA VAT NM lnua HOLE AGENTS HENRY MILLING AND CO., LTD. WHOLESALE AND RETAIL GROCERS ETC., es, seasinuaxxv-srazrr, walaitinfrox.</p>
      </div>
