      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR. A. JORDAN-RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>IA Schoolmaster retires.with Honours.</p>
        <p>---- - - - The teachers and scholars of the Parish Church Sunday Schools, met last Sunday and prdsented Mr A. Jordan, the late schoolmaster of the Runcorn National Schools, with a silver inkstand. Mr. J. Littler took the chair, and in the hearing of a large, number of teachers and scholars alluded to thel long and honourable connection Mr. Jor- dan had had with the Sunday school, and' to the loss they would sustain by his leaving the town. The presentation was made by Miss Smith, superintendent of the school, and suitably acknow- ledged by Mr Jordan, Messrs WE Dudley, Thomas Clare, T Derbyshire' and S Bowyer also added theiri voices in extolling the merits of their friend. The: inkstand bears the following inscription ;—" Run-. corn Parish Church Sunday Schools. Presented to Albert Jordan, Esq., in grateful recollection of 313 years' kindly help and valuable counsel as superin- tendent. With united love of scholars and teachers Tammy, 1901." A PUBLIC TESTIMONIAL. , The Rev Canon A Maitland Wood, M.A., presided. lovbr a crowded public meeting at the National ichools, on Tuesday night, gathered for the purpose ,f publicly presenting Mr A Jordan, with an album and watch, as an acknowledgment of his value and worth during his long term of service as headmaster of the Runcorn National Schools. Amongst those present were :—The Revs 13 B Firth, E S Oliver, Messrs E Imison, J.P., C.C., J Littler, A Jordan,J Johnson, WE Dudley, H Branch, J Davies, iVorrall (Oswestry), Lindsay (Runcorn), A Light- burn, J Wilding, &amp;c. Mr E Imison first bore testimony, on behalf of the managers of the school, to the value of Mr Jor- dan's services, and to his own personal estimation of him as a friend. Mr Worrell, (headmaster of the Oswestry Jchools), spoke on behalf of the teachers past and present, and Mr John Littler followed on behalf of the scholars past and present. The Chairman said he took it, that meeting was representative of the public feeling, and they were there in recognition of the fact that Mr Jordan was a man who had tried and succeeded in doing his duty. ((leer, hear.) His work would live after him, and have a lasting effect in the influence which would be diffused abroad by those w"oom he hadi taught in the schools. That would be his reward, but he (the speaker) also prayed God that he would get a better and higher reward in the -"ingdom beyond. (Applause). The gifts were than handed over amidst loud id prolonged applause. The inscription on the album was as follows : Dear Mr Jordan we ask you to accspt this album with the hope that yeu will remember by its means your old friends and pupils, who are your debtors, some of them for many years of loyal and helpful work, some for years of patient teaching,kind care good advice and pleasant influence; but all of them for the straightforward, noble example of a life sincerely given to faith and duty. It is their hope and prayer that for many years to come you may enjoy the rest you have earned in your own home and town; all good wishes go with you in the con- fident hope that Runcorn National School will retain a place in your affections as you will in theirs. Faithfully and truly yours, all the Man- agers of the School." Mr. Jordan having taken his place at his desk for the last time, the assembly sang" for he's a jolly good fellow," after which Mr Jordan offered his sincere thanks to Mr Imison, the Rev Canon Wood, to Mr VVorrall, and to Mr Littler for the many kind words they had spoken of him. He was quite sure they exceeded anything be deserved (Cries of " No, no.") Having paid his tribute to the managers of the school, to the capable teaching staffs with which he was blessed from time to time, and to the assistance he had received from the clergy in the matter of religious instruction. Mr Jordan wished continued success to the school; prosperity to the town and trade of Runcorn; and asked to be excused saying any more, than to assure them that he should only for a time wish them " Good-bye." (Loud and continued applause.) A miscellaneous programme followed and Mr LindQay, Mr Jordan's successor having been Intro- duced to the assembly, the meeting concluded with " God save the Rine</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>RUNCORN CO-UNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-UNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>A DOCTOR AND HIS FEES. WARRINGTON MAN SUED.— At this court on Thursday, before His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, judgment was given in the case of Dr 7. B. Delaney v. Lyons, for the recovery of £4 1.01 for Jfessional attendance, and ordered the defendant to _ pay 3s a month. The case arose through Dr. Delaney attending Lyons' son, whilst he lodged at Ellesmere - street, Runcorn. The defendant was a clogger in War- rington, and had ayreed to pay Dr. Delaney, and stated so in the presence of the landlady where his son lodged in Rundorn.—His Honour pointed out to the defendant that there was a defence, if he was man enough to set it up, under the " Statute of Frauds " which provided that "when a man guaranteed the debt, for which an- another man was liable he must bind himself in writ- ing." To avail himself of that, notice most be given, therefore he gave the defendant the option of adjourn- ment—(he paying the cot)—so as to give the notice, or the acceptance of an adverse judgmeot.—Defendant said he could not afford the adjournment..—His Honour then entered judgment as recorded above.</p>
        <p>41 prof</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUNICIPAL FINANCE. -- A Suggestion. In 1898, a series of articles were printed in the Examiner "on the finances of the borough. The thief points in the criticism had relation to assess- ments and the Corporation's indebtedness. With 1 regard to the former it was pointed out that War- ington was under-assessed, and in reference to the debt, it was suggested that the Corporation should consolidate its debts, get the time extended for the Loans to run, and obtain the money at a lower rate of interest. The author of the criticism now returns to the subject, and wishes to congratulate the authorities 'upon their successful efforts to increase the assess- ments of the borough. He points out that the de- termined efforts which have been made to increase ,the assessments have resulted in an addition of "qO,OOO since the criticism was published, that is to -jr. that the assessments of the Borough which 'then stood at .£ISQOOO now stand at £230,000. Our correspondent proceeds to complain that no taps have yet been taken towards the consolidation of the Corporation's debts. He points out that they ,have an overdraft at the bank at the present ,time of £92,000, which means a considerable item ,f!sxpenditure to the ratepayers, and one which by little skilful manoeuvring on the part of the Finance Committee could be appreciably lessened. "You ines and will Wigan, ' "be says "that such towns as vird , , St Helens and Bolton, Manches-' ter and Liverpool have created stock. but War ton still lags behind. Widnes, by the bye, II half the size of Warrington with 30,600 inhabi and yet in this matter they lead Warril way," Proceeding to expound his views as to how it could be brought about in Warrington, he continues '‘ Nothing could be easier than to get out a list of the amounts Warrington owes on different Icounts, the number of years within whic the mount has to be repaid and the rate of in rho Local Government Board should then be to give permission to repay the amounts, an 'asked to agree to the creation of Warrington cent stock. Having obtained permission.the p from whom the,money has been borrowed cot given the option to accept Warrington sti three per cent in exchange. Then if nio wanted the stock may be quoted at 97 or 98 case may be, instead of resorting to the expect issuing such an advertisement as" The Corp' of Warrington is prepared to receive loans elf six months' notice or for a term of years." "The reason why the finances of Warrin,_ in such a wretched state is because the auttioritiesi don't want to trouble about them. When the criti. cisme appeared five years ago, the Chairman of the Finance Committee (Alderman Green) replying to my accusation that the assessments were' far too low, said ' the town was fairly assessed . In view of what has since taken place, I think I lam fairly justified in saying that my contention was the right one. Alderman Shaw Green also re- plied to my suggestion with regard to the consolid- ation of the debt, and said' Speaking for the Local Government Board, it would be opposed by that body.' That has yet to be proved, and I am far from lelieving that what has been granted and proved of alue to neighbouring towns will be withheld from 'arrington. DELICIOUS MAZAWATT&amp;B TEA DBLIOIOUS BIAZAWATTES TEA • DELICIOUS MAZAWATTER TEA Sax AGENTS: HENRY MILLING Awn Co., LTD. ANALESALZ AND EMAIL 0500598 ETC. ....PATHST.T. WASHINGTON.</p>
