      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>FICAMINP,R, FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 1</head>
        <p>THE FICAMINP,R, FRIDAY, FEBRUARY 1</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON REOHABITES.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The Sixty-fourth Annual Meeting of the Warrington District, No. 4 was held in Brick-street Mission schools, ;Warrington on Saturday. The District Council was 'called to order at 3-30 p.m. by the D C R Bro. Saxon- dale of Brynn. The whale of the district officers and representatives from the sixteen tents and also a large number of Vi3itOrg were present. Tne II C R Bro. Bennett was then called upon to tare charge over the deliberations of the Counail. The report of the executive for the half year was very gratifying and encouraging, one new tent having been imstituted at Sankey Bridges, Warrington, which promises to be one of the most successful branches of the order, here was a goad increase of members from the tents, Id a total increase of 161 members during the year, ad funds amounting to £3,010. The purchase of pro- perty by the district at Wigan had proved to be a sound and splendid investment and gives the Council every, Incouragement in proceeding with the erection of several houses at Earlestown for which they _hay( nacqu"</p>
        <p>llrepresei</p>
        <p>'There</p>
        <p>Mani</p>
        <p>Mand</p>
        <p>nsevei</p>
        <p>..quired land. Bro W Wright D.S.J.T. presented his annual report which showed that very little progress had been made during the year with the juvenile work inj the tents. This was no doubt due to the great difficulty experienced in securing medical aid for the Juvenik teats. Four of the tents had decided to open new Juveniles branches:in a very short time. Number of Juvenile 408 ; total funds £136 ; 28 Juveniles having been transferred te Adult tents during the year. The election of officers was then taken and resulted as follows :—Bro W Baxenda,e D C R, Brother B Oldroyd DD R, Levite, Bro J Huddleston ; Guardian, Bro B Walker; (Bro D Byrom, PD C R), Bro W Wright DS J ; Trustees, Tnomas Hall, J Shaw, and E Walkden. It was decided to hold the next district meet- ing at Earlestown. At the close of the busines the El C R, Bro A Bennett, on behalf of the district, presented to the DC R, Bro Baxendale, a gold fount sin pen as a small recognition of services rendered to the district during his term of office and for years past. Bro Baxendale reeporided ia a few suitable and appropriate remarks. At 5 30 a public tea meeting was held, when about' 300 members and friends sat do wn to a splendid tea, the tables being decorated with plants and flowers. ik the hi</p>
