      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>LECTURE ON ANIMAL FOODS AT</head>
        <p>LECTURE ON ANIMAL FOODS AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Professor William Stirling, of Owens College. 'Manchester, continued his course of lectures on " ood and Nutrition," in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Tuesday evening. The subject under discussion was " Animal Foods." There was again a large attendance. At the outset the lecturer observed that John Bull's empire was said to be an empire on which the Sun never set. That being so it necessarily in- cluded a vast range of humanity. They would find that these different representatives fed on very different kinds of of toodstuffs under different climatic conditions. Therefore it was very feasible to suppose that the stuffs mil which they fed had some relation to the climatic and! ,eograPhical conditions under which they lived. " Jund in the Empire two extraordinary extremes. _ our own island they had the greatest living consumersof animal flesh of the present time. In another portion of his Majesty's dominions they would fild that (several races professing certain forms of religion eschewed all forms of animal flesh and lived on vegetable foods That night he proposed to direct their attention lmore especially to animal foods. Animalt foods, tee lecturer pointed out, contained much proteid, salts, id some of them fat. They were deficient in starch id , potatoes and bread which were often eaten with meat, made up the starch foods absent in the flesh Ifiumanity had found out that flesh must be eaten with some material which contained plenty of starchy or ,ugary stuffs as were fomd its potatoes and bread. Soup making, he said, was much neglected. In explain- ing why soup was the first dish at a dinner he said its thject was to stimulate the appetite and prepare the ligestive organs for what was to follow. In such a case it should be a clear soup, but where soup was intended :to be the chief dish, it should be thickened with other iubstances. Referring to meat extracts, the lecturer 'tplained how they were made and detailed the conn- ition of them. They contained no proteids, teral extractives, and were not a food. They got name of food by courtesy. It was absurd to say that these extracts contained a number of pounds of flesh for that was impossible when there were no pro- teids in the extracts. They were simply restoratives and to say that they were a food was a misnomer. A person would have to take 14 ozs. daily of these extracts is he wanted to get the amount of nitrogenouf material required to keep the body in order day by day; The extracts were made most grateut to the taste and the aroma was Most comforting, they had a relatively small amount of nourishment. was because they had that small amount of nourishmthat t r referred fer useful to th eful une m leir convalescentpoean ce of meateen bd ti n°inho ts*.g loroughly cooked, and condemned the use of animal 'blood as a food stating, that the blood was often poison- ous. Dealing with the subject of a fish dietary, the lecturer said that fish, which was more readily digested etohteuyr e w lusia flesh, was divided into two classes. Some had ' ;heir substance a considerable quantity of oil, and sot if them contained very little oil. Therefor here were fatty fish, such as salmon, eels" nackerel and herring and lean fists such as whiting,cod,l iaddock, Ike. Whiting was readily digested by invalid,' rnd convalescents because there was no fat surroundit_ fibres. The idea that fish contained phosphorus andl a therefore a good brain food was absolutely absurd. regarded !hell fish, the lecturer mentioned the fact the'</p>
        <p>mea , Hum some , sur s C 11 te , d 4. to b I subst sap's, tore PosiV sevr</p>
        <p>The tho ceature ) blo au&amp; tech tv than their a of the there k a ma'' hr - an itS</p>
        <p>Mwas</p>
        <p>Ih.elreason</p>
        <p>I ..._ dew 15 •demonstrations Lowe. Cons,' ration, wl-- . spu also folio potat</p>
        <p>aat it would require ten dozen oysters to yield tl amount of nitrogen required in a dav's ration. reason why oysters were so easily digested w beCaµ9o they contained the same substant which occurred in our own liver Eggs wen singularly deficient in water and contained no starchy stuff. Therefore bread was eaten with eggs to balance the absence of starch in the latter. Milk was a great animal food ; and contained representatives of all the groups of food stuffs. It was a food absolutely adapted for the nourishment of infants. But although adapted for the infant it did not follow that it was adapted for the adult. However useful milk might be under certain conditiona there was one factor in relation to the food of the adult. Not only must ha have a certain quantity of proteid stuffs, but these things must have a certain) bulk. The amount of direct nutriment in milk WM very considerable indeed. Referring to t, condensed milks, the Professor said that a great percentage of them' were milk robbed of their fats. The butter had been taken out and sold, and the separated milk, which con- tained practically no fat' was mired with sugar, and they had condensed milk. He also dwelt upon the fact that woman's milk contained less casein, but more sugar land fat than Cow's milk; hence in feeding children on cow's milk, it should be diluted, and sugar added to it the diarrhcea epidemic in Manchester last year, 150 aths occurred among hand-fed children, whilet only deaths of breast-fed children took place. The lecturer illustrated his remarks by a number of monstrations and slides shown on the screen by Mr. :we. Considerable interest was evinced in a bivouac lion, which by a simple arrangement of methylated irits heated the rations in the tin. Reference was iso made to the British soldier's ration, which was a: follows :—meat (with bone) 12 ounces, bread 2* ounces, otatoes 16 ounces. other vegetables 8 ounces, milk 3.23 moos, coffee 033 ounces, or tea 6.16 ounces. sugar .33, salt 0.25 ounces. Upon that ration the British Adler was able to do his daily duty. This was known e a mixed diet and was the most economical and had lmany advantages over a purely vegetable or an exclu- ively meat diet.</p>
