        <head>Meeting in Brick-street Mission.</head>
        <p>Meeting in Brick-street Mission.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington branches of th Independent Order of Rechabites a tea meeting was held in Brick-street Mission on Saturday evening. About 200 persons sat down to a capital repast, and after the cloth had been removed a public meeting and entertain- ment took place. Mr R A Naylor, who was accom- panied by Miss Naylor, presided. The Chairman delivered a brief address, durinl, the course of which, he said it had been demonstrated over and over again by statistics and3he experience of medics men that paople who abstained from intoxicants lived Longer than those who took strong drink even in moder- ation. Re believed there were societies in existence who would take a man who was a teetotaler for, ink notja.</p>
        <p>less premium than one who took drink) in the most moderate manner. They did it require drink to keep healthy and well, and if anyca. wanted to add a few years to their existence they must not touch driak. Teetotalers were very much healthier than those who drank. Drink was a very good friend to doctors. He hoped the intelligence of the people of the country would begin to assert itself and that they would not be afraid to join the standard held aloft by the temperance party. (Applause). The speaker of the evening was the Bro. A. Bennett, Edinburgh.He said that the Order,under whose anner they had aesembled, had its origin about two years before Queen Victoria ascended the throne. That was the time of little faith, but moo laid hold of the temperance principles and in spite of prejudices and opposition pursued their way. Like all the faithful they bead their reward. They had passed away, but the eaople of the present generation had entered into their 'labours and reaped the fruits of the seed 'that was sown in those days of darkness and crime. While they rejoiced at be present extent of the movement throughout the length and breadth of this and other civilized lands, they should look back with respect and honour to the memory of the men who laid the foundatiens of the movement at a time when it required all the courage and determination that men possessed, coupled with a strong and abiding faith in the righteous- ness of God. The Order commenced with ten members in the then little borough of Salford; and now it really encircled the world with its membership. In the War- rington district the cause did not make mile, head way for some years, but they were now going along !Oen- didly, and had over a thousand members. Speaking of the conditions of membership,Mr Bennett mentioned the fact that no meetings of any branch of the Order were held on premises licensed for the sale of intoxicating drink, and as soon as other societies did the same thing it would be better for everyone Connected with hem. They had not only adhered to this fundamental principle, but they had been advocating that other eocieties should do the same. Last year when the lational Conference of Friendly Societies was held, a Iresolution was adopted 'xi the effect that in future no meetings of the Conference should be held on premises. 'censecillfor the sale of intoxicating.drink. With regard' , the financial position ef the Order the speaker said .hat when they were sitting in Belfast two years ago lthey were required to ask a certain amount fro.n the members to meet all the claims that would be made mon the Central relief fund, and they found that all ;bey should need to ask was a halfpenny per member per year. That amount had so far proved more than sufficient, the relief disbursed last year, amounting to less than a farthing per member. The reason for this was that they were an abstaining society and therefore they enjoyed better health and longer life. Taking the ,society all round they were really working on a solvent contribution. The temperance question was still with them. The drink problem was the big nroblem at the opening of thlnineteentla century, The first real step to wards the satisfactory solution of the drink difficulty for them to make this the first question on the ;mai platform and demand for every locality the _sower to vote for or against drink shops in the in's- (mediate neighbourhood. The law of a professing itian land should Rive to all classes of the corns iity equal Dower to enforce their rights. So far as J drink traffic was concerned the law did not do that., Award the Seventh had been proclaimed Sing. Before he was Edward VII he was Albert Edward, Prince of 'Wales, and he (the speaker) bed been very near to his estate at Sandringham and had been told that In virtue his:position as owner of the estate, the Prince kept it dear of drink'shops. He (Bro Bennett), was of opinion hat the subjects of Bing Edward had as much right to keep the drink shops away from their doors as the Bing himself had. (Applause.) When that day came the doom of the drink traffic would be sealed. In cot ,icere</p>
