      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>At Wycliffe Church.</head>
        <p>At Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>A large number of Rechabites attended the meeting M. Wycliffe P.S. A.. Society on Sunday afternoon. They I wore their bright regalia and were accommodated with in the centre of the church. The Rev J Yonge.the it of the society, occupied the chair, and on of the members offered a very cordial greeting me to the brethren of the order of Rechabitea. /aye a pleaeant thing for them, he said, to friendly and cordial lea'ations with other bodies imselvea who were engaged in different ways dering the great cause they all had at heart. h all present were not teetotalers, he was sure ire all convinced that the greatest evil this had to face was thefevil of intemperance and the dy for intemperance was total abstinence. glad that the Rechabite brethren were fight- ;ood a battle in this great cause. They were met ;moon under a great shadow which it was im- to forget, and it would be unseemly if they did ttion it in a passing word. The emblems of on that desk and pulpit reminded them of the grievous 1089 which had fallen upon this in the departure of their beloved sovereign and He was of opinion that there would not be a mart in the country that had not felt the in-, ie of this bereavement. They respected her as a character of the finest type and as being by the spirit of Jesus Christ, and , got to know more and more of her iperaonal history and saw better into her per- ional character the more they were convinced of her iiety and devotedness to the King of kings. It was im- ible for the country to part with a Queen who bad ..,een in possession of the throne for such an unusually llong period without feelings of the deepest regret and -”erest sorrow. She would be one of the cherished ...odes of our lives, and he hoped and trusted the influence of her character would tell favourably upon *- own.</p>
        <p>It somethi horr- it</p>
