        <p>~ate rested upon the sanctity and purity of the home info of the people. When the historian took pen in hand and began to write the history of the Victorian,</p>
        <p>era ons of the things that would stand /out prominently would be the rigid regard ttie Queen took to preserve the sanctity and pur-i icy of her home life as well as her Court lie. The Rechabites, he contended were doing something to preserve the purity and sanctity of the home life of the people of this c4untry. The one htin- dred and sixty millions sterling which was annually spent in drink was worse than wasted, for it resulted in the moral and physical deterioration of the people In referring to the work whieci was accomplished by the Rechabite order, he said the order was baying in re- lief for sickness and death claims £200,000 annually. iThey took their stand on the Bible narrative and he claimed that they were working on distinctly christian lines. The organist (Mr. W. H. Payton) afterwards played the "Dead March" in "Slut" the audience standing the while. Tirr "National Anthem" was atterwards sung and an imp ressive meeting concluded with the pro- nouncing of the Benediction.</p>
