      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>1901.</head>
        <p>1901.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>eourti</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."] I Faotball on Saturday existed only in name. It was; really an impossibility for the game to be played accord-' ling to rules owing to the weather, which was of thel wettest and windiest description. Fixtures were fulfilled nevertheless, for toe Northern Union takes no count of I weather unless it be frost, when tee surface of Mother Earth forbids play. No matter what extremes the same 'arca may go ta in the other direction matches are lyed just as usual. Footballers seldom had a worse ..ay than Saturday, and the various grounds were pro- lbably never in a worse state. xi ud and water were werg where and after the first five minutes the players seemed to enjoy it except when an unnecessarily large iquantity of either lodged in an optic. As a natural con- sequence of the condition of things scoring was every- where low, and gates were down to minicnum,while toe 'emits of matches were by no means according to :pee tatioas.</p>
        <p>liStlrl</p>
        <p>Apia:</p>
        <p>lak</p>
        <p>seemed</p>
        <p>aseqi</p>
        <p>Oresults</p>
        <p>tnep</p>
        <p>Warrington naturally expected to beat Barrow, but instead they went very near to being beaten themselves. lAs Barrow never have won at Warrington this would cave, been a dire calamity. Fortunately it was averted, out only by the sacrifice of a point, the scores being wen at the close. The visitors have now got quite a reditable record behind them, and the way they went iboift their work showed that they were determined that it should tilt be spoiled in any way by the visit to War- 'ringtoa. They played a rare hard game, and if there was any inherent cleverness in the home club it never came to light. One fancied that the smarter tactics of cue Warrington backs would have given them the ver- dict, but the weather such as we had brought all men to one level, and there was nothing in it from start to finish. A draw was the fairest result, though the loss ofl three points to Barrow this season is not a very eatis-1 tactory thing for Warrington to reflect upon.</p>
        <p>dlAs</p>
        <p>tvrecli</p>
        <p>,oicarae</p>
        <p>.lui</p>
        <p>Both teams were at full strength, Warrington's two new forwards,Fell and Edmondson of Askam turning out for the first time in primrose and blue jerseys. Though both are big fellows, the Barrow team was the heavier.' all round and that extra avoirdupois ought to have made itself felt more than it did. Warrington's thruteti mg brigade distinguished themselves however, and I question whether the forwards have scrimmaged better 'luring the whole of the seasoa than they did on Satur- fay. Quite three times out of five they got possession, hough that was worth little. Passing was of little avail, sod if the backs kicked they only enabled the splendid fielders on the Barrow side to return with interest. It was somewhat of a puzzle what to do with the ball, the conditions were so dead against the game as it ought to be played. Shorter punting and close dribbling would have paid the best, but neither team attempted to make headway by this means. Footwork is not as good as it used to ho, and on _days like Saturday the fact is one to deplore.</p>
        <p>I was some what sorry to see Warrington win the ton, for they never make the most of the opportunity affor- ded by the elements, if they are favoured in th 3 initial m4iety. They would, in my opinion, have had a far -better chance of victory had they turned round at half time with a deficit of two or threo ,points and the wind in their favour. Still I would not counsel a captain to . . .</p>
        <p>_.,se the worst position delibsrately at the outeet. Hel wnuld be in a very unenviable position at the end if his, side had lost. The fact remains, however, that I gener- ally prefer to see Warrington lose the toss. They invari- ably play better after half time than before, and pir- ticularly it they kaow.there are points to be wiped off.</p>
        <p>1 The first half of the game on Saturday was full of en I deavours to cross the line by Warrington. They per- sistently attacked, but, as at Millom, they could do anything but score. It was almost impossible to get the ball out to the wings, quick enough to beat the opposition, so that when Fish kicked a ponalty goal, the point was welcomed. Once, near half time, Warring- ton's chances looked very rosy for Isherwood gave Fish • the ball with an open field before him. The wing man did</p>
        <p>_ not seem to travel so rapidly as usual, and after' running half the length of the field h 3 was pulled down from behind by Dickie, one of the Barrow centres. Another three strides, would have carried him over but it was not to be. In the second half Barrow soon worked their way to the home quarter, and Platt made the scores level by a dropped goal from the 'lase of the scrimmages. The noint was well-worked for Id smartly got. After this Warrington tried hard to into the other half but the Barrow defence was orb, and they easily kept the upper hand. Neither _ showed much aptitude in the scoring line, and it was evident the Furness men were well content with the, Ipoint they had gained.</p>
        <p>There was a lot of kicking in the game and as usual Warrington came off second best. The Barrow backs, particularly Proctor and Dickie, fielded and punted magnificently, in direct contrkst to the clumsy efforts of the home men. A wet ball is an awkward object, but the Warrington men should surely have managed better than they did. Even Hallam was unreliable, and played nothing like so sound a gams as the Barrow custodian, who added to the excellent reputation he bears. To by pita candid and impartial, one must admit that on the lay's play Barrow were slightly the better team. The 'splayed fewer faults and made less blunders than the lotne tot, and their performance was altogether a! d4oreditsble one.</p>
        <p>I;!fin—o-thing.</p>
        <p>14hotne</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>1 LATEST NEWS. ,Heaton Norris' Railway Out-</head>
        <p>1 LATEST NEWS. ,Heaton Norris' Railway Out-</p>
        <p>rages.</p>
        <p>Heavy Sentence.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon Ernst A Cartwright,aged 23, a clerk, was placed in the dock in the Crown Court, at the Mancne3ter Assiz +s, to receive sent3oce for two attempted ind c3nt a I3aults and one common assault on female, in the train at Heaton Norris, to which he on Wednesday pleaded guilty. The Judge's semence was that he should go to prison for twelve months hard labour in (me ease, and in resp3ot of each of the other two CAS '3 be would be sentenced to three years' penal servitude, the sentences to run consacutively and not con- currently.</p>
      </div>
