      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>FREE VETERINARY BOOK.</head>
        <p>A FREE VETERINARY BOOK.</p>
        <p>per; TP</p>
        <p>1 rece)</p>
        <p>We have just been informed that the Proprietors ofj Condy's Fluid, of 14, Goswell-road, London, E.C., are posting to all applicauts, free of charge, a most useful Book containing instructions from Veterinary SurgeocAs of the highest standing for the treatment and cure of Ailments and complaints affecting Horses, Dogs, Cats, Cattle, Sheep, rigs, Rabbits, Poultry, Pigeons, Cage Birds, Gold Fish, Szic. Condy's Fluid is a really Magical Remedy for suffering animals and has had an enormous sale as a Veterinary remedy for nearly half a Century. We recommend all our readers to write for this Veterinary Book to "Coady." 14. Gos Well-road, E.C. No charge for Book.</p>
        <p>liposi</p>
        <p>Cattle</p>
        <p>!Ipla; [Was</p>
        <p>:bels</p>
        <p>r sal.</p>
        <p>fartni</p>
        <p>newn</p>
        <p>,Achi</p>
        <p>lsch</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY'</p>
        <p>The most noteworthy feature at the Warrington Musical Society's concert on Wednesday next is the -rformance of the second number of Coleridge- eaylor's Trilogy "Scenes from the Song of Hiawatha." To those familiar with Longfellow'a pathetic story, told in the majestic rhythm of his blank verse,it will be sufficient to indicate that this section deals with the tragic incidents of Minnehaha's death. In his analytical notes on this ant work of the composer, and referring to the zuliarities of the ifliawatha metre, Mr Joseph ,ennett says:—" Mr Coleridge-Taylor is fertile in •hythmic device, but he could not, of course, lutralize the impression of Longfellow's unbroken echaics, nor obviate the inevitable consequence. iere is a strong probability that ' The Death of eitinehaha ' will,be regarded as capable of making a greater impression than' Hiawatha's Wedding Feast ' (the first section of the trilogy), owing to ,che element of tragedy which gives it strength, but in any case the second of the 'Scenes from the Song of Hiawatha' is worthy to associate with the first /and equal to carrying on its interest, if not of -ening:the same." The Death of Minnehahe " was produced for the it time so recently its October, 1899, at the North iffordshire Musical Festival and, in view of its eformance here on Wednesday next, it may be tteresting to our readers to quote the comments the The Daily Telegraph's musical critic on the irk itself. '"ltinnehaha' is,lin its way' he says, "as complete and adequate a musical expression as the Wedding- Feast,' and from the very nature of the case one much more moving and impressive There is in it the same frequent repetition of a few hort themes presented under varying conditions, Loh as relieve the strain of their persistency; there the same aptness of expression in the phrases,the ,ame partiality for diatonic melody, the same lwealth of resource in the orchestration, and an equal measure of the persuasiveness which brings is to think that thus and thus, and in no other way, are the poet's thoughts hest clothed upon with Imusical beauty. The fact just stated embodies the tighest triumph of the composer's means and methods, and is the more remarkable because,apart from harmonies and colours that show the freedom if modern treatment, the melodies in which the el and true spirit of music always lies have the ilicity of an older time. About them there is nitig artificial. They never suggest that they have been sought after, but rather that they have come of themselves. Handel is hardly more natural than Mr. Coleridge-Taylor in this regard, or his ebjects, so to speak, more inevitable, though, of mrse, their treatment by the old master, on the I hand, and the young aspirant, on the other, is the broadest possible contrast. Ido not in the ist degree underrate the value of Mr Taylor's swerful and expressive harmonies or the pictures- Lueness of his orchestration, but I contend, all the same, that the secret of his music's moving force lies in the happy phrases upon which be insists with such remarkable iteration. When sorrowful, there are tears in each one, and with a few notes he can touch the springs of emotion in a degree refused to many who laboar strenuously and with elaboration towards the same end. Than this I can say nothing more conclusive of the fact that the, youthful Anglo-African is a man with a miselen with a gift of free and natural elevence in a domain which is the favoured home of that priceless ,quality, and with a potentiality such as must draw to himself the hope and expectation of all who wish well to English art." From our advertising columns it will be seen that therelis also announced for this concert the re- appearance, which we doubt not will be welcomed, of the celebrated violoncellist Mr Carl Fuchs, who, we understand, will, in addition to other solo work, Ilay the principal part in a concerto by Saint lens, The choral members of the Musical Society will also contribute some interesting part songs in- !eluding Dr: Parry's Choral Ode " Blest pair of Sirens."</p>
        <p>Warrington Ragged School and Mission. By the generosity of Mr and Mrs Charlton Parr, the scholars of the above Mission and school enjoyed a tea on Sunday, consisting of currant,egg lunch and lemon buns with tea, there being 280 children in the afternoon and 70 widows and fatherless children in the evening. On Wednesday evening the choir met for practice when cakes again were given away.On Thursday and Friday 80 gallons of soup and bread were given away, the meat being provided kindly provided by Mr George Bowcock and Messrs Shepherd and Shutt,of Butter- market-street. All kinds of clothing or meat will be thankfully received by the committee, at the school, Bank-street. Post cards Br,c., to J Skelland, k63A, Bewaey-street.</p>
