        <p>Mr. Hrontax moved and Mr. A. JOLLEY seconded thdi !option of the minutes. The Citaiamax,in answer to Mr. Pilling, said the pass- of the minutes meant the adoption of the order of a Local Government Board. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE moved an amendment that much of the minutes as related to the new dietary ,Id be deferred until the conference at Liverpool bad _ held and the subject discussed. He said it was es- ernely desirable that there should practically be nal- (ormity in the treatment of inmates under the new</p>
        <p>Mr GEORGE PILLING seconded the amendmsnt. The Castsmarr in alluding to the objections which had sn raised by the Liverpool Boards of Guardians said talk about a great addition in expense in cones- luence of the new dietaries was very largely baseless.</p>
        <p>_ Mr PILLING: Oh no. _ _ The Cautameas said this matter had been talked about \t several meetings of Guardians in the country and ome very strong remarks had been made about the ad- [Monet cost. Be had not the slightest hesitation in lying that many of the remarks were mere sentiment And not based on evidence at all. The proposed change iwould not involve any add i tion al expenditure in food, It it might involve ilittle expenditure in alterations the kitchen and the appointment of an additional 'Cr. He was not prepared to say it would, but it it. Toe committee had had plenty of time to ider it, and ...he was convinced that the</p>
        <p>'expenditure would be Blight, and if properly administered, it might be less. t would help to improve the administration and keep it of the Workhouse people who ought not to be there, it who ought to be maintaining themselves outside. Guardians they were trying to make it harder for 0-</p>
        <p>suggestedP</p>
        <p>, improvident and vicious but better for the unfortunats soor and sick. It was extremely rash for anyone to tate, as was alleged in the Liverpool circular, that the \ew order would result in additional waste. Mr. PILLING did not think it was quite fair for hairman to say that the gentlemen in Liverpool, a id to deal with a larger number of inmates than they Warrington, had made rash statements. Such a .ment was very uncalled for. He did not see why _ . . .</p>
        <p>operation of ttio order should not be deferred. 71 ;ht be able to get better terms from the xrernment Board Mr. RICKMAN said the sub-committee which</p>
        <p>, the matter gave great care and consideration to detail of the new dietary. So far as they were lable to judge, the new scheme would cost very little' lore than the present system. Knowing that they bad o establish the new system on the 25th March, the sub- committee got to work at once, and he questioned whether the members of the Liverpool Board of Guard- ians had gone into the matter so carefully as they had. He did not think the Local Government Board would postpone the operation of the order, for he noticed that many Boards of Guardians were adopting it and gettir the scheme into working order. They were apt to ight of the great good that would come out of it. The' - diet given to the deserving poor and also the .dppage of many things being brought into the house, iwould leave a great effect on the administration of the thei</p>
        <p>He thought the new order was a step in direction.</p>
        <p>amendment on being voted upin was lost. Mr PILLING then proposed that the Board support the molution passed by the Liverpool Guardians. The Rev J El Rinnwrrz seconded. On a vote being taken, there was a'tie and , hairman gave his casting vote against the proposition. The minutes of the Muse Committee were then passed. INCREASE OF SALARIES,</p>
