        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>&amp;MGR ATTACX UPON A WIFE AT WARRINGTON— ? HUSBAND COMMITTED TO PRISON• At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before j Mr. F. Monks, and a fulf bench, James Gilfoyle, 19, Or-I ford.street. was brought UD on remand on the charge of committing an aggravated asssult upon his wife, Sarah, Giif 'ogle between 11 and 12 o m. on Friday the 18'hl inst.—ibe Chief Constable said the man was remanded for eight days because the condition of his wife, °win! to her having sustained a fractured skull, was very critical, and it was feared that serious complicatious and developments might take place. Fortunately the worst forbodings had not been fulfilled although the woman had to be detained in the Infirmary for eight days. The prisoner NO as a laborer and resided with his wife at 19, Orfard s rent. they had not lived very comfortably together for some time, and quarrels had ben frequent. At 630 on he ereninz in question the defendant came bottle from work, and subsequently went out. He returned ..t 10 o'clock, consilerably under the influence of drialry aad again went out and did not return until after 11 o clock. In the meantime his wife.and children had cone to bed, leaving the kitchen in darkness and tho door t. on the snack.' Prisoner immediately began to find fault with the fact that the place was not brilliantly lighted. He said he had paid for the light and light he weuld have. He called to his wife and she told him that there were lights in the bedrooms and he Inuit come to bed. This he declined to do and continued to shamble about`the house. Finally, fearing that the door might be left unlocked, Mrs Gilfoale came downstairs. When abe appeared, the prisoner began to grumble that be bad not as much light as he thought was absolutely necessary. After numerous threats, be lifted up a stool and threw it in her face, at short range, i aiding a very sevare wound over tho eye, which' bled consi ierably, and causing a fracture of the skull, The wi,man ko,cked on the wail for help, and Mrs N4Stor, a neighbour, came le: As she entered the house the pr:senar rushed not As4iitance was rendered as far as unskilled bands could do so, and Mrs Gilfoyla locked the door. Sometime after midnight the prisanel rerurned and was seen kicking at his own door by P.C. Nalco,. Ile was shoutirm to his wife to come down and opt" the door. The officer asked what was the matter, and hearing his voice the wom In opened the door, and accused-her husband of having cammitted the assault. She was removed to thr Infirmary and the prisoner was taken into custedy.—The magistrates committed the prisorer ti goal for two months with hard labour, and, granted his wife a sepa:atian order with 84 per weik lirnony.</p>
