        <head>VICTORIA AND GEORGE</head>
        <p>VICTORIA AND GEORGE</p>
        <p>The Victorian age is over. A brief week or se ago ind the lo s was too near to us to be estimated ; its 'f-ct was tee) startling. Time passes, and we begin to gradually turn our, '- to corn parisons. We realise that so great a sove _ never reigned since the days of Alfred the Great. More long, more lived she reigned than all The kings of days gone by Sc,tatre may fade and Empire fall, fler name will never die. Her I ate Majesty the Q seen wee of ,hIIIOIIP of Hen- over ; the only child of the Duke of Kot, fourth son of I George 111. Her ancestry is very interesting. The first of the House of Hanover was George I, 171.4, who was a d‘scendant of the House of Bruns- wick. Albert Azze 11., M arquis of Italy, and Lord of Este born about 996, married Cunegonde, of the House ofl Guelph. and their son was Guelph. Deka of Bavaria. Ilia descend ant, henry the Lion,married Maud,daughterl of Henry H. of England, and was the founder of OHO Brunswick family. In 1665, George William of Brunswick died, leavin Dmrothea his heiress, who, in marrying George' of Hanover (after wards George I.) founded the Weal house from which Her late Gracious Majesty; feria was descend ed, The late Empress and Queen was barn May 2411,1819, Q leen, June 20th, 1837 ; Crowned, June 28th, 1838 tAlarried, February 10th, 1840 : Celebrated her Jubilee.l 1887; and her Diarnon d Jubilee in 1897. She had nine R oval children—five daughters.or whom Is dead, and four sons, of whom two are dead. The daughters are: Princess Royal, Empress Frederick P:iscess Alice, bern 1843, died 1878 ; Prin- ., born 1846 ; Princess Louise, born 1848, ' Argyll) ; and Princess Beatrice, born 1857 Ills Gracia us Majesty the King (Albert Ed- -1841 ; The Duke Of Edinburgh (Saxe-1 1844, died 1900 ; Duke, of Connaught,l Duke of Albany, born 1853, died 1884. ginnin g of the year her Majesty had no' lying blood relatives. XL had reigLed the longest of any Soyet to Victoria. That monarch had lived et, by days only, our Queen was en this pa nod, and pass away an inetT4 greatness and virtue. e's longevity, her endurance under sti of !he Crimean War and the Indian Mu s age, the Transvaal War, was due t cars of health. Majesty r egarded the holy as a sacred ne should try to make the best. Iry 111. writ mad, and King George IV, out of his mind. not regerd health, and prevent disast, curative remedy. In 1896 Mrs Perkins' h( eil. " N3arly everything I ate came h •• N )thing would stay on my stomach ,wroot and milk, and that waa all I -s. My back seemed to be breaking in , pains shifted fro m side to side of me. As so bad that I could not sit up t. G-orge day these unmistakeable - indigestio n must have been similarly , ever a nasty, foul taste in my mouth, alarming fi tttering sensation at my t At last I tried Seigel's Curative Syrup, ' the sickness and improved the digest ;old eat and enjoy solid food. I kept ta cant inure Mrs. Parkins, address CAtage, W. Malvern, Worcestershire, " and since ate have never been troubled with indigestion aga; —May 17,1900. Through out Her Majesty'n reign it is remarkable toi think that for complaints akin to disordered livers, kidneys, foul stomechs, and the galaxy of diseases, nauseous and serious that are in the pathology of; indigestion, the remedy is still with this medicinal root! yempound known as Seigel'a Curative Syrup, Hie Maj (bog may he live !) will reign In an age when his ailing setThc's have the wisdom to use ttural vegetable remedy that was among the undis- e digestants in the reigns of Gera 111., or his George IV.</p>
