      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>H 3 '7 THE DEAD QUEEN.</head>
        <p>H 3 '7 THE DEAD QUEEN.</p>
        <p>PULPIT REFERENCES Many were the indications of mourning at the Services held in local places of worship-ca Bnuda3r.' Most of the sacral edifices had been draped in , 'black, and the sombre attire of the cmgrezations, bore evidence to the universal desire to do honour o tae memory of our late S )vereign. Rifarences to the national bsreavemant were made by the preachers, probably at every church and chapel and ppropriata hymns were sung. WARRINGTON. ---- The Parish Church.</p>
        <p>tee</p>
        <p>Toe seryicqs at the Parish Church were largelyl Lttended. The day had b3ea previeualy set apart as</p>
        <p>Temperance Sunday." and in response to the request; of the Lord Bishop of the Diocese, the offertories were! devoted to the fault' of the C.E. I.S. In the morning' the pulpit was occupied by the Rev T F Nicholas,curate rot Woolton, who referred in suitable terms to her, ;Majesty's death, taking for his text D3ut. xxxiv. 1, 2 md 3, and Luke ix. 30 and 31. Alluding to the late; 4ueen, the preacher said :—The spirit, if not the words ,l of the psalm I have just quoted, will to-day be echoing' around the world, across the dusty plains of the Orient,' %mating the mighty millions over whom she held sway int the colonies of Greater Britain ; among the purple clad; valleys of the far Pacific to-day there is universal grief.i the great monarch is no more. The great white mother; to countless throngs has passed to her well-earned rest,l wept wherever the flag of England is known. and inl regions beyond, The earliest recollections of the mai Ir-1 illy of Us recall the figure of that noble lady, who has so j wondrously graced the throne of her ancestors, coming; to the throne as a young girl. She has been part and tiskrcel of the century, and all its great m :vetnents and its minds fisurished under her beueficent sway.. Truly</p>
        <p>taGnd</p>
        <p>'t is impossible at this dist LOCO of time to accurately gauge; her great power and the immense influence she has ex. coiled ; the perspceetive of time alone will enable future aenerationa to estimate her true and real force. As to hew the example of the sovereign of a country has vital influence we have only to call to mind her great AtiCal- tor, Alfred the Great, to see how one power can mould the destinies of a nation, and every succeedirve sovereignl from his thy onward to the present reign. Toe blessing' af the Almighty seemed to follow her in her family, pub- ic, and political life, and the powerful p isition her amity and connections have gained and exercised among the nations and dependencies of Europe needs no Comment. In the political complications of the day they cut no unimportant figure ; they are the guarantee of peace. The sweet influence shed by her home life and her gentle and thouzletful ways will exert an influence for good as long as the empire lasts. In the years to otne we shall see more and rare that the wise adminis- tration she guided and controlled so often by her influ- ence in the counsels or her ministers had much to do with bringing the scattered members of a loose confedera- tion to the more listing and powerful ties of the empire, and, in these days of quick transit, making Us one great and undivided people. In the midst of the sorrow of the empire to-day there is much to console us. The Queen has passed away full of honours and mourned by half the people of the earth.We do not know which k most, to admire,the queenliness of the woman or the womanli; ness Of tne Queen." In conclusion the rev, gentleman died to the evils of intemperance, and said the con- Akin could beet show their respect for the late Queen lby -doing all they could to promote sobrietv.—The ocher at the evening service was the Rev. Dr. N cit- ., vicar of Fairfield, Liverpool.</p>
