      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>St. Peter's ;Church.</head>
        <p>St. Peter's ;Church.</p>
        <p>IP on ,</p>
        <p>At this church on Sunday, heavy black draping were, iisplayed on the pulpit, reading deem, Communion table and pillars of the church. In the centre of the pulpit lwas a heliotrope shield, while a cross of the same colour adorned the front of the reading deek. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. Morley Stevenson (orinci- al of the Warrington Training College), who took for .ie text, "Bo thou feithful even unto death and I will give tnee a crown of life:" Rev. xxi. 10 The rev. gentle- man said the late Queen duriog her life had been faithfull to her Empire, f dthful to her people, faithful to her conscience, faithful unto death. They were a nation of mourners that morning, but while they sorrowed for her loss they could not but contemplate the past without a, great teeliag of thankfulness to God for tha grand' example she had left behind. In the evening the Rev W ,Bracecamp took for his text Prov. xxxi. 30 " But the woman that feareth the Lord she shall be praised." Mr Bracecamp, speaking of woman's influence, said our beloved Queen, whose death we were lamenting, was a good Queen, a good wife, and a good mother—and he bcsoueht his hearers to company her blessed example.— The" Dead March " was played after the morning ser- vice by Mr Fred Webeter, the deputy organist,and in the evening by Mr Peter Edgar, organist. St. Paul's Church. At the morning service at St. Paul's Church the organist (Mr. Wilkes) played Chopin's Funeral Marcia, and in the evening a march by Beethoven. "God Save the King" was sung, the congregation kneeling. The 'evening preacher was the Vicar (the Rev E H Tarbuck), who adire;sing a large congregation from the words, "There is no fear in Love ; but perfect love casteth out fear ; we love because He first loved us," said: We do not yet know, we cannot yet measure nor comprehend, what we and the world at large owe to the marvellous influence of our late Queen, and even that which we do' know seems immeasurable. We seem all to be dazed by the chock, and only when toe first namhiug effect of lue grief abates shall we be able to appreciate our late evereign'e influence. and even then not fully, for as .he national life develops we shall constantly be recog lnizing the hand that under God guided the nation -eards each development of his character. ted by our late Queen, other sovereigns' kll proportion of their leisure hou-s in sin and wicklel pursuits, but she em- of that leisure in the best and fullestl rehip and ccnternplathn ot God, r home recreations or in study or mrs of leisure were apprnpriat and forethought. Was there a ocident, e. famine, a war, or any or abroaci,but the wilesved,the received a letter worded wit xprese the sympathy of the na life progressed herself lost hu many a friend, and o herself could best sense seff -red like wise ? Verily she t griefs and to carry our se Wycliffe Church, tali church had le leo draped in , te occ scion. In' a brief referet , the Rev John Tanga said I rays been ,a servant of the Lord, •bearer for Him. She had " fear, south." .Not all the porapi and 7hrone and the Curt, decoyi a her chosen path. The first re • girl-lips wee°, on that early .s airoueed froth' sleep 'to 'be to tiered : "My Lord Archbishop, symptomatic and typical of e" 'Through the who!e of her Atly and devoutly religious. , her children in the knowledge ,hem herself for their cnnfirmatin example of pers mat religion bef id all her Court. Me kept the S, orship, ever declioin.l—except lee—any attention that day Co b that persistent habit she was like Mr., lever permitted public affairs to hinder, iva'e or his worship in public. The _ euerern set an example whieb our busy men Iwould do well to follow. She probably found—what 'ways was found—that the soul that was most devoutly habitually worshipful was best capable of arduous sustained earthly service. They could not recall habits, her experiences, her character, with-1 . being supremely impressed by her piety.i ihe Queen had her jws in the early days and yeare, and keenly did she relish them. She was the daughter' of a mother who—left a widow shortly after her daughter's birth—bad only her child to live for, and who lived for her with a love that had no other thought. She was wedded to a husband of noble mien, lofty mind, and pure soul, with whom her f dlowship was as perfect as is conceivable in human experience. There grew up, ,' around her a numerous family who, in the days of youthl at any rate, to her every wish were completely devoted and loyally responsive. She speedily gained, and afeerys kept, the confidence and respect and love of her people. As the years passed she gathered round her the veneration and trust and even admiring affection of .peoples and lands. Nit alone the British Empire, _ the whole world mourned her death that, day., lAlt that that meant must have been to the Queen a 'meal joy in the Qdays of prosperity, and a precious oinforc in years of sorrow and care. But, amid it all. and :she never forgot her feeling to the King of Kings,</p>
