        <p>, . . of Queen Viotorit'a reeolution on ascending the throiae, "I will be good," and .he referred to the interest heri 3dajesty .teepfejaa the popeer. classes of England.. Mrs ! Cannon rendered," A NW chore 'year's shall' roll," the "Australian Bushranger," and" The Bible my mother gave me," with much feeling. TbeSalvationdasses also sane" When the roll is called up yonder, I'll he:there." A general sense of mourning pervaded the service. St Edward's Catholic Church, Runcorn. At this place of worship the Rev Father Chambers officiated. Ties altar front was draped with purple and eold and the altar railing with purple and black, relieved with white ribbons. The pulpit was also covered with purple and black, with the letters "YR." and '• 11.1-P." worked with white bilk. The Rev Father Chambers, in the course of his address, said .:he reign of Queen Victoria had surpassed others not only in length but in benefits to all her subjects, and especially to Catholics.' rho freedom they now enjoyed in the practice of their religion should make them ready to thank God for the lessings of the Victorian era, and deplore the loss of her who had been to all an example of queenly virtues. It was a carnation thing, even in these days, to look upon Catholics as disloyal. Never was a greater injustice done to any section of a community. Their position, was still misunderstood. True as in the 16th century were not prepared to acknowledge the King or ea as their spiritual bead, but, like their forefathers; ..sey claimed ta be as loyal and as true subjects as any in all matters pertaining to the State. From the athletics there would go up before the throne of God 2nt prayers as fervent as auy of her late Majesty's abjects, begging Gad to give her the happiness of His vision in heaven for all eternity, her children whom she left behind tee consolations God alone could give, and especially to direct his Majesty King Edward VII. in the footsteps of his Rival mether se that, together with •dl his faithful subjects, Catholics might enjoy the bless- ' age they had enj,yed in the past.— The " Doroine alvam Lc " was not sung. After the recital of the Divine praises the cmgreeation stood as a sign of res- pect for the late Q leen, wnihet the" Dead March" was played by the organist. • Runcorn Parish Church. At the Parish Church the Rev Canon Woad preached [rein tee words" True Lord magnified Salem ei exceed- ingly in the sight of all the earth,.aod bestowed upon aim suce reiyal majesty as had been On no king befor r dim of Israel." UM preacher said that had the writef .f these words been Writing at the end of ' the reign o Queen Victoria, instead of .at the beginning 'of Solomon's reign, he might still have used the same words, having aid hold of the main thought that it was all of God. The altar., choir stalls, pulpit, and reading desk were draped with black, the aitar being relieved with violets sstetully arranged in vases. Tile special hymns in- .deed, "Day of wrath, 0 day of mourning,' "Gad of the living in Whose eyes," Zee rtpi 0V0L1ia,..4 preacher was the Rev H. B. Firth, woe also referred in appro- priaeu terms to the Queen's long and beneficent reign. rile usual anthem was dispensed with. A collect from the burial servic e was u led after tree colleet for the day nil services avcrs altogether of an:imprese:ve character, and coecluded with the Dead Marcia in "Saul." - St. Michael's Church, Runcorn. . St Michael's Church also possessed deep signs of mourn Mg. The Rev E S Oliver preached on Acts aid. 38 : " For DAVia, alter lee had served his own generation by the will ot Goa, fell on ,sleep and was laid unto -;his fathers." Tee rev gentleman showed that the date 'Queen had with all her heart and mind sorved•her gener- aGlOn, done her work—and that the mist anxious and arduous work that could devolve on anyone. ' St. John's Church, Weston. On Sunday,special sermons were preached by the Rev W H Stables. The altar, choir stalls, reading deskeaud, pillars, were draped with black. 0 l either side above,' tue altar were two shields in blace, with the lettersi "V. R." worked in white. In his sermon in the ,morning, the Rev W El Stables spoke from the teat, "In flee, 0 L era. ht s been my trust from my youth up,' and referred t 3 the many exeelleut qualities of the late Queen, and the loss which the teatime had sustained. Special hymns were also sung. , Holy Trinity Church, Runcorn. ! At Holy Trinity Church, the services were Cendunted by the Vicar and tee Rev. N. Poole, curate. Tne church wore sine of mourning, the ocnninunion table and oulent being °raped with purple and black, while the ming desk and lee .ern had been - similarly treated. e front of the pulpit was relieved whys a wreath of Lite il iwers. Toe sermon in the morning was preached , the Roe. NJ Poole Irene the text " For David, after e bad served his own generation. by ttee will of Gocdfeil n sleep.' (Acts aid. 36 )-...file Vicar, preaching at the evening service, from 11. Chronicles i. 7to 13, poin'ed out the striking similarity which existed in the spirit in which boric King Silomoa and our Queen succeeded to their throne's. 8 Amnon asked Gail to give' him a wise! understanding hear: and wisdom to rule his people, and i there was abundant teitimony to ;how that thie Waft; exactly the spirit in which our youthful Queen under- cook the duties and enxieties of her lirnistecrown.l ! peclal -hy:nus were snug and tee " Dead M arch ''' i Played. , FLodsham Parish Church. I On Sunday, the RtV A Radf ,rd, of Chester.touchingly alluded to the life ot Q leaa Victoria. The church weal draped in black. The hymns "Peace,perfeat peace" andi " Now the Larsoureee task is o'er " were sung by choirl and conereeationeeed the, organist, Mr C a Hibbertt, elayed tee Dad March in "'Saul "at the clole of a very, )Imo service. Frodsharn Trinity Chapel. For the Sunday s.,rviees the chapel had been draped in hlacic,end during the morning service the choir and Sunday school caildren sing "Peace, perfect peace," Oe lorganists,M:sirs W El fi :keriag and 6 Sueip,playieg the Dead Mare sin " Saul.' EARLESTCWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>..4 text : " The - memory Of the just Is blessed." Toe nam3 of the Q leen, he said, weuld go down to pas- ty as that of the noblest woman of the 19th Centuryl ,of the greetes queen of any of the centuries. The, eeachar pro-ceded to speak of how blessed the memoryi if the good Was, and said there was not a woman who: ihould not cherish a desire to be as unselfish, as gracious,l as !ovine, as sympathetic in her personal sphere asl QueeevnamVipciteorainadheinrllifr.atiSouncfhoratm4:mmoarlyl. Q should stand as an Queen Victoria told them they could be just and goevi and live each in, their individual sphere so that men and women vv Will mourn them when they died RUNCORN. ---- st. Paul's Church. At St. Paul's Church, Runcorn, on Sunday last, the althedv.prßea.cheMdoarrthrch°ineguPsVmothnetpr 'olmi pitthienwthorednis morning, dead yet speaketh." Th 3 pulpit was draped with black, and in front Was arranged a beautiful large wreath of white flowers, surmounted by a floral crown. At the conclusion of the service the "Dad march" from oauf was impressively played. At the evening service there was a very large cert. gregetion numbers of worshippers turning out especially; to hear the Rev. Alfred Wells, deliver his promised sermon on "Our National Loss." although a fearful storm raged throughout the district, at the time for church going. The rev. gentle :nee took far his tea:, "Strength and honour are her clothing, and she shall rejoice in time to come." Proverbs 31-21. In the course of bis remarks he said : These words were the highly artistic full-length portrait of a model woman drawn, not with the pencil of a painter, but with the pen of an inspired author. History gave to them no truer illustratioa of that charac- ter than that of their late beloved Queen. They gathered that day under the shadow of a great sorrow ' • their hearts were moved at the national loss. 'Their Queen was g me. The long, lonely, weary, splendid jeurney had been accomplished, and their Queen was at rest. But with their serum, they had a prayer of thanksgiving for the 63 years which had made their Queen's reign quite unique, b oth as regarded length and character. Louie xIV. jedeed reigned 73 years' but what a contrast between his last years and those of Queen Victoria. Hie attended with reversed fortunes and covered with shame : her'i full of honour and dignity. Never had the friends of the King of Kings had better, perhaps never as Rood reasons for honouring their Sovereign with a whole-heartecl honour than they had. " Strength and lSty " had "been her clothing." True the Queen , dead, as the placards in bold type proclaimed it : Ibut was it net equally true that she " being ' dead speaketh," and would it not be pleasing to listen the voice that came from her bier. First awl 'lost he would plane the voice of gracious woman- ... Surely, their Queen, in her adminiltrative fae.ut- ,les, and intelligent use of power, and in the capacity of lunderstanding and dealing with,anct the grip she had on whatever interested hum ani:v, had been to them an infinitely more eloquent justifieation of the " rights of vromen," than had all the blatant speechmaking and petitioning with regard to woman's rightful position. The Queen by the pains she had taken to Mentally equip herself for her duties ; by the high tone of her private life, as a devoted wife and mother ; and by ber rigid institution of a high plane of living for her court, had steadily though quietly educated her people to recognise the dignity and nobility of woman ; and bad done her share in helping woman to take that place which the Great Creator first made possible. Listen to the voice which spoke of duty. Her position was no sinecure. She was no " puppet-head." To her, as Mr Balfour expressed it, " There was no holiday, and no Intermission of toil." Domestic sorrow, and domestic sickness made no difference in her labeurs. Words could give no adequate lescription of the intense patience with which she pur- -1 discharged to the full her share in the Govern- this Empire, Truly, " Strength and honout clothing. The p-etcher then gave several Ilustratims, and spoke of the quiet unobstrtsive don and moral influentee which seemed to be eta of her late IdsjoscY's life; and after teferring illy and vast changes which had taken place - reign, declared that whilst all these chanees . brought about by eta%) machinery, they ad w, and telt, that behind all, and working alway righteous end, there had been the true heart of eon. She had taught the hetion lessen upon courage, of self-eentrol, of charity, of true sympathy, and tenderness. She lase taught I what it meant to weep with those who wepti ~ with these who rejoiced. Then, too, she had, the lesson of religious toleration andl , charity. Whet a splendid rebuke to. eligious bigotry of the day was life. She loved the Church of her choice. but - scouted other sections of the Church. Shei ,tomolitan, imt truly, was she catholic in the of that term. She was a godly woman, and, shown what it means for a rc'er to keep stead.' ;he fire the source of his or her virtue. Her life teat life and it had the fortune of a happy end. reign, and no ouch endiag had ever been knowni 7 before. Full of years and heneur she hall Ito rest clothed with " strength and honour."i new crowned with glory and hoeour.l ould leave her areateess to the nil- judgment of time, and treasure hwr !AS a precious recellectien. The future - u analy- en rpolicy as a ruler. They would cherish ier memory as that of a godly woman, a gentle lady, a' I ying mother,a devoted wile, and a blessed Queen; and, as the poet Whittier put it : " He whose love exeeedeth ours Has taken home Ills eland." end• ao we pray : " Fold her ri Father In T line arms, And let her henceforth be A messenger of ityVd between Our human hearts an 4 Thee. " Still. let her merit rebuking stand Between us and the wring. And her dear memory serve to make Our faith in goecluet“ strenz.- And s o shall we ani she rejoice in time to come. The special mu tic included Pepe'. Ode " Vital Spark," and, " But the Lord is mindful Of His p :ople." ,Bethesda Congregational Church, Runcorn At Bethesda C oegregational Church, the It 'v. J iborne, M.A , took tor his tteot Mat'. v 8 , ti Blessed :e the pure in heart, for they shall Fee God." Ile wished stake those words as a m itto for the noble life of zueen Victoria. In seemed 90 hard that so lone, so iplendid, so beautiful a life should close amid the dread talarnities of war Knowing as they did how secisrive :he Queen was to the suffdrings of her people, the weight of sorrow from the war must have b .ea very h save for ner to bear. It was the sad duty of the nation to enter i'upon it, but its tale of woe was harrowing to the heart of th 3 Queen, and no doubt hesteeed the end. Rather would they have seen her die amid the glori-_.s of peace and goodwill am mg men. But the Providence of God tad willed it otherwise; the long, splendid, yet weare journey had ended at last. In the presence of such a strong grief silence seemed to be bee, tor no one could cope to estimate the gr,wtnesis of their less. Time would surely show that the estimate could not be placed too high. The years of the future would fill the world with 'wonder at so exalted and so rich a life. It had in the Providence of God been given to England to possess one of the noblest, it not the noblest of wi men that the ,world had aver seen, aw, mao every nation admired land loved, and whose death Was a universal sorrow, Nations vr, re not judged ty any particular political act :but by the quality 0, the manhood and womanhood tha ;they produced. Ihe Queen was very generous and tel e iant towards Nonconformists, from whcm she selecte iher most trusted yersoual attendants. She recogniz e ,the good work done by any soet Or denominateen in Christ's name. Taking her all in all, the Queen was the ' 'greatest woman in the world at the time other departure , from it. Ilry thanked God for the glory of her life—</p>
