        <p>The season is coming when people will fear • attacks of influenza. Whether it becomes prevalent or not, it is none the less wise to guard against it. But if it comes, the disease itself is not to lot, feared nearly so much as th 3 weak and exhausted con- dition which follows it. Few diseases that como SO quickly and go so quickly leave the system so utterly exhausted and helpless as this one, and we take pleasure In placing before the reader a letter bearing upon this subject : .</p>
        <p>" °Amberley House, • " Norfolk-street, Strand; . " . •. "April 27ch, 1900. "leaf have much pleasure in letting yoliknow of the great benefit I have derived from Your spleadid remedy, Scott's, Emulsion. When I commenced to use your preparation I had been Suffering from dyspepsia and weakness' following Influenza, and very speedily found great relief from its use. It was impossible for ma to take •The.plald cod-liver glitt to'say I experienced no difficulty' whatever in taking Scott's Emuleion as I found it most palatable. I may say I have thor- oughly tested the merits of Scott's Emulsion and consider it invaluable for nervous disorders, dyspep- sia and all cases of weakness. The progreas I have made , from its use is rerrivkAble, and it give; Inc great pleasure to testify to 80 genuine a remedy.— Yours truly,</p>
        <p>" (Signed) F. MArrsows."</p>
        <p>was toe welfare of 'bee plople, toeir wet- being, tin ir Peace and happiness, crud) an 1 piety.. , She la,ked on those as the one thing to be desired by her as our Sovereign Ruler. When she was speaking to the young he Queen of Hollaud at Windsor s said " It is the duty' 1 ot a Constitutional Sovereign not to govern, but CO set au example." She Set an example of constant endeavour 110 r the benefit tlf her people. Not to amass WeaLtl, not if or that glory and greatness which she did at am n to,-but Iter the welfare of her beloved people. she livei with them, and for them, she devoted herself from girillood Ino extreme old age in the seryics of her people. We all know this, her peopleLbut we cannot know it too well. We know that she was a constant worker thy by day, The burden and tatlxiety was always on her Mind. The Queen had a great accumulation Of knowledge, and was mole to give Her Ministers great at&amp; stance in the carrv• ng on ot the affairs of State. It was because she iived for her peopli, find her' efftirts were increasing for ihegood of bet people, thet her pe late loved-her, :tad thewould be loved !ay sacseeding eeeerations for , con- stant efforts on their behalf. Let us in our bumbler stations in life go and do likewise. Let us go and gain she love of others by serving them, following the Queens' txample, as she followed the hiehest of all examples. St John's Church, Earlestown. ' , • At St John's Church, Earlestown, the service was folly appropriate to the occasion. The Rev T R Matthews cooducted it assisted by the Rev J C Matthews, curate. The vicar in his pulpit remarks said they were gathered to- lay under circumstances new and sad. We have all had at some time, to mourn someone near and dear to .ue, but we have never had to mourn anyone as we have had to- day. Our late Queen was a noble and goad woman— 'still, good and true, she was not too good to b i called home. When she knew first that he was taloa the bead of so great a nation as uurs, her early resolve was to -clo Igood, and, this resolve had been carried out as it de- served to be. In 1862-3 at the time of the cotton famine, her Majesty's kindness in every good work was well known, Kindness itself was written in her emiht:ee- anee. He Would never forget the great interest ' she took in the SundaylsChool, and when she met that great iaasembly of 1,800 Sunday school schola'rs triSalford, and ,he was among the gathering, a Bible was presented Ito her that day,•which, it was said was the secret of England's greatness. She had besn described in a very . 1 few words by a wellsknewn poet ' A perfect woman, noble heart, fit to reign and to command.", Her Majesty was a great friend to the temperance question. In the 114% chapter Of St Paul's epistle to the Romans, thee would find these words "It is good ceith.ir ts eat flesh, 'nor to drink wine, nor anything whereby thy brother eumbletb, or is offended, or is made weak." Such Was 'hte opinion of our great Queen. I St. Peter's Newton. Oa Sunday special set.% ises were held at St. Peter's , Ohneeb, Newton-le-Willows. At the morning service . i altered edifice was crowded, and everyone Present cognised the sorrowful oceagion by wearing mourning . in some form or other. Many were in deep mourning.? IThe pulpit and readisg desk were draped ittpurple and ' black. Dr. E. Button Middleton, F.R.C.0.. DresideO at the organ, and with sublime taste and appealing senti- ment played as opening voluntary," Blest are the de- l parted," from Spohi's "Last Judgment." The hymns Iselected for the occasion were" Great God, what do I see and hear ?". "On the Resurrection morning," ' "Holy Father in Thy mercy," and the Psalms were Coe , ,90.1), 27th, and 46th. 1 The Vicar (the R. v. Jas. Ryder) took for his rest the t 2,d Book Samuel, 3 chapter, and parts of the 38h and r !39:11 verses: "Know ye not that t'iers' is a Prince and a great man fallen this dav in Israel ?And I am this day weak though anointed King." Having referred to the, loss sustained by the people of Israel through the deatti. of their beloved King Aborr, whose passing away had ... plunged them into the deepest mourning and had caused them to pass through most acute sorrow, the preacher • turned the application of the text to the blow which had hefallen our country. When we look back upon . the reign he said we saw how it was marked by its ' kgreatness and its beneficence. N.ivr, why was this so ? , iSurely, it was because she had been a wise, a loving, , land God-fearing woman. Think of the life ! Notice illie blending of her t ighest quelities of head and heart .1 l ,—the rare combination of sweetness and strength—see lhow atninlces in public and in private that life had been, la life open to tee eyes of the world, and he asked where could we find a flaw ? Never in the whole world' could we find a. tru-r ideal .:,f true wo...anhcod ! ' LHe could not think of a more oeattlilui life, ,and though I her loss was irreparable to the nation, we might look . ab•ck upon the past, the commeec-onent of that reign, !..nd trace it to its cOusurnmatiod, and we found now . l'rue the prayers of the people had tKen answered. that God load granted her health sue bolo Life,, and Ha hal strengthened-hat that she rnight.Vauq Ilea 11. i ,ivx :lam</p>
