      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>is Mr husband is plain.spoken ' • he calls a spade a spade." "So does mine ; butl must decline to -repeat what he calls the lawn-mower." "Everybody says you married me only for my money." He: "But I didn't dear. I know you look it, dear, but I didn't." MISS FROCKS: "Mr. Spokes, do you like Songs Without Words '?" Mr. Spokes "Well, I very much •intfer them to songs without sense. • 0-•,r, ' '- CLARA : "When I was out on my wheel thi, morning I cracked my enamel quite badly." Maud: "You must learn not to smile." "An, the charms of the vanished childhood !" sighed the sentimentalist. "I've forgotten all of them," said the prosaic person, "except a charm for warts." " Tnis is the bill from your oculist," said the collector to Mr. Grimley. "Just take it back to him and tell him that I can't read it with those glasses he sold me." LADY : "Now that you have had a good dinner, are you equal to the task of sawing some wood ? " Tramp: " Madam, equal is not the proper word ; I'm superior to it." HE: " Will you be my wife?" She: "The idea I Don't be ridiculous." " Yes, I know it sounds ridiculous ; but, then, I'm not so particular as some men are." "LOOK here, my hod," said a burglar, "I ain't so bad as you think I am. Only give inn time and I'll reform." And his lordship gave him seven years to do it in. INTIMATE FRIEND: "Has your husband's love grown cool ?" Sarcastic wife : " Oh, no I He loves himself just as much now as he did when we were married 20 years ago. NEW MILK.—Mrs. Perkins : "Did yer sea any of them horseless kerridges up in the City ?' Mr. P. : "No—o, but I had same of their cowless milk." _ . .</p>
        <p>PLENTY OF ROOM: Poet : " I have a little con- tribution for—for the waste-paper basket." Editor •••.‘ We have no waste-paper basket." Poet :" I am delighted to hear that.' Editor : "We use a barrel." " Puffins answered an advertisement in which somebody offered to sell him the secret for pre- venting trousers from getting fringes round the bottom." What did they tell him?" "To wear knickerbockers."</p>
        <p>Undertaker: "And what kind of trimmings will you have on the casket?" Widow: "None whatever; a plain casket. It was trimmins that killed him." Undertaker: "What?" Widow : " Yes ; delirium trimrnins." Confidence is not alwaye„* growth of time. There are minds that meet each other with• a</p>
        <p>--- species of affinity that resembles the cohesive pro- perty of matter, and with a promdtitude and faith that only belongs to the purer essence of which they are composed. "Ain't you going to take your fan with you, Maud ?" "No." "Well, I think you ought to." " What's the use? It is not too hot in the theatre just now." "But I should think you would want something to cover yourself with after you trke off your cloak." INJURING THE HORSES.—Corporal (to new cavalry recruit) : " Never approach the horses from behind without speaking. If you do they'll kick you on that thick head of yours, and the end of it will be we'll have nothing but lame horses in the squadron." "Can you use this?" timidly inquired our ama- teur poet, as he laid a bundle of manuscript on Top's desk. "I think I can," said the great man, affably. "I am just about to light a fire in the back office." Tali teacher was trying to make the little girl understand the word queen. "Queen Victoria," she said, in the course of her illustration, "began to reign at the time of her coronation, and she is reigning now." "And can she make it rain, Miss Ruthie, whenever she wants to I' " asked the little girl with great interest. "WHEN I get to be a man," said the boy who has a good memory for phrases, "I'm going to strive to cultivate an unselfish nature." That's right," replied the father; "how are you going to go about it?" "Well, in the first place, if I have I any little boys, I'll let them shoot their own fire- works, instead of telling them they must let me do it for fear they will hurt themselves." TOLD OF PAT. Cardinal Wiseman enjoyed stories and often told them at his table. One of his favourite anecdotes was that of an Irishman, who while taking a barge up the Shannon„was asked what goods he had on board, and answered: "Timber and fruit." "What kind of timber and what sort of fruit ? " '‘ Well, an' if ye must know, the timber is just birch brooms, and the fruit--</p>
        <p>Well, it's petaties. MIGHT HAVE Dosn WoRsE.—A man was taken ill, and his wife, in her search for remedies, came across a box of little black pills, which she thought Were liver pills. These she gave faithfully to her husband, who improved under her care. In about a week, when he had taken all but one or two of the pills, the busbrnd happened to turn the box over. He gave a yell that startled the whole neighbourhood. His wife ran to him, thinking that he was dying. "Look," he cried; "read what it says on the bottom." She did as he bade her. And this is what she read : " Choice crown convolvulus seeds." THEY were very fond of'each other, and had beim engaged; but they quarrelled, and were too proud to make up. He called afterwards at her house—to see the old gentleman on business, of course. She was at the door. Said he—" Ah, Miss Blank, I belive ? "Is your father in ?" No, sir," she replied • "pa is not in at present. Do you wish to see him personally ? " " Yes " was his bluff response, feeling that she was yielding; " on very particular personal business," and he proudly turned to go away. "I beg your pardon," she called after him, as he struck the last step, " but who shall I say called?" He never called again. A LAWYER, after a close cross-examination of a witness, an illiterate Irish woman, in a Boston court the other day, in reference to the position of the doors and windows, &amp;c., in her house, asked the following question—" And now, my good woman, tell the court how the stairs run in your house ; " to which the good woman replied—" How do the sthairs run? Shure, whin I'm oop shtairs they run down, and whin I'm down, they run oop." HAD HEARD OF IT. —Willei M—was sent by his wife to market to sell a pig. On the way Willie met some friends, and proposed a dram. The pig was tied by the leg to a cart-wheel lying near. On emerging from the inn the pig was no- where to be seen and search as he might Willie never learned seen, it had gone. A couple of weeks after this Willie was met by a friend. "Well, Willie," he said, "have you heard any. thing o' the pig yet?" "Heard 1" replied Willie ; "I've heard o' nothing else for the last fort-</p>
        <p>nicht.' ATE THE MESSEN6ERS.—A story is told in an Antwerp newspaper of some carrier pigeons. The editor of a journal published in that city sent a reporter to Brussels for the King's speech, and with him a couple of carrier pigeons. At Brussels he gave the pigeons in. charge of a waiter and called for breakfast. He was kept waiting for some time, but a delicate dish atoned for the delay. After breakfast he paid his bill, and called for his carrier pigeons. Pigeons " exclaimed the waiter. Why, you've just eaten them." SHARP.—Duting a general election the late Mr. A. B. Winterbotharn, M.P. for East Gloucester- shire, was driving near Northleach in his con- stituency, and had to seek the assistance of a juvenile to open a troublesome gate. To a country- man near he said "Shall I get into any bother under the Corrupt Practices Act if I give the boy sixpence 1" "No, sir," replied the sharp-witted Cotswoldian, "not if you give him a shilling." Mr. Winterbotham saw the joke. •</p>
        <p>A CLERGYMAN was one day walking along the banks of a canal, when he came across a promising member of his Bible class engaged in catching rats With two fox-terriers. " I am very much sur- prised, John, to see you catching rats on a Sunday afternoon. What is the price of those dogs you have 7 ' J3hn duly informed his reference that they ware first-rate animals, and worth a sovereign saoh, " Don't you think," inquired the rector, "that it would look very much better if you sold tbose terriers and bought a couple of pigs with *he money ? " " Aye, an' a pretty fool I should catching rats wi' a couple o' pip," exclaimed</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
