      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <p>THE EXAMINER Ie_RIDAY FEBRUARY 1 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>enwommingrorrnr, CIIIIRCHES.</head>
        <p>enwommingrorrnr, CIIIIRCHES.</p>
        <p>=77=117E E ,</p>
        <p>THE PRESS AND THE FREE CHURCH MISSION.</p>
        <p>Perhaps no religious movement, at the same stage, has ever received so much attention from the Press as the simultaneous mission, organised by the National Free Church Council. It would be much easier to name the journals that have not printed articles on the great campaign than those which have given space to it. As we write we learn that the Lord Mayor of London has intimated his intention of being present at the service to be conducted in the Guildhall by Dr. Parker.</p>
        <p>"ALL THINGS NEW.".</p>
        <p>• This is the title of a little book for new converts that has been written by the Rev. G. Campbell Morgan, and published by the National Free Church Council, at the dlemorial Hall. Mr. Morgan came from Bir- mingham to North London a few years ago, and in a very short time made his mark. He is in the spring to take up the Northfield Extensionl7ork of tbe kateyr..,D. L. Moody. For the two weeksi Oi the London campaign Mr. Morgan is editing the Mercury, a weekly paper circulating in the Hackney district, and making it the organ of the mission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>WAR-CRIES FOR TEE COMING CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>WAR-CRIES FOR TEE COMING CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>The February Free Church Chronicle, of which we. have seen an early copy, is a special `mission number. It contains between thirty and forty photographs of missioners, and messages bearing on the mission from the most sminent ministers and evangelists of the Free Churches. ,The supplement is a list of the mis' - si4" mers and their stations for the whole country. A glance at it is sufficient to con- vince the most casual reader of the thorough- ness of the preparations that have been made for this campaign.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>LATE BISHOP OF LONDON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE BISHOP OF LONDON.</p>
        <p>The death of Dr. Mandell Creighton is deeply and sincerely regretted by Free Churchmen. From the beginning of his career he was on excellent terms with Nonconformists. When Vicar of Embleton, Dr. Creighton was very friendly with the Presbyterian minister of the place. When Bishop of Peterborough, he welcomed 'the Con'gregfitional Union to Leicester, and he addressed the recent World's Christian Endeavour Convention. His sympa- thies were wide, and, in consequence, his sincerity was suspected in some quarters— very unjustly, we believe. He had a trying and laborious time in London, but he always esemed brisk, and cheerful ano:Lnlways im- mensely clever. It will be long before we see his like again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>SICK LIST.</head>
        <p>THE SICK LIST.</p>
        <p>We regret to hear that the Rev. W. Cuff, of Shoreditch Tabernacle, is again very unwell, and will be compelled to take a rest. Mr. Cuff, when on duty, works to the limit of his strength. He has not yet learned the art—a difficult one to a popular preacher of husbanding his resources of strength. Mr. Cuff is to be one, of, the inissioners for Bristol. The mission does not start until February 16, and we trust that by that time he will have recovered. Dr. Waller, a popular ex-President of the Wesleyan Conference, fell and injured his arm while crossing the Atlantic. No bones have been broken, fortunately. Dr. Waller is on a visit to the Wesleyan missions in:the Bahama Island. Mr. A. G. Hooper, one of the Treasurers of the Congregational Twentieth Century Fund, has broken down as the result of overwork, and has started on a two months' sea voyage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>BIOGRAPHER OF WHITEFIELD.</head>
        <p>THE BIOGRAPHER OF WHITEFIELD.</p>
        <p>The resignation of the Rev. J. P. Gledstone, of Streatham-hill Congregational 'Church, has been passed over with comparatively little notice. Mr. Glecistone is a man of very con- siderable literary gifts, and if he had devoted himself more heartily to work giving scope to the exercise of these he would probably have made a deep mark. He has written an excel- lent bio,graphrof- George Whiteßeid, one of the best religious biographies (and there are plenty of them) in the language. Mr. Gledstone was closely associated with Mrs. Josephine Butler in her famous crusade, and has been chairman of the Directors of the London Missionary Society. Ho has been thirty-eight years in the ministry, twenty-three of these at Streat- ham. CHURCH FEDERATION IN SCOTLAND.</p>
        <p>The Union of the Free Church of Scotland and the United Presbyterian Church has in- spired many earnest men in the North with a fervent desire for the complete union of Scottish Presbyterianism. The Established Pres- byterian Church, so long as it is established, is an insuperable obstacle to Union. Even within the Establishment, however, there is a strong desire for closer union. Dr. Paton, the brother of the famous missionary, moved a resolution at the Glas- gow Presbytery in favour of the appoint- ment of a committee to confer with the other churches on the question of closer fellowship, organised co-operation, and Federal Union. The resolution was defeated, but it received considerable sympathy. The spirit of it is growing and will soon, we believe, find some practical embodiment for itself. Federal Union of the Free Evangelical Churches of England has been practically secured by the National Free Church Council, and the simultaneous mission is bringing them closer than ever together.</p>
        <p>KNITTING IN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>An all-day's prayer mooting was held in London as a preparation for the simultaneous mission. The Rev. F. B, Meyer, 8.A., recently expressed the hope that. ladies who came to it would bring their knitting and sandwiches, 013 as to spend the whole time, from eleven a.m. to ten p.m., at the meeting. A correspondent informs us that he once saw a woman knitting in the City Temple during Dr. Parker's famous Thursday morning service. But knitting at a prayer meeting!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>MILLION PLEDGES CRUSADE.</head>
        <p>THE MILLION PLEDGES CRUSADE.</p>
        <p>The promoters of this movement, greatly im- pressed probably by the marvellous organising powers of the National Free Church Council, have resolved to ask the National Council to take up the crusade immediately the simul- taneous mission is over. The matter must, ol &amp;ours°, come before the Executive Committee Some time ago the Committee decided to give the organising of the Free Church Girls' Guili the first place in its programme after March.</p>
