      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <p>A but in east the the steer, dered nort tion ma the</p>
        <p>DERELICT THAT DRIFTED TEN</p>
        <p>THOUSAND MILES.</p>
        <p>al instructive article on the work of the Hydro- 'graphic Office, appearing in the February number of the " Windsor Slagaziee," some interesting statistics are ,iven concerning the longevity of derelicts. '1 It is not known precisely what derelict has had the longest life, it the "Fanny E Wolston," a schooner abandoned I 1191, is known to have floated three years and ix days and to have drifted over 10,000 miles. she was (abandonsd off Cape Hatteras, and floated in a north-, 'easterly direction with the Gulf Stream until she passedl the sixtieth meridian: Than she wandered off toward he south-east and circled around in the track or teamers west-bound from Gibraltar. Next she wan- ered south, tr,t‘l she reached the northern limit of the nth-east trade winds. She followed the general dire°. nof the saili.i route from the Equator, approaching United States Coast until she encountered the Gulf am,which carried her northward again three dcgrees of where she was wrecked, and there she was last</p>
        <p>north</p>
        <p>ghted. She was reported by various vessels, and in aui about forty-five times. Anoth,r famous derelict was the schooner " W. L. White," wrecked off the Delaware ICapes during the great blizzard of 1888. She was prob- ably the most to be feared of all the derelicts, as she was constantly in the track of the tralsatlantio liners Her course was remarkable, and her floatink rate was very fast. Instead of foundering,as her crew thought that she would when left to herself, she sailed straight away. The Gulf St ream carried her in a north-easterly direction until she reached the Grand Banks of New- foundland. She then circled around and around for months, within RR area of ten degrees of latitude and longitude, maintaining a posNon not only in the track of the ocean steamers, but also in the region of almost constant fog. Suddenly she started off again to the north-east, and finally gro tz nded on one of the Hebrides In her cruise of ten months she drifted 6,800 miles, and t was reported 45 times. D using part of her drift she accomplished an average rate of thirty-two miles a day. 'She is blamed f)r several °emu catastrophes. But the most curious of all derelicts wag the " Fred B. Taylor." She collided with the steamer "Trave," and was literally cut in two. But she did not sink ; the two parts, bow and stern, each s'arted out upon an independent voy- age for itself. Tao stern went north; the bow went sour • -</p>
        <p>48chool</p>
        <p>„oath. The stern brough t up on Wells Beach ; the t. (drifted down opposite to the Carolinas, and was 'Atha= I ately destroyed.</p>
      </div>
