        <p>the temptations and excitement of the world—when wel see in her the devoted Christian, the airectionate mother, lof her people, showing compassion te the suffering and, igenerosity to the destitu,e,though surrounded by luxury' 'and splendour that too often chill and flattery that too often corrupts the mind, we cannot but pay reverent homage to such a life. Her glory was not borrowed l from the plumes of State ; it was the glory of her uncrowned womanhood. In her public life, she was an example to every Sovereign ; in her private, life, she was an example to every el tiz 12, ... . - . . .. .</p>
        <p>A more sublime example of motherly devotion and rega wisdom than that shown by Victoria we have not had! lin the long history of our race. It is no exaggeration tol say that the standard of civilization will be raised and this world will become wiser and hotter as the result of her noble life and the memory of her long, unsullied and ;odly reign shall stand on the pages of our history as me of its most glorious records. Truly she lived a life of virtue which crowned her with a diadem worth imore than all rubies and worth more than all the gold Ophir. Alluding to the new King, the preacher said; -Let us pray that the King may impart that purity of -als and integrity of sentiment which will prove the antidote against the vice and folly, the specious libertinism which seem to threaten us at this all impor— tant hour in the history of our land. Let our work as subj^cts and citiz ins of this great land be done for God for sing, for country ; let us ever emulate the beauti ful example of our dead Queen, who will, I am sure, be lenshrined in the memory of us all. A Queen indapd, Beyond all titles, and a household name, Hereafter. thro' all times, Victoria the God.</p>
        <p>ddepi</p>
        <p>St. Mary's (R.C.) Church. The pulpit and altar rails and stalls at this church were draped in Ns,* and purple. Toe celebrant of the last mass was the Rector (the Rev Father Wilson). The. mass music was solemn and funereal,and lent additiona, impressiveness to the service. In tha course of his ser' mon Father Wilson said the period of our history occu pied by the Queen's reign had been one of vast import- ance, net only on account of its length but. of its character. Very great had been the :Menges, constitutional, social and religious. It sad been a period of unparalleled activity in every department of life and of unexampled imperial • enterprise and developm let. It had consequently been a time of constitutional peril, social danger and national anxiety. That notwithstanding,we had passed through this time without disaster, that it had been a time of;1 comparative peace, of great prosperity, material better- ment, educational advancement, religious liberty and social progress was due in no small measure to the per- sonality of the monarch we had lost, to her wisdom, unselfishness and virtuous example. It was no small blessiug to the age that a noble woman of blameless , life shtuld be raised to such high experience. The eyes of all the world were upon her. From her throne she had set an example to all the world. As Queen she had set a high example of self-sacrifice, unshrinking devotion to duty ; in sorrow and in joy she forgot herself to be mindful of her people's welfare; as wile and widow and !mother she upheld a noble and lofty standard of all 'domestic and social virtues, of all those virtues which were the indispensable foundation of good Christian citizenship. At the conclusion of the Mass the organist (Mr T Mather)lplayed the Dead March in "Saul." Padgate Church. On Sunday references were made both morning and evening to the great national bereavement in the death of the late Queen. The Vicar (Rev. W Pollock-Hill), preached in the morning from the text " Sorrows are passed and in the end la showed the treasure of immort- ality." (11. Esdras viii. 54). He said a great sorrow had ' been cast upon the nation, grief stricken by the terribles suddenness of its irreparable loss, that very suddenness ; teeing from her late Majesty's consideration for her übjects, in not wishing to needlessly alarm them: . flee preacher then proceeded to point out how the late Qiseen had given to the nation au idea of sovereignty which was quite new to it, how her reign from its very beginning was a contrast t 3 those which had preceded it. How the tone of the Court underwent a wholesale reformation, and had now reached the proud position of being the beet and purest in the world, how all this had had a reflex action on every grade of society—from the highest to the lowest. He alas showed how above all Queen Victoria had shown herself to be a woman in the higest and truest sense of all that WiS womanly. 'Pile preacher in the evening was the Rev. Canon Pearhyn, Rector of Winwick, who had originally arranged to preach on behalf of the C.E.T.S. Penketh Wesleyan Chapel. The morning service was entirely memorial, and was conducted by the Rev John Gunnell, who, in referring to the (loath of tbe Queen, said : "Nothing in living 4 memory has so moved the nation, and one may venture' to predict that nothing like it awaits those who may live throus,h the greater part of the new century. Whatl gave our late Queen such a unique position, and such a' mighty hold on the affections and confidence of her sub- jects ? The reason is not to be found in the length ofi her reign nor in the marvellous prosperity and expan- sion, of her Empire, but in her personal qualities and sabits. And chiefly in her goodness. We may have sad more brilliant monarchs, but never one with larger aid nobler heart qualities—never one so sincerely and (manifestly good. She was marked by all the inborn 'grace and gentleness and goodness of a true woman.' The worthiest of women ever crowned." The interest 'se took in her people was more than queenly, it was motherly. It is small wonder that such a monarch should have been almost idolized by her people. But in our profound and loving veneration for the dead Queen we, must not forget the living King and his gracious Con- sort, " God bless the King " " Long live the King," is a prayer to which all will add a very hearty amen." Several, of the late Queen's favourite hymns were sung, and at the close of the service the organist, Mr Wiefield, played, the Dead March from " Saul," the congregation stand-, ag with bowed beads the w bile.</p>
