        <p>. . 4, motions of the human heart. They were built upon a A r land. The unsullied purity of her character asp and intensegratitnde for the sympathy and good will has influenced every section of society, and has in- she meted out to them after 300 years of bitter:persecution delibly left its impression upon the world., The liberty which they enjoyed in the free exercise of their holy religion to-day, a liberty' which was greater , even than that afforded to Catholics in many Catholic Court misused and nutrias, was due in great measure to her liberality and 1*,.. . dignity,. ' broad-mindedness. to describe the nature of the less ''' that the nation and the Empire bad suffered was almost ;!tat large. The pomp and pageantry of the; improperly understood! -.'in other days was by her invested with true' eget and yet such was her charm of manner and so gracious her demeanour, thati impossible. In dealing with a character like that the love of bet people (to secure and retain whichi possess id by the Queen, one shrank from the ordinary she laboured without ceasing), went out to her in; ianguage of praise as something unbecoming. One illimitable measure. Her exalted position Was no pro- could not praise a well-loved mother when she died. She tection to her from the woes which are common to o was beyoad all praise. And the nation had begun to re-= us all. Truly our Queen's cup of sorrow has beenl gard the Queen with an affection %Ouch was in no forced filled to the brim. She sustained her greatest loss, sense that of filial children for their mother. There wasat the period of life when the blow was mosti scarcely one in the country, capable of any feeling Her loss was not the romance of youth but all, who did not feel a sense of love and admire-,tu .a overthrow of a ripened happiness, at a period don for the Queen, entirely apart from the loyalty Us to her person as head of the State. , ''‘ Rer womanly virtues were so perfect a getting that her Queenly qualities shone all the more gloriouay. It was 11:1 #i arL"severe. hen she could feel with all her strength. The 'death of Albert the Good might have ended the most prosperous reign and broken the most perfect' these sterling qualities continually bubbling up from the ,hanitiess at once, and yet such was her courage depths of her noble heart that had made her reign so and faith in the Divine power, that although glorious. Whilst,then, they deeply:mourned for her death he reeled under the blow, she applied herself' let them pray that God might keep alive and increase ° again with unceasing industry to the responsible the good which she had done; might her people never influence long remain . duties of her high position. Since then one sorrow forget one whom the Poet Laureate called "The Lady of ‘ has chased another, and hardly a year of her life has the World," and might her good in been free from the gravest anxiety and from the with them. ' ! 1 sharpest bereavement, but through it all she has; ,been in tauch with her people in ail circumstances and in all conditions. We have read her innocent natural messages and understood them as the simple sincere language of a sweet and good woman, and so they were, but they were a great deal more. They were wise and healing. They gradually drew the heart of the biation. They had their soothing influence even over foreign counties. She never made a mistake; she was never found wanting. She had power always to say the right word, and to say it so much out of her soul, that it was felt that the woman was even more than the Queen. As one of our eloquent writers and statesmen describing her first Council wrote :—" Fain and serene, she has the blood and beauty of that Saxon. Will it be her proud destiny at len zth to bear relief to suffering millions, and with that soft hand which might inspire trouhadours,and guerdon Knights, break the last links of the Saxon thral- dom ? " To-day in the words of another equally 1 eloquent writer "We can answer ' Yes 'to thisl wistful question of genius and humanity. If there is one feature in the life of the ladt 60 years on which Englishmen may juetly pride themselves it is the amelioration of the social condition of the workers. Putting aside all ecclesiastical revivals, all purely political changes and all appeals,however successful to the horrible arbitrament of the sword it is social reform which has made Queen Victoria's freign glorious. We have seen the regulation of !dangerous labour, the protection of women and children from excessive toil, the removal of the tax on bread, the establishment of a, system of National Education. We have seen the restriction of capital punishment, the reformation jot the penal code, the abolition of the duel, the iprohibition of brutal and degrading sports, and at 'any rate some legislative interference with cruelty to animals. With all these reforms we know that the Royal and motherly heart of Queen Victoria was in keen sympathy, and many of them she per- sonally and actively promoted. Whatever else she, was or,was not,the Queen was essentially a worker.t In her public letters to her subjects the idea of / work was always dominant After the jubilee 01., 1887 she wrote of the welcome she had received. "It has shown that the labour and anxiety of 50 long years have been appreciated by my people. This feeling and the sense of duty towards my dear country and subjects, who are so inseparably bound up with my life,will encourage me in my task,of ten a very difficult and arduous one, during the remain-. der of my life." And now the eyes which have beamed with tenderness and love upon her people so many years are closed, the tired hands are peacefully folded, and she having "fought the fight "has en- tered into rest. We look back and we look forward. We give most grateful thanks for a reign dedicated throughout its unparalleled length, to the things which are pure and lovely, and of good report, to the abatement of human misery and to that right- eousness which alone exalts a nation. We face the future with hope and confidence. We believe that the son of such a mother,naust prove himself worthy of his illustrious traditions, and that, having as he had already told us determiaed with God's help " while he has breath in his body, to work for the good and amelioration of his people," he will lead as forward on the royal road of virtuous living and social service, and confident in this belief we offer to our Gracious Lord and Sovereign, King Edward VH., and to his illustrious Coasort, our respectful ,and loyal homage. Alderman SUTTON said : It will be impossible in the few brief remarks I will make to find words sufficiently adequate to express one's acute feelings in regard to the • sad loss the nation has sustained by; the death of our late beloved Queen. Under the present circumstances I feel that any attempt on my part to add much to what you have already said, sir, would only fail to do proper justice to the immortal memoryjuf bullet° Majesty, and only convey a very faint idea of the real nobleness of her character in connection with every act of her public and priva,te life during the 63 years of her happy and glorious • reign. Suffice it, therefore, to say that her late Majesty Queen Victoria was the embodiment of all that was good and virtuous° that as Queen, wife, and mother, and as a ruler of her people, she set a worthy example to all the world, that the charitable deeds and kindly sympathies continually extended to the poorest and humblest in her dominions endeared her to the hearts of all her subjects ; and that apart from all these good qualities we have it on the authority of our leading -tatesmen and those most competent to know that Queen Victoria constantly rendered some valuable ser- vices to the State and did such good work as constitu., tional Sovereign that her judgment in all important matters was ea respected Dy her Ministers that her advice was often taken against that of all others. No , wonder, then that so much grief should be manifested at so great a loss, and it is fitting that we should alsoi join in the condolences which are being transmitted fromi almost every part of tbe globe. I beg to second the resolution you have proposed. The resolution was seconded and carried unani- mously. The members then dispersed. Warrington Board of Guardians. Reference was made to the death of the Queen at th ,meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians. The CHLIRMLN (Mr A J Jolley) said ; It was suggested to me a few days ago that our meeting should be post- poned as a mark of respect to the memory of the late Queen Victoria: There being both practical and legal Idilliculties in the way, I was unable to act on the suggestion, nor does it seem to me that we could better manifest our regard for the venerable ruler who has been at last taken from us, full of years and honoura, after a ite devoted to the good of others, than by quietly pro. *ding with the discharge of the duties immediately fore us even though our hearts be heavy with the urden of a great sorrow. She whom we mourn has I(ton set us a bright example in this matter, deprived by atts of those most near and dear to her, she has calmly rued to her work, not in a fatalistic mood but in the spirit of a brave Christian woman. Let us then, lanimated by the same cheerful resignation and rational piety, proceed with our work. It is nevertheless fitting that the sorrow we feel should re- -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Arrangements for To-morrow at' Warrington.</head>
        <p>The Arrangements for To-morrow at' Warrington.</p>
        <p>To-morrow will be observed as a day of general mourning in Warringten. The banks will be closed and business suspended. The works will close for the day and almost all the business establishments will likewise be closed. The markets are being held to-day (Friday) in Bea of Saturday. Railway traffic will be conducted as on Sundays, the places of amusement will be closed, and the public houses will only open for a few hours. Business will . therefere be at a standstill. Memorial services will be held at several pL of worship. The Mayor, accompanied by the mem- bers of the Corporation and officials, magistrates, • guardians, and leading towasmen,together with the volunteers, firemen and police, will attend the memorial service at the Parish Church. The pro- cession will leave the Town Ball at 11.30, and walk- ing at a slow pace they will proceed to tho parish Church. The Mayor and Corporation will be accom- modated on one side of the central aisle, and thel officers and soldiers from the Barracks on the other.j The service will commence with the singing of the, hymn " The Saints of Gal," after which the opening sentences of the Burial service will be chanted by the choir. Psalm xc. will then be sung, and the les,on from the Burial Service read. The hymn " Now the labourer's task is o'er " will be sung,followed by the Versicles in the Burial service, the Lord's Prayer and four collects. After the Benediction has been pronounced, the Volunteer Band will play the Dead March in " Saul," which Will be followed by the singing of the National Anthem by the congregation. Na addreas will be delivered. We are requeste4 ,tcr state that no chil- dren will 14 Ildtaitted, Fe Churo'clea of the town have decided told it {united memorial service in Bold-street Wesleyan Church at 12 noon on Saturday. A speciall form of service will be provided and a shor.t address' delivered. The service will be concluded in time to hear the Bing proclaimed. On the return of the Mayor and Corporation to the Town Ball, the declaration of the King's Accas-, Mon will be read by the Mayor. This will take, place about two o'clock. Ttie Mayor, immediately on his return, will divest himself of all signs of mourning, and accompanied by the Town Clerk members of the Council, the magistrates of the Borough, and the Co poration officials, take up a position on the steps of the Town Hall. The buglers of the Volunteer band will blow a fanfaronade. There will be a call for silence by the Town Clerk in the following words: "Oyez ! Oyez I Oyez I Let all persons keep silence whilst his Worship the Mayor proclaims his Majesty." The Mayor will then read the Proclamation. At the close of the words" Clod save the Bing." the bugl will repeat their fanfaronade. The Natiot Anthem will be played, the people joining in, and the proceedings will then close. Already the shops in the main streets have been, draped in purple and black material and signs of mourning are very general. Some of the trades- men are exhibiting interesting mementoes of the early years of her late majesty's reign. At the establishment of Mr Alf Taylor, the enterprising boot dealer, of Horsemarkst-street, a copy of the, Liverpool " Courier" of 1861 is on view, containing' an account of tha death of Prince Albert.</p>
