      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Volunteers' Mourning Orders.</head>
        <p>The Volunteers' Mourning Orders.</p>
        <p>The following Volunteer Order has been issued ,by Colonel J C Ilidgway,V.D.:—Warrington,January 28th, 1901. The following army order, dared 24th 'January, 1901, is published for information Orders for mourning for the Army of her late, Majesty Queen Victoria, to be wora until 24th July,l 19J1. His Majesty the King commands that officersl of the army shall wear mouring with their uniforms on the present melancholy occasion, as follows:— Officers are to wear black crape on the left arm of ithe uniform and of the great coat. The drums are Ito be covered with black, and black crape is to be Ihung from the top of the colour staff lof Infantry, Lead from the standard staff and trumpets of Cavalry, until after the funeral of her late ,Nlajesty Church Parade.—ln order that the battalion may ;how their sense of loyalty, duty and affection for aer late Majesty Queen Victoria, the battalion will; 'made at headquarters on Saturday morning next' eleven o'clock, to attend the -divine service to be ield at the Parish Church at the time of the funeral. 3ress review order (helmets and sidearms) Great coats to be worn. By order (Signed) J. D.BucaroN, Major. Acting Adjutant Ist Volunteer Battalion (South Lancashire Regiment).</p>
        <p>Honour for a iLocal Guardsman.</p>
        <p>Among the special Guardsmen bearers at the, ./ueen's funeral is Squadron Corporal-Major Taylor' (of the let Life Guards. Taylor is a Warrington -lan, and before he joined the Life Guards he was .4ployed at Messrs Fletcher, Russell's gas engin] leers, Warrington. He hai been in the Army four- teen years, and some time ago he returned from South Africa, having been staff signaller under Gen- 3ral French, and in that, capacity he had the honour f sanding to England the first news of the relief of Kimberley. He was in Warrington a fortnight and !visited a large number of friends. Naturally the! 'honour conferred upon him has caused great stale- 'faction among his old fellow-employees at Fletcher,' Russell's.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAMBER CHAMBER OF COMMERCE. -- Annual Report.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAMBER CHAMBER OF COMMERCE. -- Annual Report.</p>
        <p>The 24th annual report has just been issued, and rill be presented at the annual meeting to be held it the Town Hall on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>NEW TECHNICAL INSTITUTE</head>
        <p>THE NEW TECHNICAL INSTITUTE</p>
        <p>In referring to the proposed new Technical Insti- tute, the report says that although the Committee have not seen their way to fall in with the sugges- tion of the Chamber, and establish, as a part of it, al ‘ermanent Museum of Local Industries,they intend, s was pointed out in the last report, to arrange for loccasional exhibits in the corridors; whilst Messrs W A Bishop and Co., Ltd., have presented to the Museum Committee a representative collection of i exhibits relating to borax and the allied industries, and example which, the Council hope, will be fol-1 lowed by other local manufacturers, as, in this way,l " the nucleus of a much more extended collection might readily be formed. __ _</p>
        <p>PROPOSED BOARD OF CONCILIATION. At the suggestion of the Warrington Trades andl Labour Council, the Chamber are again considering , the expediency of forming a representative Boardl of Conciliation in local trade disputes. IMPROVED LOCAL RAILWAY SERVICE. As the res'ilt of a correspondence between the Chamber and the London and North-western Rail- Way Company, in which the Secretary pointed out that Warrington was not treated by the Company "with the consideration to which it is entitled as a reat and rapidly increasing industrial centre," a ,number of satisfactory alterations were made in the local service. Further concessions were prom- ,Bed as the result of a subsequent conference at the [`own Ball between the Corporation, the Chamber A Commerce, and the Railway Company; but the/ Council are of opinion that there is still much room for improvement. RIVER DREDGING.</p>
        <p>A conference took place between the Elvers Committee of the Corporation and the Warrington traders, as the result of Counsel's opinion on thel position of the Corporation, the traders, and thei 'hip Canal Company. From this ic appeared that . reference to arbitration was contemplated by the Corporation, and that in the meantime, they de- sired to be fortified with necessary evidence as to the neglect of the Canal Company to carry out their undertaking. The matter was duly reported, and it was resolved " that, the attention of the Council having been brought by members who are greatly interested in the navigation of the Mersey to the fact that the dredging is quite inadequate, and that it is impossible to use the river as in- tended until the Canal Company comply with their obligations under the Act, the Secretary is re- guested to urge them to give the matter their serious attention forthwith." A copy of this reso- lution was forwarded to the Town Clerk, who sent the following v./31y. "Town Clerk's Office, Warring- ton, sth October, 1900.—Dear Sir, Rsferring to the resolution forwarded by you on the 31st July last. Lam now instructed by the Rivera Committee tom- form you that the subject matter of the resolution las had and is having the serious attention of this 7ommittee, and that they hope that the steps they ire taking in the matter will lead to a practical suit, yours faithfully, J. LYON WHITTLE." THE PROGRESS OF WARRINGTON. At the close of the century, the report continues, ore may well congratulate the town upon the; iastonishing progress it has made in almost every, lepartment of industry. Few towns in the United ingdom can look back upon a growth so rapid, or Aicipate with greater confidence the prospects of he future. To improve still further all our natural !acilities of rail, canal, and river ; to keep abreast f our competitors by a complete and satisfactory oitern of co.nosercial education ; to promote the, possible understanding between employersl employed; to attract new industries by suit-1 ibis conditions; and to co-operate with all thel nambers of Commerce of the Empire in maintain- ing and extending English industry throughout the (world—such is the work of the Warrington Chain- er. Its success will be proportioned to the help; hich it receives from those it claims to represent.: THE YINALTCHS. The balance sheet shows that the total receipts in- lading a balance carried forward of £l2 15s, mounted to .£5B 19s. After meeting all exper ,here is a balance in hand of £l3 19s 101</p>
        <p>61 serious</p>
      </div>
