        <p>.1 it well, Their hearts were sad: bow could they anything else. Their religion would assuage their sadness, but the memory of Victoria the noble, the great, he good, would remain with them and with t oir sue- le9sors. Her noble acts, her kindly sympathy, her ighteous judgment, her great desire to be an xarnple o her subjects. were known in a measure to all, but only so ; there was much that was hidden of her good- ness, which would only be revealed at the Great Day of Judgment. Have pity on me! This was their prayer ,to the All-wise God who had in His mercy seen fit to 'afflict our nation.and God would hear them if they prayed AM faith. And in their sorrow they must remember that; eildeat'</p>
