      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>St. James' Church.</head>
        <p>St. James' Church.</p>
        <p>ley</p>
        <p>Oithe</p>
        <p>thie church references were made to the death ofl s Queen and the accession of the new King, the pul- being occupied morning and evening by the Rev. F.l Powell, vicar. In the evening be said a deeperl ilw could not well have fallen upon our nation. Notl</p>
        <p>gpit E. she on</p>
        <p>.1 these islands alone has the great sorrow descended, but on the remotest extremities of the earth. The re- moval of that one little human figure, of that lady who had long passed the Scriptural span of human life, has called forth an exprecsion of grief as genuine as it is universal, of sympathy as deep 83 it is wide-spread. In death of our beloved Queen the world loses the ~..eatest personalty of the age. We have to go back 1 exactly three centuries before we can see the throne of England occupying anything approaching the popularity we see it occupy te-day. But how great the contrast be- ;wee° the characters of Queen Elisabeth and Queen 7ictoria I We have to go back exactly a thousand' 'ears to find an English Sovereign awakening an affec- ion and an attachment similar to that we see aroused 0-day- And yet bow vast is the change which has ome over England and over the ideas of Eng- lishmen since the time of Alfred the Great ! le great value of the Queen's life consists in the fact it she was always aware of the moral power and aendous responsibility with which her high position ssarily clothed her. That thoughtful consideration , shown so incessantly for her retainers and servants, cer her soldiers and sailors—that tender sympathy— xpressed now to the widow of a President of the United ;ates or of an archbishop, now to the orphaned and lesolate of some co:liery or seafaring district, and again for the ,victims of some terrible disaster—that wise interest in the affairs and prosperity of the nation—now opening a ship canal or an exhibition, now attending some civic function or public ceremony, that active benevolence, now subscribing to some charity, now patronizing some elevating enterprise—these, while good and useful in any ordinary person, become intensified in usefulness when proc-eding from one occupying such a, positiontas Sovereign. She used her influence not for self-aggrandisement—there have been some who have found tault with her because of the quiet homeliness of tier Court—but t 3 advance all that was good and of use o her people. Of her tactful sympathy, of her sweet Ifishness, of her exquisite gentleness we have had andant proof—and these qualities—added to a :renuoas d,.votion to duty, which have endeared the :ood Queen to tier subjects. The value of her example, ishe unsullied purity of her life, the touching devotion ito the memory of her good husband, with whom, after a separation of 40 years she is now united in rest and cane, the exemplary influence she brought to bear on sr large family—who can properly estimate what all as has meant to our national life ? May we serve our aneration so nobly and as truly in our measure and pportunity as she did, and then death will but be as era was—a -happy going home to those "loved long ince arid lost awhile," a sweet falling asleep after the hood honest work of along, long day. At the conclusion of the service,l'which was a very impressive one throughout, the organist played the Dead Illarch in "Saul," and the congregation subsequently !sang "God save the King."</p>
