      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>Emmanuel Reformed Church.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Reformed Church.</p>
        <p>The reading desk, lectern and pulpit desk at the 'above church were draped with black cloth. Hymns iprupriate to the occasion were sung, and at the close . the morning and evening services the Dead March was expressively played by Miss Eva Lees, the organist, the congregation remaining standing until the close. Sermons were preached in connection with the sad occa- sion by Rev. E T Reed. In the course of his sermon in the morning the preacher after testifying to the personal character of the Queen,said thatduting a time of unexame pled prosperity, experienced in connection with a reign of such goodness and berretta )nae, there is much danger ' less the heart becomes too much fiX.3l upon earthly things. With so mach to satisfy the requirements of the body and of the mind it is, portaaps, not surprising • that the needs of the spirit should be negleeted and put one side. Then a sharp reminder comes of the mut • ability of human things, and a nation and Empire is plunged into the deepest sorrow by the removal of its human head. At this time it is well to lift up our hearts and to remember that we have with us at all times a living Christ who is the King of kings and Lord of lords, and as we say adieu to our late revered Sovereign we love to think of her as being " with Christ—which is very far better." in keeping with the inscription placed by her own command in the Mausoleum of Prince Albert the Good at Frogmore "Farewell, beloved ! here at last I will rest with thee ; with thee in Christ I will rise again."</p>
        <p>Appropi lof</p>
        <p>Bold• Street Wesleyan Church. At this church, on Sunday evening, the organist (Mr lHickman) played the Dead Msrch in "Saul." The preacher was the Rev. H G Reberts, who spoke upon " Victoria the Good," his text being—" Blessed be the Lord thy Cind,which delighteth in thee to set thee on the tmone, to do judgment and justice." He said : Ts pay adequate tribute to Victoria the Good—a life insep- .ably associated w;th thelprogiels of the pasemintury, al .ife upon:which the light of God rested for more than 80' 'years, and one that swayed an unrivalled sceptre for 63 years, a life that stands prominently forward to-day 551 one of the most honchered lives:of the age—is impoisible.l I am perplexed to say anything that should not have the iappearance of simple iteration. It seems impossible to add to what has already been said, and said with such ' .graceful eloquence and tender feeling. When Peel died, even else matchless eloquence of Gladstone scemed poor by the side of the Duke of Wellington's sobbing tribute,, and so it is with us to-day—we feel.but we cannot speak.' At first we could scarcely persuade ourselves that the</p>
      </div>
