        <p>A special meeting of the Warrington Town Counoi was held at noon on Wednesday for the purpose of pass- ing a resolution of condolence on the death of Her Majesty, Queen Victoria, His W3rship the Mayor presided and there were also present : Aldermen Sutton, Francombe, Hutchinson, Heaton, Shaw Green, Fair- dough, Monks. Councillors Tinnion, Plunapton' Smet- hurst, Bennett, 9 Davies T B Malkin, J Baird, William Roberts, A 3 Jolley, L Ellison, Rogers, F R Roberts, W Brough, J Sampson. Wilkinson,with J Lyon Whittle (town clerk), 8 Wilson (deputy town clerk), C Madeley (librarian), J Fairhurst (borough treasurer) T Longdin. I(b)rough surveyor), Dr Gornall (medical officer), J W Grunsdale (electrical engineer). •A telegraphic message of regret for abiknce was re - dyed from Councillor William Pierpoint. The Mayor wore his full official robes. His chain of office had been draped in crape, and the mace had been similarly treated. Daring the proceedings the Council remained stand log, and the scene was a deeply impressive one. The MAYOR moved the following resolution :—" To his Majesty King Edward VII. May it please your Msjeity. The Mayor, aldermen and councillors of the Borough of Warrington, in special meeting assembled, desire respectfully to record the profound grief with which they have received the intelligence of the death of their deeply beloved and venerated Sovereign, Queen Victoria, and their devout gratitude to Almighty God for her unparalleled and beneficent reign; and they beg save to be allowed to tender to your Majesty, and to le other members of the Royal Family, their heartfelt ,ympathy in the irreparable loss which you and the jwhole Empire have sustained. They desire also to offer 'to your Majesty the assurance of their loyalty to your Majesty's throne and person." The Mayor said :—The resolution which it is my sad and solemn duty to submit to you for accept- lance, only very faintly expresses the deep seated' _ _</p>
