      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>For the services on Sunday the church was suitably Jraped. The choir rail, pulpit and communion table, ion which were white flowers, were covered with black overlaid with purple, tied with white ribbon. The ser- vice in the morning, conducted by the Rev. C. H. Hocken, was of a memorial character. In his sermon the preacher said : The death of our Queen closes an era unexampled in the annals of this country. Her death comes as a personal bereavement not only to them, but also to the distant colonies,the millions of India and our kinsmen in the United Ssates of America,and causes a sense of loss throughout the world. It is well to remember that her world-wide influence rested on an ea-</p>
        <p>the</p>
        <p>dcha</p>
        <p>.iL)I</p>
        <p>,alted position adorned with-piWy,-devotion to duty, zenderl sympathy and rich mental endowments. It is iriot too much to say that she seemed to us the e mbodi- ment of the ideal wife, mother and Queen. Moen on great occasions she sent flo me message of sympathy or encouragement to an afil cted home or community, the people felt she had rightly expressed the best that was in their hearts. It is the destiny of some, as lir. Balfour said, to outlive their fame. But during the long' reign of 63 years all classes and countries had felt the .. ._ _ _ _ _ '</p>
        <p>iset</p>
        <p>magic power of our Queen, and her influenee for good had grown in strength and intensity With her in ere ling years. Now like a full orbed summer's sun she</p>
        <p>iters tier rest surrounded with clouds of glory, ad- mired, revered, almost adored, the ideal Queen aud Mother of her plople. The tender prayer is answered— .' The love of all thy sons encompass thee, The love of all thy daughters chedsh thee, The love of all thy people comfort thee. Till God's love set thee at His side again."</p>
        <p>_ The preacher also referrcd to the influence of her example the unique position amongst the crowned heads of the world. Do we wish to perpetuate her memory ? Let us strive to be worthy of her. Let u 3 build her an everlasting monument by carrying forward the best traditions of her reign. The omens are not all propitious. The war which saddened the last days of our Queen,ther resentment against us as a nation in Europe, and ourl social troubles. should Chasten U 4. But if wo imitate" our noble Queen of blessed memory during her glorious reign, and with her godly courage and fidelity work out with God the salvation of our cmintry she will not have lived in vain and the future has no terrors. At the close, of the address Mrs D Stone touchingly rendered " 0 rest in the Lord," and a hymrn " For the King," was sung The service closed with the Dead March played by Mr B Wadsworth. The Rev C El Hocken also in the evening made further references to the Queen's death. " I know that my Redeemer liveth "was sung by Mrs, D Stone and the hymn "For the King" was repeated. 1</p>
        <p>ti alit</p>
        <p>4sul</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>Cairo-street Chapel.</head>
        <p>Cairo-street Chapel.</p>
        <p>The interior of the Cairo-street Chapel was suitably draped on Sunday. when the pulpit was occupied by the Rev J Soiree ler. of the Rome Missionary College, Min- chester, who paid a graceful tribute to the late Q/een, and at the close of the service the Dead March in was played.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>Baptist Church, Latchford.</head>
        <p>Baptist Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening a tribute was pail to the deceased monarch at the Baptist Church, L itchford, by the minister, the Rev J F Matthews. Few deaths, he said,l within the memory of living man have called forth ouch univereal sorrow as that of the august lady who for sixty-two years was the supreme governor of this realm. sorrow has been profound, for Queen Victoria had ated a home for herself in the hart of every one of ler subjects. Perhaps no monarch who has ever reigned over our country, has gathered up into his own person. and illustrated with quite such clearness and force, the genius and spirir of our Constitution as Queen Victoria has done. To say that she has not interfered in the general home policy of the country is but a fair witness to her supreme wisdom,and her insight into our national -ter. She has :Wed by going the way of her</p>
        <p>.8 'created lon g ihr</p>
        <p>ge</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Church, Legh•street. I Preaehing last Sunday evening at Legh-street Primi- tive Methodist Church, the Rev. M. H. Chapman said they were met together with a great sorrow in their hearts. Our beloved Queen has pas -A from us. There was singular pathos in the interest wt.i.e3 had been die. played by all the civilized nations during the Queen's illness, and when the end came they had poured into our country their tribute of admiration and love. And where must we look for that which has raised our Queen to such pre-eminenoe among the nations of the world and wedded the hearts of her own people so closely to her ? It is in her character—the pure and saintly woman whose sympathy was so wide that it touched the life of her people in its every aspect. It is easy to speak of the reign of Queen Victoria as great, but we shall only realise its greatness the farther we are removed from it. The Queen displayed large tolerance in matters religious. It is said that among the servants of the , royal household was a Methodist who was zealous in her attendance at the class meeting. She came in for a good ! indeed matters became as acute some of me of the bigoted servants threatened to deal t o leave if the Methodist servant was retained. The matter reached the ears of the Queen who said ".1 will go my_ self to the meeting and see if it is aet place for a see vent to go to." On her return to the palace the incident closed with the Queen's remark " Never neglect your Class meeting, I only wish other servants would go Qdeen has passed from us andl look into the future, enmotay the solemn utterance wa7: ishtoith'be"hewoPl ehi touuugssi r3t that e;wrrloohhwviael evyclee ltw' wleif e shall last " he will en- deavour to the best of his ability to fulfil the duties Iwhich devolve upon him. During the service the , g Mr F Podmore played "The Dead March" the iocronagnriesst, a tion atanding.</p>
        <p>:country</p>
        <p>dself</p>
        <p>4dea'</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>The preacher at this Church on Sunday morning (Revl .L. Milne,) took as his subject, the godly life of the, late Queen, taking as a text, the words, 'So teach us' to number our days, sl that we may get us an heart of I wisdom." The pulpit was draped, and at the close of ithe service the organist (Mr Jones) played the Dead IMarch in "Saul," the congregation meanwhile standing.</p>
        <p>Ito</p>
        <p>1 Ft jars' Green Chapel. , Mr. Lig'lt preached in the above place of worship on I, Sunday RI oruina from the words of St. Paul to Timothy: a vi"l am now ready to be offered up." He said the Queen's i A , nrb e life was a pattern to mothers and subjects.' Efweoulcd bonerlludedotthby successor to so noble n ancestor.and y praying that our present Sovereign that his end would be as glorious as hers. At the con-1 elusion of the service the congregation remained stand— id g whilst the Dead March was played.</p>
