      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>Hill Ii ffe Baptist h ape!.</head>
        <p>Hill Ii ffe Baptist h ape!.</p>
        <p>The morning service at the Hill Odle Baptist Chapel Artoo% almost entirely of a mem qial character, the iymne, less me, addreS9 to the children, prayers, and ermon having reference to the great loss which the nation has sustained through the death of the Queen. The address to thechildren which the pastor,the Rev J S Hughes, always gives at the morning service was based ! on the words uttered by the Queen when she became cognizant that she was likely to become Sovereign : " It! is a solemn thing. X will be good." and his sermon was! ! a tribute to the memory, of the Queen. He dealt with her devoted life, lofty character, and national worth. Her many sorrows, her devotion to duty, her sad ! bereavements, heroism in the greatest trials, her great iage, her warm sympathy with others, and the essential iwomanline3s of tier acts touched our hearts and trans- figured her character. She had bequeathed a royal heritage, which we must cherish and bold as a sacred ;trust. She had left the heritage of a good name. She ihad left the heritage of a pure Court. She had left a IRoyal heritage of religous freedom. As Baptists, and as .embers of the Free Charches,they were not unmindful aor ungrateful of the high privileges they had enjoyed under the beneficent end peaceful reign of Victoria the Good, nor were they lacking in sincere appreciations of those high and ratable qualities which made her great and helped to make her country the home of tho brave and he free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-02-01_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>sicreasi,</head>
        <p>sicreasi,</p>
        <p>!people.</p>
        <p>Runcorn. Board of Guardians.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the above Board on Tuesday at ''Dutton the Chairman said, before proceeding with the ordinary business he desired to refer to an event which was uppermost in their minds. Since their last meeting. they had sustained a great national loss by the death of Her Majesty the Queen. He did not propose to mike any lengthy remarks, as to the wonderful and splendid life of Her Majesty in the capacity of Queen of this great empire. Enough had already been said at the various councils and boards of guardians In the country' to express what they felt in the matter of admiration for the late Qmsen's life. Although they were a small community, they were none the less loyal to the Crown, and .none the les desirous of expreising their appreciation of the virtues and estimable qualities evidenced by her late Majesty's iife as a mother wife and Queen. As poor law guardians, they might fairly say. that during the Queen's reign. the condition of the poor. and the care of children in workhouses had been won- derfully improved, and he thought they haci good reason for believing that the Queen's sympathy, and kindly feeling towards her people, had helped to bring about many at the improvements which had taken place. He proposed, that their clerk record upon the minutes, the fact that they expressed themselves as in full sympathy with the Royal family, and the nation generally, in the loss sustained by the sad event. They wished to be second to none in the expression of their loyalty and affection towards the throne- (Hear, hear.) Mr. J Stokes seconded. He felt be was expressing the feeling of every member present when he said that through the death of the Queen they had sustained a loss which it would take a loag time to remedy- There seemed to be a pall hanging over the whole land in every home. The late Queen had endeared herself to all classes—rich and poor alike—by her kindly sympathy in the time of trial and trouble ; and, they knew from experience that their best friends, and their truest friends, were thJse who stood by them in the hour of need. The Queen had set a noble example in this direc- tion, and one that all would do well to emulate. The Queeu was a deeply religious wonsan,and honoured God in all things, from the day she succeeded to the throne to the day of her death, and she had the reward men- tioned in hely writ—" Them that honour me I will honour." She had left a name behind, that wJuld liV3 for all time. (Hear, hear.) The resolution was carried, the members meanwhile tandinz.</p>
        <p>41secont</p>
        <p>411seel</p>
        <p>Rural District Council. The Chairman at the meeting on 7 uesdav, touchingly referred to the loss the nation and the Royal family had sustained by the death of her late Majesty, Queen Vtctoria, and moved that a letter ef condolence with the Bing and the Royal Family,be forwarded to the proper quarter, and a record of such be placed anon too minutes of the Council. The Chairman of the Board oE Guardians had made a few choice remarks about the Quesn,which was a very easy matter for any one'to do, for she bad been a good Queen: (Hear,hear.) He, too, considered it was a good thing for the country they had someone willing to try and follow in her footsteps. (Hear,. tar.).</p>
        <p>sMr B W,sod war in seconding' said they had lost, as the Chairman of the Board of Guardians expressed so finely, not a queen ; but a woman—a woman who had been a pattern to all of them and a mother. (llear, hear.) It was not merely a national loss, but he felt it personally. He was quite in symoathy with the notion that day, to express their sympathy with the Royal Family, and express to them their sense of the great less which they personally and as a nation bad s ,ffered. (ljear, hear.) Ele was glad also that the King who now sat upon the throne had begun well ; and, he lik.d the exprvssion, made by him the other day, that he would try to follow in the footsteps of his mother. He could not do better, for the late Queen had discovered the art of how to govern ; and he (Mr. Woodward), was of opinion it was expressed in the advice, which she had given • to one who was governing another country, when she said " That the business of a Queen was not to govern, but first to set her people a good example." (Gear, hear.) The King would truly have something to do to follow In her footsteps, so he said with all his heart" God save ,the Bing."</p>
        <p>(The</p>
        <p>The v.-cite was unanimously passed, all standing. . Special Meeting of the Runcorn Urban Dm- trict Council. , The Council Chamber at the Town Hall, Runcorn was on Tuesday evening last, draped with evidences of 'deep mourning as a mark of respect to the memory of our . late Queen Victoria. The councillors bad been specially " lied together for the perpose of placing on record the found regret and sorrow felt by the Council through ,e death of her Majesty the Queen: The attendance in- cluded :—Messrs William Handley. J P, (chairman). S Taylor, JP, E Indison, CO, J P. E Gatley, F Wiswall. El J Grice, T Jones, T Than, R El Posnett, J Wilson, S Walton, H G Kirkham, J M Sweetman, S Waltem, D Bisbrown, JII Salkeld, George Hewitt, F A Lake (law- Clerk), E Marshall, (secretary), J Wading (surveyor). 'nci J Percival (inspec^or). Mr Handley said: We are met under the 1 shadow of a great national sorrow. It is not necessary for me to enumerate or extol the virtues of our deceased illustrious Queen, ay are written in the imperishable history of our Em- and deeply engraved on the hearts of her subjeccl leg her reign the Queen's example has outshone that A all her predecessors in its tender sympathy and grac- ious influence for the well-being of the people. Let us rather express our thankfulness to Almighty God and acknowledge His gracious mercy in having given to our , country so unselfish a life and noble a character to guide its destinies for so lengthened a period. I will ask , you to pass in silence the following resolution: "This Council on behalf of the town of Runzorn desires to place on record their deep sense of the loss sustained by the nation in the death of Her Majasty;Queen Victoria ' and to associate themselves with the increased sorrow which fills the hearts of all. AA wife and mother by the touching beauty of her life she endeared herself to all her subjects: As Queen throughout her glorious reign ' remarkable alike for its length and beneficence by her loyal adherence to the Constitution and ,her courageous influence for National righteousness he has bequeathed for all time the supreme example o great monarch and a noble woman." The Chairman read an extract from the Poet Laureate's poem, and the members of the Council rose,and remain-. led standing during the cenclusiOn of the Chairman's impressive address. AThe resolution was passed.</p>
